1785607927403323	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAQ		item.comment	bn-3p8u	{"body":"PR-8 IMPL-02 delivered (1/2 — design): deterministic BFS in crates/continuum-engine-reference/src/bfs.rs (777 lines) + 40 tests in two new suites. Crate total 112; the 72 IMPL-01 tests are untouched and green.\n\nDESIGN. Explicit FIFO VecDeque of (state, depth); BTreeMap<State, usize> visited set; one state expanded per iteration. The queue carries each state's depth rather than looking it up, deleting a 'cannot happen' arm the no-panic covenant would otherwise have to handle as data. Iterating the BTreeMap yields the reachable set already in the wire form's ascending state-table order (wire.rs:89-92, 616-620) with a parallel depth column, so certificate emission is a copy, not a sort whose stability would have to be argued. Two decisions worth review: (1) a state is expanded ATOMICALLY — the whole row is computed, then bounds are consulted, then every new target is admitted or none is; admitting part of a row would leave an explored set that is not a prefix of the canonical walk and a frontier that cannot be resumed from; (2) new targets are admitted in ascending STATE order (BTreeSet of the row's new targets), not the row's (action, target) order — both deterministic, but ascending makes queue order, hence the frontier a bounded run reports, independent of how the actions are NAMED.\n\nBOUNDS. Three, declared per call; no Default on Bounds and explore() takes it by value, so an exploration cannot begin without someone naming its limits. MAX_STATES = 1<<20 and MAX_TRANSITIONS = 1<<22 are kernel-core wire.rs:131/139 verbatim (MAX_TRANSITIONS is 'the largest number of transitions a finite-closure certificate may carry in total, across every successor row' — exactly what the engine counts, in the same u64 the kernel accumulates while decoding). MAX_DEPTH = 1<<20 is the ENGINE'S OWN and stated so: no certificate carries a depth at all (docs/03 6.1 lists state table, initial IDs, action schemas, rows, invariant data), so there is nothing to cite; it is set to the ceiling the state bound already implies (n states means depth at most n-1) and kept as a separate knob because bounding search without bounding size is IMPL-04's natural query. kernel-temporal's smaller MAX_STATES (1<<16, temporal wire.rs:136) is deliberately NOT the bound, with the reason recorded: the fifth bone emits a kernel-CORE finite-closure certificate, and the temporal bound would refuse models the core checker accepts.\n\nPARTIAL RESULT. Exploration = Complete(Reachable) | Exhausted(Partial). Partial carries the explored set + the frontier (discovered-but-unexpanded, in QUEUE order — sorting would discard the breadth-first position that makes a resumed walk agree with an unbounded one; it is still a function of model and bounds alone) + which Bound tripped + the Bounds it was given. Exploration::closed() returns Some only on the Complete arm — the seam that stops a finite-closure certificate being built from a truncated set, in the type rather than in prose. reachable() gives the states either way, because a violation found in a partial set is a real violation while NOT finding one is Inconclusive/ResourceExhausted; that asymmetry is documented and left to IMPL-03. Bound has three members rather than one Exhausted flag (INV-008). Checks run in a fixed order (transitions, depth, states) so an expansion breaking two names one of them deterministically; the depth and state checks are skipped when a row discovers nothing new — an expansion that adds no state cannot deepen the walk, and stopping on it would report a bound never actually reached (a self-loop model completes under a depth bound of 0; the counter chain does not).\n\nRESOLUTION PASS: DECLINED, structurally rather than as a preference. BFS never constructs an Environment. Model::successors builds it internally from &self.variables, so a resolved name-to-index environment is unreachable from bfs.rs; using one would mean bypassing successors() and re-implementing transition evaluation — precisely the private path to the answer diehard_evidence.rs exists to prevent. Zero lines of model.rs and expr.rs changed, no new public API on either. Outside the new files the only edits are lib.rs's mod/pub-use lines and the crate doc paragraph that said 'Nothing here searches. There is no reachable-set computation, no frontier, and no visited set in this crate's shipped code' — which this bone falsifies, so I corrected it rather than leave it standing.\n\nTELEMETRY SEAM: none, deliberately. No callback, observer or channel — a progress hook is ambient effect by another name, the same construct expr.rs refuses for guards. Counts ride on the result (len, expanded, transitions, max_depth, plus the frontier when partial); streaming them as task milestones is documented as PR 5+ daemon work (docs/35:99-110, RFC 0026 task.status).\n\nSEAMS for the siblings, as a table in the module docs: IMPL-03 reads Reachable::states plus an empty successors row, surfaced and never judged; IMPL-04 reads depth_of and layer (a state at depth d has a d-step path, and its predecessors are the depth-(d-1) states whose row contains it); IMPL-05 reads Exploration::closed, getting the ascending table with Post(S) subset S by construction rather than re-checked."}	blake3:fJwrMJ4O1_Gokgu21U1v1pSZRuF0VZ_mypqD63KNbvE
1785607951110234	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAg		item.comment	bn-3p8u	{"body":"PR-8 IMPL-02 (2/2 — evidence, gates, concerns).\n\nDIE HARD THROUGH THE ENGINE. 16 states (exact ascending list), 96 labelled transitions, expanded == len == 16, max depth 7, the full depth map frozen ((4,3) at 6, (4,0) at 7), the eight layers spelled out and asserted to partition the set, and closure re-checked against the returned set rather than trusted from the Complete arm. The corroboration is the real point: the_engine_agrees_with_the_hand_walk asserts the engine's depth map EQUALS the one produced by diehard_evidence.rs's own nine-line VecDeque closure, recomputed independently in the new file — two implementations of one breadth-first definition, now a differential pair rather than one asserting and one assuming. diehard_evidence.rs itself is byte-for-byte unchanged, as the fence required.\n\nTESTS (40 new; 17 + 23). bfs_diehard: the three frozen facts, the depth map, the eight layers, closure, the ascending state table, engine-vs-hand-walk, determinism for both a complete and a bounded run (equal AND byte-identical rendering), each of the three bounds tripping on Die Hard with EXACT explored sets, depths, frontiers and counts, bound exactness (16/7/96 completes; 15/7/96, 16/6/96 and 16/7/95 each trip the matching bound), a prefix test showing every bounded run's states carry the depths the complete walk gives them and every frontier state is in the explored set, and the crate-root re-export. bfs_contract: six linear models (counter chain, runaway counter, self-loop, two-starts, enumerated fork, overflowing guard) covering the complete case, deadlock surfacing (the chain's last state is in the set, expanded, contributing zero transitions — and explicitly NOT judged; whether an empty row is a defect is IMPL-03's policy), each bound naming itself on one chain, no bound ever exceeded or clipped, depth-0 both ways, exactness of the state and transition bounds, deterministic reporting when three bounds trip on one expansion, frontier length == len minus expanded, both error arms, and determinism across four bound settings including a failing run.\n\nADVERSARIAL. ExplorationError::Evaluation carries { state, depth, action: Option<Ident>, source: Box<EvaluationError> }. The action comes from a diagnosis pass (culprit()) that replays Model::successors' ascending action scan with Model::action_successors, on the error path only. That pass earns its keep on the overflowing_guard model: a guard that overflows produces EvaluationError::Expression(Overflow), which names NO action, and the exploration error names it anyway. The UpdateOutOfDomain case names the state (3), depth 3, action Bump, variable n, value 4, domain 0..=3. No partial set rides with the error, and the reason is tested rather than asserted: bounding the walk short of the defect returns an ordinary Exhausted partial, so a broken model's well-defined part is re-derivable exactly — nothing is lost by dropping evidence a bound can reproduce, and something would be lost by reporting a partial reachable set for a model that has none. Nothing panics on any path.\n\nGATES. cargo fmt, clippy --workspace --all-targets -D warnings, cargo test, cargo doc all clean; full 'just check' run ONCE, exit 0. START_HERE PR-8 bullet 2 annotated; traceability regenerated (PR-8-IMPL-02 active -> satisfied, 727 -> 726 active). validation-results.json NOT touched. Working tree is exactly three new files (src/bfs.rs, tests/bfs_diehard.rs, tests/bfs_contract.rs) plus lib.rs and the three plan-notes files. Committed as 31795bc; no merge, no push, no bn state change.\n\nCONCERNS. (1) ExplorationError::Evaluation's source is Box<EvaluationError> to satisfy clippy::result_large_err rather than an #[allow]. The justification I wrote is real — a well-formed model never produces the variant, so widening every caller's Result charges the success path for a failure it does not have — but it is a lint-shaped decision and the lead may prefer the allow with a comment instead. (2) culprit()'s None arm is unreachable from explore(): it needs Model::successors' state check to fail ahead of the action scan, and every state explore() holds came from successors(). Kept because culprit() is total and cannot prove the impossibility; it is untested. (3) The START_HERE bullet deliberately avoids underscored identifiers — traceability.py:77 strips underscores as markdown emphasis, so MAX_STATES would land in the registry summary as MAXSTATES. I reworded to prose rather than ship a wrong summary; the generator behaviour may deserve a follow-up bone since the next author to cite a constant will hit it. (4) req:phase-a is on this bone and is NOT annotated. PR-8-EXIT correctly stays active: it is a daemon-API claim ('Die Hard returns 16 states and depth-6 solution through the daemon API') and this bone touches no daemon code. (5) The frontier is a resume POINT, not a resume MECHANISM: there is no explore_from(frontier) entry point, because the continuation grain is RFC 0026's task.resume and belongs to the daemon bones. The prefix property that would make such a resume correct is tested; the resume itself is not, because it does not exist."}	blake3:XwCATdjBeOSIuZ2DQEbWtS9-vVfSMZvqyhUHjDusZvw
1785608056103016	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyqgc		item.comment	bn-3p8u	{"body":"Landed @ aff2360. Lead ran all suites in-workspace (112 crate tests green). Ratified: atomic row expansion (partial rows would break the prefix property), admission in ascending state order (frontier independent of action naming), no-Default bounds (exploration cannot begin unnamed), Complete/Exhausted with closed() as the type-level seam against certificates-from-truncated-sets, MAX_DEPTH as the engine's own with the honest nothing-to-cite statement, temporal's 1<<16 correctly NOT the bound, resolution pass structurally declined, frontier-as-point with resume correctly deferred to RFC 0026 task.resume. The differential pair (engine depth map == independent hand-walk) is the right corroboration structure. Box-vs-allow accepted as shipped. traceability.py underscore-stripping (MAX_STATES→MAXSTATES) routed to bn-1hg4o."}	blake3:nsFDqMvATtGF4Ry9qIkjzoVYsET-oB36irTPCTVYcrI
1785608056137539	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyqwc		item.move	bn-3p8u	{"state":"done"}	blake3:G9j4EjGZxshCrfETP5cu-iWnnOCsdZIITvjyUlDeteA
