1785610572877458	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAQ		item.comment	bn-ww5ic	{"body":"Implemented: certificate.kind gains 'state-type' member (kernel-core's StateType family, previously folded into closed-set); typed assurance_class (enum CHECKED_CERTIFICATE/TRUSTED_SOLVER, docs/03 §7 + ADR-0017 verbatim) and trusted_components (array, minItems 0, uniqueItems, kebab-case+optional :SUFFIX pattern) fields added, both REQUIRED. All four receipt.rs modules updated: claim.text no longer carries 'trusted components: ...' or (kernel-smt) '; assurance ...' — those facts now emit as typed fields; kernel-core/-sat/-temporal each get a documented constant CHECKED_CERTIFICATE (no solver in their loop, per ADR-0017); kernel-smt reuses its existing derived AssuranceClass. Four golden receipts regenerated (byte-for-byte from to_json()); example instance updated. just check: fmt/lint/test/boundaries/covenant(KCOV-09 incl. self-test)/governance/dossier/lean all green, exit 0. check_kernel_covenant.py untouched (its generic schema-subset validator needed no changes). No plan-notes prose touched; traceability regen was a no-op as expected."}	blake3:FoAWbI7pJUp5lvcW8epBndkwqYrpL2UYNZ0fpHSp858
1785610703618010	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEywgc		item.comment	bn-ww5ic	{"body":"Landed. Lead ran covenant + all four kernel suites in-workspace (green). Ratified: both new fields REQUIRED (optional would recreate the prose gap — exactly right); the two-member assurance enum closed at what the crates actually produce, with docs/03 §7's wider families recorded as non-breaking future widening; the permissive-not-overfit trusted-components pattern; the machine facts moved out of claim.text with the human sentence retained. KCOV-09 needing no code change is the generic-validator design paying off."}	blake3:IM41ir1YRrQKxUaTgREhM-_mayg-6eS7Vhykt25VhKY
1785610703647848	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEywwc		item.move	bn-ww5ic	{"state":"done"}	blake3:mn9OdZIOXDBUSvnjdSJkvBCd0pi2FZGvtDAO4z0k4U4
1785610895802638	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAQ		item.comment	bn-3e0m	{"body":"PR-8 IMPL-03 delivered (1/2 — the vocabulary and the two policy decisions). crates/continuum-engine-reference/src/checking.rs (1153 lines) + 36 tests in two new suites (checking_diehard 15, checking_contract 21). Crate total 212; the 176 tests from IMPL-01/02/04/05 are untouched and green. Zero new dependencies; bfs.rs, witness.rs, certificate.rs, model.rs, expr.rs and diehard.rs are byte-for-byte unchanged, as the fence required.\n\nOUTCOME VOCABULARY. Not the kernel's Verdict and not a bool: the ASSURANCE RESULT's, which is the vocabulary a daemon task result has to carry. schemas/assurance-result.schema.json:203-210 fixes verdict as exactly {established, refuted, inconclusive} and its own description states the two separations INV-008 needs — budget exhaustion is never a verdict, and unsupported/engine-error are not verdicts either but inconclusive WITH a reason. So CheckOutcome has three arms, Holds{states} / Violated{state, depth, violations, evidence} / Inconclusive(Unresolved), and checking::Verdict{Established, Refuted, Inconclusive} carries the schema's own kebab-free spellings ('established'/'refuted'/'inconclusive') with a test asserting them. The shape argument is kernel-core verdict.rs:1-36 restated on the untrusted side: the kernel must keep 'this artifact is invalid' apart from 'I do not implement that'; an engine must keep 'no state falsifies this' apart from 'no state I looked at falsifies this', and both are kept apart by being an arm.\n\nINV-008 MAPPING. Unresolved has exactly two arms — ResourceExhausted{tripped, explored, frontier} and EngineError{state, source: Box<EvaluationError>} — and as_str() returns 'ResourceExhausted' and 'EngineError', the spellings continuum-value's InconclusiveReason::as_str and both normative schemas use. It is a NAMED SUBSET, not a copy: it carries the evidence for its reason (which bound, how many explored, how big the frontier; which state, which model-layer error) which the dossier enum does not, and it does not offer four arms a finite reference engine cannot construct — Unsupported is a fragment question ModelError already refuses, InsufficientTelemetry has no telemetry here, AbstractionAmbiguity has no correspondence map, IncompleteProofSearch has no proof search. continuum-value was NOT imported: bn-2d0e's decline was ratified on the shortlex-vs-byte-order argument (two orders on one concept, GOV-1-12), lib.rs states the crate depends on nothing, and reversing that for one enum in a crate a sibling agent is concurrently editing is a bigger move than a string test. The 1:1 mapping is asserted as strings instead — a test the two crates share without an edge.\n\nTHE ASYMMETRY (the load-bearing decision). bfs.rs:542-546 states the rule and assigns it here verbatim: 'a violation found in a partial set is a real violation ... the converse does not hold, and that asymmetry is the checking bone's to encode'. So the four cells are: Complete+falsified = Violated with a shortest witness; Complete+clean = Holds; Exhausted+clean = Inconclusive(ResourceExhausted) naming the bound; Exhausted+falsified = Violated, UNWITNESSED. The fourth cell is the one that would be wrong in both directions — reporting it inconclusive throws away a found counterexample that no larger budget can un-reach, and attaching a path calls something shortest that a larger budget could beat. witness::shortest refuses every Exhausted exploration by ratified design (bn-2pmc, 'even when the endpoint was in fact discovered at its true depth'), so I did NOT work around my sibling's refusal: Evidence is a two-arm type, Shortest(Witness) or Unwitnessed(NoWitness), and the truncated cell carries NoWitness::Truncated verbatim. Nothing in the module can produce Holds from a truncated exploration, at any bound; no_bounded_run_ever_reports_holds asserts it across nine bound settings on the one model whose complete answer is known.\n\nDEADLOCK POLICY. is-a-terminal-state-a-defect is the caller's declaration, and the declaration already exists: the intent contract's completion policy (schemas/intent-contract.schema.json:772-780, RFC 0015), a CLOSED four-member enum whose description reads 'No engine may silently change this'. DeadlockPolicy is its engine-grain projection with the mapping written into the type docs — deadlock-violation to Defect; stutter-forever and finite-trace-only to Allowed; and 'closed' (environment-closed completion) DELIBERATELY UNMAPPED, because this layer declares no environment and translating it to either arm would invent the missing half. Two arms rather than four, and named 'defect'/'allowed' rather than borrowing an intent spelling, precisely because Allowed covers two members and reusing one name would claim a correspondence that does not hold. DeadlockOutcome has four arms, not three: NotJudged{terminal} exists because 'no state is a deadlock' and 'no state was asked to be' are different sentences and only one is a claim — under Allowed the terminal states are still COUNTED (a fact about the model either way, INV-007) and none is judged or witnessed. docs/27:47 is the citation for the question being separate at all: 'Deadlock is a semantic property distinct from invariant failure.'\n\nTERMINALITY IS RECOMPUTED, NOT READ OFF THE WALK. An empty successor row is a fact about one state and the model alone, so the deadlock scan calls Model::successors for every DISCOVERED state rather than only the expanded ones — the same move certificate.rs makes when it recomputes rows as it writes them. That is what makes a deadlock sitting unexpanded in a bounded run's frontier reportable, and it is tested with a model built for it (a_terminal_state_in_the_frontier_is_still_reported): ascending admission puts the smaller successor at the queue head, so a state bound tripping during its expansion leaves the terminal state discovered, never expanded, and reported anyway."}	blake3:swgFxrpETfDNTYBSD9Du6FBhd8uArJOOc9-lSqLxeS4
1785610982369428	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAg		item.comment	bn-3e0m	{"body":"PR-8 IMPL-03 (2/3 — API, rendering, evidence).\n\nAPI. check(model, exploration, obligations) -> Result<CheckReport, CheckError>. Obligations is data — a BTreeSet<usize> of predicate indices plus the policy, never closures, the same Target discipline witness.rs holds and for the same three reasons (ambient effect reaching into the check, a property outside the model the result is about, and nothing a property digest could be taken of). A set, not a list: declaring an invariant twice declares it once and the report's rows are ascending index order whatever order someone typed, so no DuplicateInvariant error is needed. No Default on Obligations — the deadlock policy has no safe default value, only a popular one, which is bfs::Bounds' argument. CheckError has one arm, UnknownPredicate{index, declared}, validated for EVERY index before any state is examined, so a refused request leaves no partial report (certificate.rs's typed-failure-before-any-byte discipline). CheckReport carries Scope — Complete{states} or Bounded{states, tripped, frontier}, which are docs/03 section 3's EXHAUSTIVE-FINITE and BOUNDED-STATES exploration levels named — so a reader never has to infer the scope from the outcomes, and verdict() folds with ONE rule: a refutation dominates. Letting an inconclusive sibling mask a found counterexample would be budget exhaustion behaving as a semantic answer. Vacuity is possible (no invariants + Allowed folds to Established, as an empty conjunction is true), is documented on verdict(), is tested, and cannot be hidden: Display always prints the scope, the policy and a line per invariant.\n\nRENDERING. Value-like and byte-stable: Display emits scope / policy / one line per invariant ascending / the deadlock outcome / the folded verdict, with indented continuation lines for witnesses, per docs/34's terminal contract (one result per line or group, stable ids, no color-only meaning). Two tests pin whole renderings byte for byte, including Die Hard's. The --json half of that contract is deliberately NOT here: this crate declares no serialization dependency, and a second encoding of the same value invented in the engine is the duplicate payload docs/34's agent principles forbid — it is the daemon's.\n\nEVIDENCE (36 tests; crate total 212).\n\nchecking_diehard (15): TypeOK Holds over the closed 16; NotSolved Violated at (4,3) at depth 6 with violations=2 — the report says two states falsify rather than showing one of two silently (INV-007) — and the witness EQUALS the pinned Lean path (DieHard.lean:14-22), restated here because this is where that path arrives as a check result rather than as a witness extraction; the reported state equals witness::shortest's endpoint (two scans, two modules, one rule, asserted rather than assumed); no deadlock under the strictest policy; the whole report's byte-exact rendering; all three bounds making BOTH invariants Inconclusive on the three bounded runs bfs_diehard.rs already froze state-for-state (depth 2 = 6 explored / 3 frontier; states 3 and transitions 10 = 3 explored / 2 frontier); nine bound settings none of which ever yields Holds, on the one model whose complete answer is known; the truncated-refutation case at depth bound 6 (14 explored, (4,3) in, (4,0) out, violations=1, Unwitnessed(Truncated{Depth,14}), and the report folds to Refuted over an Inconclusive sibling); determinism twice, including across a rebuilt Model.\n\nchecking_contract (21), on linear one-rule models — a chain, a self-loop, a two-way branch, a chain carrying an unevaluable predicate: a violation at an initial state is a witness with NO steps rather than an absent one; a self-loop is not a deadlock; one exploration under both policies (Deadlocked with a four-step path vs NotJudged{terminal:1}; Refuted vs Established); the closed policy vocabulary and its distinct spellings; a terminal frontier state reported out of a bounded run; a bounded run with no terminal state Inconclusive about deadlock; an unjudged report unaffected by a bound; a predicate overflowing i64 at a reachable state giving EngineError naming state (1) — the ascending-first failing state, deterministic — and the predicate by name, with the SIBLING invariant still answered Holds (one predicate failing does not cost the report the answers it does have); the same model exploring cleanly, which is what makes it a check failure and not an exploration failure; the refusal naming the least undeclared index; obligations-are-a-set; the empty and vacuous reports with their renderings; refutation-dominates; the verdict and INV-008 reason spellings; error source() chaining; determinism over four bound settings and across a rebuilt model."}	blake3:agLhLx5T_UYZf_AoVrKcSkWKV7svHjekcTItRd4FGoU
1785611015241747	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAw		item.comment	bn-3e0m	{"body":"PR-8 IMPL-03 (3/3 — the DEL-07 reading, gates, concerns).\n\nPHASE-A-DEL-07 ANNOTATED, and the exact reasoning. The sentence is 'executable finite reference engine and certificates from Revision 2'.\n\nENGINE HALF: complete with this bone — model + BFS + checking + witness, all five PR-8 bullets now delivered, all executable (the tests run them), all the docs/01 section 7.1 reference path.\n\nCERTIFICATES HALF: 'from Revision 2' means the Revision 2 certificate spec, which for a FINITE engine is docs/03 section 6.1 'Closed finite state space'. bn-3e4l emits it as wire bytes and an independent checker verifies it from bytes alone, sharing no codec with the producer. I walked section 6.1's content list item by item: canonical state table, initial-state ids, action schemas, per-state successor ids or disabled witness, and model/property digests are all carried; the symmetry representative map is explicitly optional; and 'invariant evaluation data or values sufficient to recompute it' is the state table under the state-domain property class.\n\nI also chased bn-3e4l's own honest concern (4) — that SPIKE_REPORT.md:9-12 reads 'closure/type certificate accepted by independent checker' and only the closure half was emitted. On the evidence that concern is over-cautious: kernel-core check.rs:93-146 shows check-finite-closure performing S subset P FIRST (the state-TYPE obligation, docs/16 PO-MOD-003) and then Init subset S and Post(S) subset S, all discharged in one Verdict; the standalone StateType family is a strictly weaker claim over the same table whose wire body is a prefix of the closure body. One artifact discharges both halves, which is why the spike report wrote it as one hyphenated phrase. Emitting kind 2 separately would add no obligation.\n\nWHAT I CHECKED AND RULED OUT of this bullet. (a) The other five docs/03 section 6 families: inductive invariant is solver-backed, refinement and liveness are the temporal lane, partial-order coverage the dossier itself calls research-grade, probabilistic is declarative until the ADR-0016 lanes ship. None is finite-reference-engine output, and the bullet names the finite engine. (b) Dining Philosophers, whose spike facts also read 'closure/type certificate accepted': TV-007 is a WAVE 1 corpus anchor (corpus/tla-examples/PORTING_WAVES.md:22-38 — procedural-to-relational lowering, program counters, atomic labels, fairness, process symmetry), none of which this engine's declaration layer has; its own README says 'P3 remains open until fair temporal semantics and source parity are checked'; docs/26 files it under G1. It is named in the PHASE A EXIT sentence ('Die Hard and Dining Philosophers ... through native API, CLI, and an agent client with identical artifacts'), which is a separate requirement, and not in this deliverable bullet. (c) The label set agrees: req:phase-a-del-07 sits on exactly bn-2d0e, bn-3e4l, bn-3e0m and bn-15n, and all four are now delivered.\n\nSo my honest reading is that the sentence HOLDS, and I annotated. The annotation names the five PR-8 bones and states the closure/type finding, the not-finite-engine families, and the Dining-Philosophers-belongs-to-the-exit-sentence reading in the bullet itself, so the reasoning lives in the plan rather than only in this comment. If the lead reads 'certificates' plural as requiring a second FAMILY rather than a second obligation, the annotation is the thing to revert — that is the one place the reading could differ.\n\nGATES. cargo fmt, clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -D warnings, cargo test --workspace --locked, cargo doc: all clean. Full 'just check' run ONCE, exit 0 (fmt-check, lint, test, boundaries, covenant, governance, dossier, lean). START_HERE PR-8 bullet 3 annotated in prose with no underscored identifiers (traceability.py:77 strips them as markdown emphasis); plan section 21's PHASE-A-DEL-07 bullet annotated; traceability regenerated — PR-8-IMPL-03 and PHASE-A-DEL-07 both active -> satisfied, 722 -> 720 active, 0 untraced Bones, every active requirement still mapped. The registry diff is two status flips plus line renumbering from the 19 lines plan.md gained. validation-results.json NOT touched. notes/plan/plan.typ NOT touched: it is a stale rendering that carries none of the existing (delivered:) annotations and no tool reads it. Commit d4822e3; no merge, no push, no bn state change.\n\nCONCERNS. (1) bn-15n (continuum-kernel-temporal checker, a PR-9 bone) also carries req:phase-a-del-07. It is done so the annotation is not blocked, but the label reads as a mis-attachment — DEL-08 is the kernel-crates bullet and already names bn-15n. Worth the lead's eye. (2) DeadlockPolicy is two-valued and a reviewer may read it as a bool with a hat on. It is not — it renders, it has ALL and as_str, and its docs carry the four-to-two mapping with one member deliberately unmapped — but if the lead prefers a four-member mirror of completion policy, 'closed' would need an invented engine-grain meaning and I declined to invent one. (3) Evidence::Unwitnessed can in principle carry NoWitness::Detached or ForeignExploration; both are structurally unreachable for a report built here (the endpoint came out of the exploration's own table and its predicate was already evaluated there) and both are untested. They are carried rather than collapsed to Truncated so that a mismatched model and exploration is reported instead of silently rendering as truncation. (4) The invariant half reports the shallowest violating state and COUNTS the rest; the deadlock half reports ALL terminal states. Deliberate and documented (an invariant violation is answered by the shortest counterexample, one path by definition; a deadlock report is a report about a set, and docs/27:41 names Dining Philosophers' deadlock by WHICH state it is) but it is an asymmetry and the lead may want them the same shape. (5) A predicate evaluation error aborts that invariant's scan at the ascending-first failing state, so a model with two unevaluable states names the smaller. Deterministic and documented, but it is a first-failure report, not an error set — expr.rs collects an error set WITHIN one expression; across states I stop. (6) Model::successors is recomputed once per discovered state for the deadlock scan even under Allowed, where only the count is used. A reference engine can afford it and the count is genuinely needed, but it is the one place the module does work proportional to the state space for a question the caller declared uninteresting. (7) PR-8-EXIT correctly stays ACTIVE: it is a daemon-API claim ('Die Hard returns 16 states and depth-6 solution through the daemon API') and this bone touches no daemon code. Every input that exit needs now exists in the engine; what remains is the daemon operation carrying a CheckReport as a task result, plus the CLI and agent surfaces the PHASE A exit sentence adds on top."}	blake3:F3f2_WUyyxh587cU2R2iWp6NYVqqbX72Xan96G0NWnU
1785611153072970	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyxAc		item.comment	bn-3e0m	{"body":"Landed @ d161ff3. Lead ran all 14 crate suites in-workspace (212 tests green). Ratified: the schema-anchored outcome vocabulary (assurance-result's established/refuted/inconclusive, spellings tested); Unresolved as a named subset not a copy of InconclusiveReason (carries evidence the dossier enum doesn't, omits arms a finite engine can't construct); THE TRUNCATION ASYMMETRY (refutation survives truncation, clean prefix never passes — Exhausted+falsified Violated-unwitnessed, no workaround of the sibling's refusal); closed-deliberately-unmapped in DeadlockPolicy (translating it would invent the missing environment half); per-state terminality recomputation catching frontier deadlocks. DEL-07 ANNOTATION RATIFIED — the §6.1 item-walk and the state-type-obligation-checked-first finding (kernel-core check.rs S⊆P precedes closure) resolve bn-3e4l's over-caution; certificates-as-obligations is the correct reading. bn-15n's DEL-07 label noted as inert mis-attachment (bone done, requirement satisfied). PR-8-EXIT correctly active — the daemon-API claim awaits PR 5."}	blake3:1pUJ0eJmR6_79kEOLet9FwPfzMvUVPpbNFVY2YzMlUo
