1785697034582367	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyygg		item.comment	bn-1604	{"body":"INV-016 evidence delivered: crates/continuumd/tests/inv016_untrusted_source_evidence.rs (21 tests, all green), commit 19446c7 in-workspace (bn-1604). No src/ changes — the guard is architectural and already in place (registry-driven typed dispatch; rule envelope.no_prose; Fault.detail is &'static str by construction); this bone makes it an executable, permanent regression.\n\nDESIGN\nThree independent legs, named in the file's module doc:\n1. positive — hostile, instruction-shaped strings (an operation name, a JSON injection, protocol framing bytes, \"ignore previous instructions\" prose, a marker-like line, built linearly/const, each <8192 bytes) planted in every free-text/payload field reachable from the 25 *landed* operations' requests, driven through Daemon::dispatch. Asserts control-flow invariance (status/error/verdict/whether a task was minted) and, honestly, the three ways a field turns out inert: never read at all (byte-identical output), closed-vocabulary equality (same Fault, code+detail, for any non-matching string), or content-addressed/echoed (deterministic identity; byte-for-byte echo where the wire reads it back, checked against codec::json::Json's own escaping).\n2. structural — an IDL-derived, registry-driven walk (idl_conformance.rs's own style) mechanically enumerates every free-text field reachable from a landed request and checks it against a hand-maintained list (SD-12/budget_dimensions.rs's anti-drift device). Separately derives the 25-of-72 landed/unlanded boundary mechanically via codec::operations::decode_arguments (never hand-copied off its doc comment) and proves every unlanded operation is inert for *any* payload whatsoever, including bytes that are not even valid JSON.\n3. mutant — a small, src/-untouched stand-in for an intent.reject handler that *does* let `reason` decide its answer (Accepted vs Rejected), proving the invariance assertion the other legs run actually catches that class of violation and is not vacuously silent.\n\nCLAUSE -> TEST MAP\n- FileOverlay.content (workspace.create, raw file bytes — the plainest \"production payload\"): positive_workspace_create_overlay_content_is_stored_never_interpreted\n- FileOverlay.path: positive_workspace_create_overlay_path_is_a_structural_identifier_not_an_instruction\n- IntentChangeSet.rationale (\"comments\"): positive_intent_propose_revision_rationale_is_never_read_at_all — byte-identical Error across every hostile shape\n- IntentRejectRequest.reason: positive_intent_reject_reason_is_never_read_at_all — byte-identical payload\n- IntentLockRequest.policy keys/values (closed RFC-0037 vocab): positive_intent_lock_policy_key_is_closed_vocabulary_equality_not_a_parse / _value_\n- EvidenceQuery.claim_id: positive_evidence_query_claim_id_is_a_closed_equality_filter_not_a_keyword_search (substring non-containment checked both directions)\n- ObserveIngestRequest.instrumentation_profile (\"logs\"): positive_observe_ingest_instrumentation_profile_is_echoed_verbatim_never_interpreted — flagship byte-level echo via observe.ingest -> evidence.get's provenance.tool\n- Target.id (\"model strings\"/target identifiers) under AllClaims: positive_verification_start_target_id_under_all_claims_is_hashed_and_echoed_never_branched_on — hashed into task identity, echoed verbatim via verification.start -> verification.result\n- Target.id under Property/Claim: positive_verification_start_target_id_under_property_is_closed_vocabulary_equality_not_a_parse — Model::predicate_index equality lookup, contrasted against the one real predicate name (diehard::TYPE_OK) which is admitted\n- Structural inventory + landed/unlanded boundary: structural::the_free_text_inventory_reachable_from_every_landed_request_is_exactly_this_list, ::exactly_twenty_five_of_the_seventy_two_operations_are_landed, ::every_unlanded_operation_is_inert_for_any_payload_whatsoever (+ 3 anti-vacuity/narrowing tests)\n- Mutant/anti-vacuity: mutant::the_invariance_assertion_passes_the_clean_fixture, ::the_invariance_assertion_catches_the_violating_fixture\n\nTEST COUNTS / GATES\nNew file: 21 tests, all passing. Full continuumd suite: 16 pre-existing suites (221 tests) unchanged and green, plus this 17th suite — nothing in crates/continuumd/src touched. Gates run in-workspace, all exit 0: cargo fmt --all --check; cargo clippy -p continuumd --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings (zero warnings); cargo test -p continuumd --locked; full `just check` (fmt, workspace-wide clippy+test, boundaries, kernel covenant, governance x3, dossier, lean) — ran to completion, status \"pass\", exit 0.\n\nDOSSIER\nAppended `(delivered: bn-1604 — crates/continuumd/tests/inv016_untrusted_source_evidence.rs: ...)` to the plan.md INV-016 heading; regenerated notes/plan/notes/{PLAN_REQUIREMENTS.json,PLAN_BONE_TRACEABILITY.md} (INV-016 now \"satisfied\", invariant category 18/18 -> 17 active + 1 satisfied). validate_dossier.py exits 0. validation-results.json untouched. No governance checker's tolerance needed extending — check_code_policy/check_adr_process/check_claim_governance all pass unchanged.\n\nCONCERNS\n- Opaque-carried \"production payloads\" (IntentChangeSet.changes, IntentAcceptRequest.acceptance — canonical-JSON blobs, not free text) are deliberately out of this file's scope; documented in the module doc rather than field-audited, since the one place this workspace looks inside one (propose_revision's lock check) already reduces to the same closed-vocabulary-equality shape this file proves elsewhere. A future bone giving continuum-intent's own JSON-field handling INV-016 evidence would sit in that crate, not here.\n- intent.propose_revision has no real \"propose\" implementation yet (unconditionally UnsupportedSemanticFeature unless a locked field is touched), so rationale's \"never read at all\" result is honest today but should be re-asserted once that operation's real logic lands — the test will keep passing vacuously true in spirit only until then; flagged in the file's own doc as an honesty boundary, not hidden.\n- The 47 unlanded operations are covered by construction (decode_arguments never even parses their arguments), not by per-field fixtures; structural::exactly_twenty_five_of_the_seventy_two_operations_are_landed will fail loudly the moment a new family lands, which is the intended forcing function for extending this file rather than a gap."}	blake3:taXjw-CAL9mt36u_AySLIrdhojnnkiNvStK6oQmm2Qo
1785697122670104	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyywg		item.comment	bn-1604	{"body":"Landed @ 310cd679554b. Lead re-verified post-rebase in-workspace (rebased over wave-23 closes; single traceability conflict resolved --keep epoch + regen): all continuumd suites green including the new 21-test evidence file, validator pass, no src changes confirmed by diff.\n\nRATIFIED. (1) The three-leg design: hostile payloads through real dispatch with byte-level echo checks against the codec's own escaping; the IDL-derived free-text inventory held against a hand-maintained list (drift fails loudly); the landed/unlanded 25-of-72 boundary derived mechanically from decode_arguments rather than copied from a doc comment, with the other 47 proven inert for arbitrary garbage. (2) The src-untouched mutant handler as anti-vacuity — proving the invariance assertion catches the free-text-decides-the-answer class without planting a real vulnerability. (3) The honest scope statements: Opaque-carried payloads documented as continuum-intent's audit scope, not silently skipped.\n\nCONCERNS RECORDED. (a) intent.propose_revision's rationale 'never read' result must be re-checked when real propose logic lands — noted for the intent-crate backlog rider. (b) The structural leg's 25-count assertion failing loudly when a new family lands is a feature: the follow-up it forces is per-field fixtures for the new surface. (c) 47 unlanded operations covered by construction — acceptable at this phase; the per-field sweep grows with the landed set.\n\nINV-016 satisfied via heading annotation; coverage stays on this bone's label per the heading-category convention."}	blake3:oa-FXq3I-mT7DTwVXGvtpV4a4Dp_XUi9HNirZfXq3EY
1785697122710565	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyzAg		item.move	bn-1604	{"state":"done"}	blake3:x1ycEM4q0EeRda8ZCmGVyoXe649Otx910_Wy7AsBfm0
1785698415643783	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyzQg		item.comment	bn-nio3	{"body":"T12 evidence delivered in-workspace (bn-nio3), commit 4c7dae2.\n\nSIX-CONTROL AUDIT MAP (file / rule id / fixture, cited not rebuilt):\n1. pinned lockfile — Cargo.lock committed; every non-fmt cargo gate in Justfile\n   carries --locked (lint/test/build); rust-toolchain.toml exact channel pin +\n   GOV-1-01 toolchain-pinned (check_code_policy.py, fixtures gov-1-01-*).\n2. dependency allowlist — GOV-1-07 dependency-rationale (check_code_policy.py)\n   against tools/governance/dependency-rationale.toml; every dependency has\n   exactly one rationale + TCB class entry, fixtures gov-1-07-*.\n3. cargo-vet/advisory scanning — GENUINE GAP, now closed (see below).\n4. unsafe boundary audit — GOV-1-02 unsafe-forbidden (check_code_policy.py);\n   workspace-wide unsafe_code = \"forbid\", no relaxation; fixtures gov-1-02-*.\n5. reproducible build metadata — GOV-1-01 (toolchain pin) + --locked gate\n   discipline + Cargo.toml [profile.release] overflow-checks = true +\n   KCOV-09 receipt-conformance (check_kernel_covenant.py: kernel receipts name\n   WIRE_EPOCH_ID and build/checker identity per INV-014). No single existing\n   id covers this bullet; treated as an explicit composite citation rather\n   than inventing a new one.\n6. minimal kernel dependencies — GOV-1-06 no-network-in-checker\n   (check_code_policy.py) + KCOV-06 dependency-freedom (check_kernel_covenant.py:\n   ADMITTED_KERNEL_DEPENDENCIES is the empty set, so the four\n   continuum-kernel-* crates admit zero deps, workspace or external) +\n   check_crate_boundaries.py (certificate-checker-not-search,\n   kernel-is-synchronous).\n\nGAP FILLED (control 3): tools/governance/check_dependency_audit.py — a new\nstdlib-only, self-testing checker. Design: a committed cargo-vet-style audits\nmanifest (tools/governance/dependency-audits.toml, [[audits.<pkg>]] entries)\nchecked against Cargo.lock's externally-sourced package closure (blake3 + its\n9 transitive crates: arrayref, arrayvec, cc, cfg-if, constant_time_eq,\ncpufeatures, find-msvc-tools, libc, shlex — the whole external dependency\ntree; workspace-internal crates are out of scope, that's GOV-1-07's job).\nEach entry pins version, checksum, who, criteria (closed set\n{safe-to-deploy, safe-to-run}), and review notes. Rule fails closed on: no\nmanifest; malformed manifest; a Cargo.lock external package with no matching\nentry (new OR upgraded dependency, untrusted until vetted); a stale entry for\na package Cargo.lock no longer resolves; missing/short who or notes;\nunrecognized criteria; and — the flagship hostile-mutant case — a checksum\nmismatch between the audit entry and Cargo.lock for the SAME version string\n(a same-version supply-chain substitution). No network access anywhere;\nrefreshing the manifest against an advisory source is an explicit human\naction, stated as such in the module docstring. 9 named sub-checks, 10\nfixtures (one per sub-check, two for every-external-dependency-is-audited\ncovering both the new-dependency and upgraded-dependency cases). Evidence:\ntools/governance/evidence/dependency-audit.json.\n\nEVIDENCE BINDING: tools/governance/check_t12_evidence.py assembles\ntools/governance/evidence/t12.json, binding all six controls to their\nenforcement points. Critically, it does not copy status strings out of\npossibly-stale committed evidence files — it subprocess-invokes each delegate\nchecker's actual --self-test and real run (check_code_policy.py,\ncheck_kernel_covenant.py, check_crate_boundaries.py, check_dependency_audit.py),\nreads the specific requirement ids off their JSON reports, and adds two direct\nchecks of its own (locked-gates, overflow-checks) that no existing id owns.\nA regression in ANY cited control — including the five pre-existing ones —\nfails this gate too, verified live: I temporarily deleted the shlex audit\nentry and confirmed check_dependency_audit.py failed, check_t12_evidence.py\ncorrectly reported only \"cargo-vet/advisory scanning\": \"fail\" (the other five\ncontrols stayed \"pass\"), then restored the file byte-identical and reconfirmed\ngreen.\n\nGATE WIRING: both checkers added to the Justfile `governance` recipe\n(self-test then real, matching check_code_policy.py/check_adr_process.py/\ncheck_claim_governance.py's exact pattern). `just governance` and full\n`just check` (fmt, lint, test, boundaries, covenant, governance, dossier,\nlean) both exit 0.\n\nDOCS/TRACEABILITY: docs/09 T12 heading annotated\n\"(delivered: bn-nio3 — dependency vet/advisory-scanning gap closed, six\ncontrols bound in tools/governance/evidence/t12.json)\" — no parens inside the\npointer text, since notes/plan/tools/traceability.py's delivered-stripping\nregex truncates at the first \")\". Regenerated traceability\n(notes/plan/notes/PLAN_REQUIREMENTS.json T12 now \"satisfied\";\nPLAN_BONE_TRACEABILITY.md threat row updated); dossier validator exits 0.\nvalidation-results.json untouched. No crates/ or .bones/ changes.\n\nCOUNTS: 2 new checker scripts (~590 + ~510 lines), 1 audit manifest (10\nentries), 2 evidence JSONs (both byte-stable on re-run, verified), 10 new\nfixtures under tools/governance/fixtures/dependency-audit/. 4 files modified\n(Justfile, docs/09, PLAN_REQUIREMENTS.json, PLAN_BONE_TRACEABILITY.md).\n\nCONCERNS:\n- \"Reproducible build metadata\" and \"pinned lockfile\" are composite citations\n  spanning multiple ids/direct checks rather than one owned rule — stated\n  explicitly as such in check_t12_evidence.py's docstring and the evidence\n  \"boundary\" field, not hidden.\n- The audit manifest's per-crate review notes (arrayref, arrayvec, cfg-if,\n  constant_time_eq, cpufeatures, cc, find-msvc-tools, libc, shlex, blake3)\n  reflect general knowledge of these well-known crates, not a fresh\n  line-by-line source diff performed against a live fetch (the checker\n  itself makes no network call, by design; a human refresh against whatever\n  advisory source is trusted is the intended follow-up action, stated in the\n  manifest's own header).\n- check_t12_evidence.py does not query git to confirm Cargo.lock is tracked\n  (no git subprocess); relies on file-presence + the fact that no gate in\n  `just check` would survive an untracked, gitignored Cargo.lock. Noted as a\n  stated limitation, not hidden.\n- README-gov1.md still says GOV §1 checkers are \"Not yet wired into just\n  check\" — that line was already stale before this Bone (the Justfile already\n  wires check_code_policy.py in); left untouched as out of scope for T12.\n\nBone left open per instructions (not closing bn-nio3)."}	blake3:JQB9wPLEtZYaWD6tUpWcJS1HbQomkvl3Hwo4mRkrNdA
1785698509649718	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyzgg		item.comment	bn-nio3	{"body":"Landed @ b878b00b79cb. Lead re-verified post-sync in-workspace: just governance exit 0 (all five checkers, self-test first), validator pass, docs/09 T12 heading annotation matches the T01/T02 convention, Justfile wiring matches the existing pattern exactly.\n\nRATIFIED. (1) The audit correctly cited five live controls rather than rebuilding them — GOV-1-01/02/06/07, KCOV, boundaries — and closed the one genuine gap (cargo-vet/advisory posture). (2) The manifest design: full audits only (stricter than upstream's delta audits), two criteria defined once in-file, and the checksum binding — an audit certifies the reviewer saw the same bytes Cargo.lock pins, so same-version different-bytes substitution fails as loudly as a version bump. Fail-closed on new, upgraded, stale, and malformed entries. No network in any gate — refresh is deliberately human friction. (3) check_t12_evidence.py re-RUNNING the delegate checkers rather than copying status strings — the binding cannot go stale — plus the live single-control-regression demonstration (deleted audit entry surfaces as exactly that control failing). (4) The disclosure discipline: the manifest states in-file that entries are documentation-level reviews by continuum-dev with no live fetch, and that a future refresh may name a different reviewer. DISPOSITION FOR THE RECORD: these vet entries are the honest floor, not a claim of deep source audit — a human reviewer deepening any entry supersedes it by editing who/notes, and the checker's shape makes that a visible one-line diff.\n\nT12 satisfied via heading annotation; coverage stays on this bone's label per the heading-category convention."}	blake3:EENEDA1F2KKVXxTfM2fb53oKWr9_AJa-5gZcyYA6w0M
1785698509689608	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyzwg		item.move	bn-nio3	{"state":"done"}	blake3:b7sB_6RxMJ5NQbw20WJU5eO0xQJex-WusEGHm73JTaQ
1785698522104419	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy0Ag		item.comment	bn-23j7s	{"body":"Delivered (1/2 — criteria and wire audit). Two commits in .maw/workspaces/bn-23j7s: f91b34d (implementation + evidence), 0d17829 (START_HERE annotation + regen). NOT closed, not merged.\n\n## Per acceptance criterion\n\n**CLAIMED — `verification::unenforced` gone; omissions derived.** Deleted. `daemon/budget.rs::omissions_of` asks `BudgetLedger::omissions` over `METERS = MeterSet::STATES_ONLY`; the eight subjects, the `unsupported` reason token and their SD-12 order are what the array emitted, so `verification.start`'s bytes are unchanged. The derivation is held load-bearing rather than re-spelled: a unit test asserts the count equals `MeterSet::STATES_ONLY.unmetered().len()`, and that a `MeterSet::all()` ledger owes nothing with no edit here.\n\n**CLAIMED — TaskEntry on the ledger; bounds_of and cost as projections.** `TaskEntry` lost `budget: Budget` and `bounds: Bounds`, gained `ledger: BudgetLedger`. `budget()` is `wire_budget(ledger)` (lossless both ways — every wire dimension is an optional u64 under one of three newtypes), `bounds()` is `budget::bounds_of`, `cost()` is `budget::cost_of`. `TaskEntry` also lost `Clone/PartialEq/Eq` (a ledger is not `Clone` by design); all three derives were unused.\n\n**CLAIMED — `task.update_budget` five arms, Suspend included, walked by a daemon test.** `budget::update` applies the request's ceilings per dimension and returns `Vec<UpdateOutcome>`. Walked by `lowering_below_committed_spend_parks_with_evidence_and_a_continuation`, `the_first_three_arms_of_the_legality_table_are_live_on_the_wire`, `a_ceiling_nothing_meters_is_recorded_and_named_on_the_answer`.\n\n**CLAIMED (identity + wire surface) / DECLINED (durability) — committed partial evidence with artifact identity.** `Publications` replaces the `publications: u32` counter. Each publication carries the content identity of the campaign that produced it (task handle, commit ordinal, snapshot, state count, closure, queue-ordered frontier), through the daemon's existing `ContentIdentifier` seam. It is a linear typestate: `stage` -> `commit` | `discard`, no third exit, and `committed()`/`artifacts()` cannot see a staged one — so \"uncommitted partials are absent, never half-visible\" is a property of the type, not of `run_in`'s paths being careful. Wire surface is `ResultEnvelope.artifacts`: `kind: \"task\"`, the task handle, the publication's own `commitment`, monotone across a resume. **Durability is not claimed** — nothing writes a task table to disk and that is bn-3dr's. What is asserted is the honest half: `two_daemons_name_one_campaigns_publications_identically`, i.e. the name is a function of the campaign and of nothing about the process, so it is something a store could later be asked to return. Stated the same way in the annotation and in the test file's header.\n\n**CLAIMED — BudgetExhausted carries a continuation or the typed non_resumable_reason (SD-13).** `result::failure` hard-coded both `Error.continuation` and `Error.non_resumable_reason` to `Absent`, so every `BudgetExhausted` this daemon raised was exactly the dead end SD-13's `oneOf` forbids. `Fault` gained a typed `Resumption` (`NotApplicable` | `From(cont)` | `NonResumable(&'static str)`); `Fault::exhausted` and `Fault::exhausted_from` are the only constructors of that code and neither can produce it without one of the two answers, plus a `debug_assert` at the envelope. The reason is the fault's own detail — one string, which is what `region::NO_CONTINUATION`'s doc already records the wire field as being written from — so `task.status` and the failing call cannot disagree.\n\n**CLAIMED — START_HERE annotated.** Bullet annotated with the honest scope; the paragraph below rewritten from \"why this is not annotated\" to what now exists and what does not; the intro sentence corrected (one thing not claimed, two bullets that changed hands). Regen + validate_dossier exit 0. `validation-results.json` untouched.\n\n## Wire-visibility audit\n\nByte-identical for every existing flow — the whole pre-existing suite passes with **zero** behavioural test edits (only `entry.publications` -> `entry.publications()`, a field-to-method accessor change at 11 sites). Specifically unchanged: `TaskRecord.budget`, `TaskRecord.cost` (including `states` *absent* on a task with no campaign — `cost_of` gates on a campaign having given the meter a reading, rather than reporting the ledger's `Some(0)` starting point), `verification.start`'s omission list, and `TaskRecord.non_resumable_reason`.\n\nThree changes are wire-visible; none is silent drift:\n\n1. **`ResultEnvelope.artifacts` on task-observing answers**, `[]` -> one `ArtifactRef` per committed publication. Required by criterion 4 (\"the wire surface\"); RFC 0026 treats `artifacts` as *the* list a result names its artifacts in (\"a dedup that is invisible in `artifacts` but visible in reported cost is still an oracle\"; `ArtifactRef.redacted` is declared as a member of this list). Additive; no golden test asserted emptiness for a task operation.\n2. **`Error.non_resumable_reason` on `BudgetExhausted`**, absent -> present. Required by RFC 0026 §10.3 and SD-13; a conformance fix, since the field has existed since 3.0 and was being defaulted away.\n3. **INV-007 manifest on `task.update_budget` and `task.resume`.** An operation that *accepts* a budget owes the manifest for it, which is what `verification.start` has always done; these two accepted a ceiling nothing meters and said nothing, which was the `Unenforced` arm having no wire surface at all. `task.status`/`subscribe`/`cancel` are reads and keep their current manifest — stated in code so the asymmetry is a decision rather than an oversight.\n\nNo IDL, schema or registry change: every field written already existed. **No F-flag raised**, and I want that decision visible for review — all three land inside fields the IDL declares, with RFC 0026 or SD-13 as the governing text, so there is no shape the wire does not already carry for a flag to be raised against. If the lead reads (1) as needing one, it is one paragraph in RFC 0026's flag section and nothing in the code moves."}	blake3:jApuGsW0jCb7KR56L0ch4tczKsc0jUXQwH6jhIanauk
1785698547484568	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy0Qg		item.comment	bn-23j7s	{"body":"Delivered (2/2 — design decisions, counts, gates, concerns).\n\n## Design decisions\n\n- **Charge the delta, not the run.** `bfs::explore` has no partial-state entry point, so a resumed walk re-explores the parked prefix. `budget::charge_states` charges `campaign.states() - recorded`, making spend the walk's high-water mark: a Die Hard campaign that parks at 3 and closes at 16 cost 16, not 19. Without this, `cost` projected from the ledger would have broken the existing 16-states assertion after a resume — i.e. the projection would have been wire-visible where it must not be.\n- **A ceiling below committed spend is recorded and is not a bound.** `budget::bounds_of` floors the engine's state bound at recorded spend. This is what makes the `Suspend` arm mean something at a synchronous grain, and it fixes a real bug: before, the lowered ceiling became the engine bound directly, so the next `task.resume` came back with a *smaller* campaign and a monotone `cost.states` went **down** — the silent truncation INV-009 prohibits. `lowering_below_committed_spend_parks_with_evidence_and_a_continuation` pins the fix: the ceiling is recorded at 2, `cost.states` stays 3, both publications survive, the task stays `Suspended` with its continuation, and the resumed run parks again rather than un-exploring.\n- **B18 is not a status transition here.** A parked task is already `Suspended` and already holds the continuation, so the arm re-states a state rather than moving to one, and `rule task.status_monotonic` is undisturbed. The new `task.budget_suspended` milestone is what makes the arm legible beside `task.budget_updated`: both facts are true — the ceiling was recorded, and it cannot bind this run.\n- **Publications named on `ArtifactRef`, not on `TaskRecord.committed_evidence`.** The latter is `list<EvidenceHandle>` (`ev_`, PR 7's evidence graph); filling it would mint handles that resolve nowhere and would silently retire the `task.committed_evidence` omission. Rejected.\n- **`Fault` is no longer `Copy`** (a `Resumption` can carry a handle). No call site needed changing.\n- **`task.resume`'s budget goes through the same ledger call** as `task.update_budget` rather than a second write of the two fields the ledger replaced; the `Suspend` arm's effect there is the `bounds_of` floor, and the milestone is recorded.\n\n## Diff shape\n\nZero diff in `crates/continuum-task/` — the budget modules and `region.rs` needed none, as the bone predicted. `crates/continuumd/src/daemon/region.rs`: zero diff. New files: `crates/continuumd/src/daemon/budget.rs` (the join, 11 unit tests) and `crates/continuumd/tests/pr6_impl02_budget_evidence.rs` (10 tests, module-doc clause→test map, house style). Touched: `daemon/{task,verification,family,result,mod}.rs`. `crates/continuumd/tests/inv016_untrusted_source_evidence.rs` (bn-1604's) untouched and passing.\n\n## Counts and gates\n\n- continuum-task **141** (139 + 2 doctests) — unchanged, as the bone required.\n- continuumd **274** across 18 suites. The bone's stated baseline was 232/16; bn-1604's `inv016_untrusted_source_evidence.rs` (21 tests) was already merged into this workspace's HEAD at 310cd67, taking it to 253/17; this bone adds 11 unit tests in `daemon/budget.rs` and the 10-test evidence suite.\n- `cargo fmt --all --check` — 0. `cargo clippy --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings` — 0. `cargo test -p continuumd -p continuum-task --locked` — 0. `python3 notes/plan/tools/generate_traceability.py` then `uv run --with jsonschema python3 tools/validate_dossier.py` — 0 (`status: pass`, `program_status: ready`). Full `just check` — 0.\n\n## Concerns\n\n1. The **`artifacts` decision** is the one I would most want a second reading of: it is the broadest of the three wire changes, and its governing text is inferred from RFC 0026's reasoning *about* the field rather than stated as an obligation on task operations. If the lead wants it narrowed (e.g. only the answers that report committed *partial* evidence — start/cancel/update_budget — rather than every task-observing answer), it is one call site each.\n2. `run_in` mints the publication identity from the campaign's own facts and does **not** include the epochs, unlike the `cont_*` preimage. Deliberate — a publication is the campaign's content and the epochs ride the record — but if bn-3dr's store wants epoch-scoped publication identities, `budget::publication_commitment` is the single function to widen.\n3. `bounds_of`'s floor makes `TaskRecord.budget.states` and the bound the next run actually takes **differ** on the B18 path. That is `Suspension` exactly as bn-1gc ratified it (the ceiling is refused *as an enforceable bound on the current run*, not refused outright), and it is documented at both ends, but it is the one place where a caller reading `budget` alone would predict the wrong bound. If a future revision wants that visible, the honest surface is an omission or a warning on the update answer, not a change to the reported budget.\n4. `TaskEntry` losing `Clone`/`PartialEq`/`Eq` is a public-API narrowing of `continuumd::daemon::task`. Nothing in the workspace used them, and the wire value `TaskRecord` is still comparable, but a downstream reader of the daemon's state would notice."}	blake3:f3U7UrhaxhKLjSjF1DuOiMRBR07InYJT1Br9lujqED0
1785698679267933	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy0gg		item.comment	bn-23j7s	{"body":"Commit-hash correction and one extra commit. The lead rebased this workspace onto bn-nio3 (T12 evidence) while I was writing the report, so the hashes in comment 1/2 are stale. Current: dfc0b4b (implementation + evidence), 047e354 (START_HERE annotation + regen), 9f27ee8 (traceability regen).\n\nThe third is a merge artifact, not new work: bn-nio3 and this bone each flipped one requirement, and the rebase left PLAN_BONE_TRACEABILITY.md's summary line disagreeing with its own per-category table by one active requirement — which validate_dossier.py's staleness check catches. Regenerated, nothing else in the diff. Full `just check` re-run on the rebased tree: exit 0. Worth knowing for the next sequential merge: any two workspaces that both touch a requirement's status will produce this, and the fix is always a regen."}	blake3:LCHjUEwq1dZdWY3Hgx5E4KRycRgZvQog6T0S0Zy1uIg
1785698785869634	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy0wg		item.comment	bn-23j7s	{"body":"Landed @ 36a49bdb2d34. Lead re-verified post-sync in-workspace: all 29 suite results green across continuumd (274/18) + continuum-task (141, unchanged), clippy clean, validator pass, zero-diff in continuum-task/ and daemon/region.rs confirmed by diff.\n\nNO-F-FLAG DECISION RATIFIED, with the second reading requested on artifacts. All three wire-visible changes write fields the IDL has declared since 3.0, each with governing text: (1) ResultEnvelope.artifacts — RFC 0026 treats artifacts as THE list a result names its artifacts in, and the all-task-observing-answers scope is ACCEPTED over the narrower alternative: narrowing to start/cancel/update_budget would make task.status report [] for a task whose park just reported artifacts, an inconsistency worse than breadth; additive, no golden asserted emptiness. (2) non_resumable_reason on BudgetExhausted — a conformance FIX (SD-13's oneOf forbade the dead end being emitted); the typed Resumption with no constructor that can omit both answers is the house pattern. (3) INV-007 manifest on the two other budget-accepting operations — an operation that accepts a budget owes the manifest; the writes-vs-reads asymmetry stated in code as a decision.\n\nRATIFIED FURTHER. (a) The INV-009 silent-truncation fix: floor-at-recorded-spend making the withdrawn ceiling recorded-but-not-a-bound is exactly bn-1gc's ratified Suspension semantics reaching the engine bound; monotone cost.states can never go down. (b) Charge-the-delta (spend as high-water mark) — required precisely so the ledger projection stayed byte-invisible on existing flows. (c) Publications as linear typestate (stage→commit|discard, no third exit) making uncommitted-never-half-visible a type property. (d) The committed_evidence rejection — filling list<EvidenceHandle> with handles that resolve nowhere would have silently retired an honest omission; right call. (e) Public narrowing of TaskEntry (Clone/PartialEq/Eq unused) accepted.\n\nCONCERNS ROUTED. Publication identity omitting epochs → noted on bn-3dr (widen budget::publication_commitment there if the store needs epoch-scoped identities). budget.states-vs-actual-bound divergence on the B18 path → documented at both ends, accepted; if a future revision wants it wire-visible the honest surface is an omission/warning on the update answer, not a changed reported budget — recorded here for bn-1n6r's exit narrative.\n\nPR-6 IMPL-02 identity+wire half CLAIMED, durability honestly with bn-3dr. All six PR-6 implementation bullets now annotated."}	blake3:mo-HAmhZkH3WZgmY9WJXXm3-EiV3Xor3ArOsWoQHjjw
1785698785909178	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy1Ag		item.move	bn-23j7s	{"state":"done"}	blake3:yuQ9dFPg4AmULd5rD1RC21lTUESHJF9bIHC0RvGwGtA
1785698785938716	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy1Qg		item.comment	bn-3dr	{"body":"Note from bn-23j7s's landing (lead): publication identity (budget::publication_commitment in continuumd/src/daemon/budget.rs) deliberately omits the epochs — a publication is the campaign's content and the epochs ride the record. If this bone's durable store wants epoch-scoped publication identities, that function is the single point to widen. Also: IMPL-02's durability criterion ('committed partial evidence survives daemon restart; uncommitted partials are absent, never half-visible') now has its identity+wire half landed — publications are NAMED (two_daemons_name_one_campaigns_publications_identically pins process-independence), so the store can be asked to return them; this bone owes the restart machinery."}	blake3:9sVSA9ZLqMQjZY9S43qus29BYxOGUcHlZ6Akzc7j39c
