1785699982593170	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy2gg		item.comment	bn-3p32	{"body":"G0-DX-14 falsification campaign — FALSIFIED-WITH-PIN (closure, not the ledger).\n\nCommit 652f20f in .maw/workspaces/bn-3p32. Two new files, no src/ change, no dossier change.\n- crates/continuumd/tests/dx14_falsification.rs (13 attacks)\n- crates/continuum-task/tests/dx14_falsification_calculus.rs (5 attacks)\n\n== THE ATTACK THAT LANDED ==\nA1 falsification_a_cancelled_task_is_still_writable_through_task_resume.\ntask.resume applies the request's optional budget to the task's BudgetLedger BEFORE it checks terminality (crates/continuumd/src/daemon/task.rs, fn resume: the 'if let Optional::Present(budget) = &request.budget' block sits above the 'if entry.is_terminal()' early return). task.update_budget refuses a terminal task and documents exactly why — 'a terminal task's budget is a historical fact, and rewriting it would make its recorded cost unreadable'. Nothing prunes the continuation table, so a cancelled task's cont_* still resolves. Therefore the wire sequence\n\n  verification.start (parks) -> task.cancel -> task.resume(continuation, budget)\n\nreaches that write on a task whose status is already Cancelled, and:\n- TaskRecord.budget is rewritten after the task closed — the exact rewrite update_budget refuses, reached through a different door;\n- with the new ceiling below recorded spend the B18 arm fires and 'task.budget_suspended' is appended to a CANCELLED task's milestones (plus a Milestone TaskEvent), i.e. the record claims a terminal task parked;\n- the cancelled task then reports a cost above its own declared budget.\n\nScope of the break, precisely: the pass condition's two NAMED conjuncts held. No obligation leaks (TaskRegions::is_total asserted after every beat of every attack, incl. all 120 orderings of A11) and nothing left neither committed nor absent; the resume returns before verification::advance so it opens no region, runs no work, publishes nothing, mints no continuation, status stays Cancelled. What broke is the CLOSURE the row's question presupposes ('close correctly'): a cancelled task's record is not final. A2 is the localising control — the same sequence with no budget leaves the record byte-identical — so the repair is one line moved, and A2 catches a repair that moves it too far.\n\nPinned, not fixed. Module-doc labelled FALSIFICATION with what should hold / what actually happens. Matrix row NOT edited and NOT reverted: status challenged, the row edit is the lead's.\n\n== WHAT bn-2zy TOOK FOR GRANTED, AND WHAT I ATTACKED ==\nbn-2zy drives each lane's intended program and cancels after each phase. It never issues an operation AFTER the cancel except another cancel; never sends a budget on a resume of a dead task; its byte-identity check is on a rendering, not on wire frames; the region-arm/continuation agreement is only a debug_assert_eq! in src (compiled out in release, and only on profiles it happens to reach); and at the calculus grain it only ever issues LEGAL steps.\n\n== ATTACK CATALOGUE ==\nDaemon grain (13):\nA1 cancel-then-resume-with-budget — FALSIFIED (above).\nA2 cancel-then-resume-without-budget — held (record byte-identical).\nA3 region arm vs task continuation vs wire nullable over 8 reachable residual profiles (fresh-park, twice-parked, closed, parked-then-closed, failed, rebudgeted-park, lowered-park, read-only) — held; the debug_assert is now a real assertion and the sweep asserts it reaches both arms.\nA4 the cancel-arm constructor claim as a WIRE property: continuation present => artifacts non-empty; artifacts empty => continuation null; task.cancel agrees with task.status — held, all three arms reached.\nA5 idempotence: three cancels under one request_id and three distinct idempotency keys; second and third byte-identical as wire frames — held.\nA6 B18: lower below committed spend, then cancel — held (arm stays committed-with-continuation, artifacts named).\nA7 B18: lower/resume/lower x3 — held (spend and publications monotone, still parked, total).\nA8 non-resumable lane (states=0, BudgetExhausted + non_resumable_reason) through raise/cancel/raise — held, never acquires a continuation.\nA9 cancelled daemon's continuation on a fresh daemon — held (single CapabilityDenied, no task created); control: once B runs the same campaign it mints the identical task_* and cont_*, so the refusal was about held state.\nA10 stale continuation replayed x3 after cancel-and-re-resume — held (no scope opened, nothing published).\nA11 all 120 orderings of {cancel, resume-wide, resume-narrow, lower, raise} — held; is_total and 'nothing staged at rest' asserted after EVERY step of every ordering, both-or-neither on every cancel.\nA12 identical verification.start re-issued after the cancel — held, CONCERN recorded below.\nA13 the whole attack programme rendered byte-identically on two fresh daemons — held (so the A1 violation is deterministic too).\n\nCalculus grain (5) — the composition attack the wire cannot make:\nC1 all 216 three-operation hostile programs {close,cancel,drain,finalize,spawn,step} injected against a worker mid-publication, then a teardown — held: total, balanced, every admitted worker terminal (incl. one a hostile spawn added), staged half never in flight and never manufactured into a commit; asserts it reaches both the discarded-staged and committed-staged endings.\nC2 the same 216 cross-checked against EvidenceBook::reconcile, plus 'no reservation survives a teardown' — held.\nC3 control: a deliberately withheld mirror IS caught by reconcile — held (cross-check load-bearing).\nC4 composed refusals leave no residue: 216 programs against a finalized region, all 648 calls refused, tree rendering (incl. obligation ledger) byte-identical every time — held.\nC5 four-deep chain, workers in four states with the deepest mid-publication, cancelled at the root — held: every level cancelled, staged half discarded, parked level's committed evidence untruncated, ledger balanced, book reconciles.\n\n== CONCERN (not a violation, pinned in A12) ==\nA task_* handle is the content identity of (snapshot, intent, target, portfolio, priority, budget, epochs), and verification.start's cached-result lane fires only for a Completed task. So re-issuing the identical start after a cancel returns the CANCELLED task on the task_started lane and runs nothing: a cancelled campaign's identity is permanently un-restartable on that daemon unless the caller varies the budget or target. task.status is authoritative and says cancelled, so nothing is misreported to a caller who reads it, but the envelope lane says task_started for a task that will never run. Pinned so a change to it is a change to a test.\n\n== COUNTS ==\n18 attacks / 18 tests, 17 held + 1 falsified-and-pinned. Sweeps: 120 daemon orderings x up to 5 dispatches; 216 x 3 hostile calculus programs (x2 sweeps) + 648 composed refusals; 8 residual profiles x 3 tests. All bounded and linear; largest model is 16-state Die Hard. ~0.35s (continuumd), <0.01s (continuum-task).\n\n== GATES (in-workspace) ==\ncargo fmt --all --check OK; cargo clippy --all-targets --locked -D warnings OK; cargo test -p continuumd -p continuum-task --locked OK (all pre-existing tests unchanged and passing); just check EXIT 0. Dossier untouched so gate 4 not applicable. No .bones/ change in the commit. bn-1kp6's dx03_falsification.rs and the DX-03 row untouched.\n\n== FOR THE LEAD ==\nRow edit is yours. My reading: every claim in the Evidence column survived — no leaked obligation and no half-published artifact under any of the 18 attacks — but the Status rests on 'cancellation closes correctly', and A1 shows a cancelled task is still writable through task.resume. Suggested repair (not made here): move the terminal check above the budget application in fn resume, or refuse the budget arm for a terminal task as update_budget already does; A1 and A2 pin both directions. Worth a follow-up bone: continuations of terminal tasks are never pruned, which is what makes the dead task reachable at all."}	blake3:HConAf5-PlT2dzD_XvBuUCW_pG2G_iYwVbR8nxNZT7k
1785700114528404	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy2wg		item.create	bn-10093	{"description":"# Context\nbn-3p32's DX-14 falsification landed attack A1: in crates/continuumd/src/daemon/task.rs fn resume, the budget-application block sits ABOVE the is_terminal() early return, while task.update_budget refuses terminal tasks with a stated reason ('a terminal task's budget is a historical fact, and rewriting it would make its recorded cost unreadable'). The wire sequence verification.start(parks) → task.cancel → task.resume(continuation, budget) rewrites a Cancelled task's ceilings; below recorded spend, the B18 arm fires and appends task.budget_suspended + a Milestone TaskEvent to a cancelled record, which then reports cost above its declared budget. The pass condition's two named conjuncts held (no obligation leak — resume returns before advance, opens no region, publishes nothing); what broke is record closure/finality.\n\nPinned as FALSIFICATION in crates/continuumd/tests/dx14_falsification.rs (A1); A2 (same sequence, no budget → byte-identical record) is the localising control that also catches a repair moved too far.\n\n# The fix\nRefuse the budget arm for a terminal task in fn resume, matching update_budget's own rule and reason (or equivalently move the terminal check above the budget application — but the refusal must produce the same observable update_budget produces for terminal tasks, so the two operations cannot disagree). No IDL/schema change expected; behavior moves TOWARD the documented rule (task.status_monotonic + update_budget's stated refusal), so no F-flag anticipated — audit and confirm.\n\n# Also owed\n- Flip the A1 pin from FALSIFICATION into a regression guard asserting the correct behavior (record byte-identical, matching A2).\n- Record the falsification-and-fix cycle on the G0-DX-14 matrix row DX-13-style (evidence column names the campaign, the defect, the fix bone; Notes updated) + regen/validate.\n- DISPOSITION (record, do not implement): continuations of terminal tasks are never pruned — kept-with-refusal is likely correct (RFC 0026 handle-validity within a protocol major; resume validation refuses terminal), but state it in code where the continuation table lives.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] cancel → resume(cont, budget) leaves the cancelled record byte-identical (A1 flipped, A2 still green).\n- [ ] update_budget and resume produce the same observable for a terminal task's budget arm.\n- [ ] Matrix row records the cycle; validator green; full gate green.","kind":"bug","labels":["kernel","phase-a","req:phase-a","req:pr-6","req:g0-dx-14"],"parent":"bn-19u","size":"xs","title":"task.resume applies budget before terminal check — cancelled record rewritable (bn-3p32 A1 fix)","urgency":"urgent"}	blake3:mvCDR11dixw7Zo6R2dAkGZwhqti69yQH-s5vzjE2bQ0
1785700114528533	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy3Ag		item.link	bn-1n6r	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-10093"}	blake3:Oyis4y2BjfnVyf-gk5D-ohjNfAPWnhp_Sk4-S4iTLHo
1785700114728692	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy3Qg		item.create	bn-y9f7i	{"description":"# Context\nbn-3p32's A12 (pinned as a concern, not a violation): a cancelled campaign's content identity is permanently un-restartable — verification.start's cached lane fires only for Completed, so an identical re-start returns the cancelled task on the task_started lane and runs nothing. task.status is authoritative and honest; the envelope lane is not.\n\n# Decision needed\nEither the answer lane distinguishes returning-an-existing-terminal-task from starting-work (wire-visible — needs the F-flag discipline and RFC 0026 text), or the current behavior is ratified with the lane documented as 'the task this identity resolves to' rather than 'work started'. Wire-visible change requires the flag discipline; do not change silently.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Disposition recorded (RFC text or ratified doc), A12 pin updated to cite it.","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","req:phase-a","req:pr-6","spec-debt"],"size":"xs","title":"verification.start on a cancelled campaign identity answers task_started and runs nothing — envelope lane honesty (bn-3p32 A12)"}	blake3:Ro3nHxv4qkmcNejm4UsvENVJNyKrD2RbDULqaeQjiu4
1785700150812315	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy3gg		item.comment	bn-3p32	{"body":"Landed @ 809f081260e8. Lead re-verified: two test files only (no src, no dossier — diff confirmed), all 31 suite results green, based on current trunk.\n\nFALSIFIED-WITH-PIN ACCEPTED — the campaign did exactly what the bn-21dd precedent demands. A1 is a genuine closure defect: task.resume applies the request budget above the terminal check while update_budget refuses terminal tasks with a stated reason, so cancel → resume(cont, budget) rewrites a Cancelled record and can fire B18 on a dead task. The precise scoping is ratified as stated: both NAMED conjuncts of the pass condition held under all 18 attacks (is_total after every beat incl. all 120 A11 orderings; the resume opens no region and publishes nothing) — what broke is the closure the row's question presupposes. A2 accepted as the localising control that also bounds the repair.\n\nROW DECISION (lead): Status stays Evidence (reference implementation) on the strength of the named conjuncts; the Notes column now carries the full adversarial-retest disclosure including the A1 falsification and fix routing — the DX-13 pattern, with the cycle record owed by the fix bone. FIX ROUTED: bn-10093 (urgent, parent bn-19u, blocks bn-1n6r — the exit evidence must assert post-fix behavior; flips A1 into a regression guard; records the cycle on the row; carries the pruning disposition: continuations of terminal tasks kept-with-refusal per RFC 0026 handle validity, stated in code). A12's envelope-lane honesty concern routed to bn-y9f7i (wire-visible options need the flag discipline; no silent change).\n\nRATIFIED FURTHER: the what-bn-2zy-took-for-granted list (never operates after cancel except cancel, never budgets a dead resume, rendering-compare vs wire frames, debug_assert compiled out in release, legal-steps-only at calculus grain) is the right statement of why falsification is a separate discipline from construction — worth citing in bn-1n6r's exit narrative. The 216-program calculus composition attack with its withheld-mirror mutation control (C3) is the strongest single piece: it proves the attack harness itself is load-bearing.\n\nG0-DX-14 coverage carried forward on bn-10093 (req:g0-dx-14)."}	blake3:ZcWapVPzyJw3gw7xnvayrWnM204Fxli7GxbIs0udKV4
1785700150842103	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy3wg		item.move	bn-3p32	{"state":"done"}	blake3:Jn08umEL3xpMfPAJovSMRqr01RK30VMHnBMd-o3QSvw
1785700347995219	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy4Ag		item.comment	bn-1kp6	{"body":"G0-DX-03 falsification campaign (1/2) — **FALSIFIED-WITH-PIN**. Row NOT upgraded.\n\n## Result\n27 tests in `crates/continuumd/tests/dx03_falsification.rs`: the 5 R3 spike §3 baseline\nscenarios re-run at the production grain + 22 adversarial attacks over four axes.\n**17 attacks held; 5 landed, on 3 independent daemon-layer defects.** Two of the pass\ncondition's three conjuncts (idempotence, stale-snapshot rejection) are falsified;\ndeterministic resume held on every axis attacked. `src/` frozen throughout (bn-21dd\nprecedent). All five reproductions run un-ignored and **green** — they assert what the\ndaemon does today, with FALSIFICATION-labelled doc comments naming what should hold and\nthe assertion that flips when fixed.\n\n## The three defects (lead to route)\n\n**D1 — `workspace.create` rewinds a lineage (3 tests).** `daemon/workspace.rs::create`\nderives the lineage `ForkName` from the *content-addressed* `ws_*` handle of the snapshot it\ncreates, then calls `DaemonState::put_lineage`, an unconditional `BTreeMap::insert`. A second\ncreate naming identical components ⇒ identical handle ⇒ identical fork name ⇒ the live `Fork`\nis replaced by a fresh `Fork::diverge` rooted at the original snapshot. Every advance is\nforgotten. The DX-03 experiment's own sentence, run to its end:\n- `verification.start` over the superseded snapshot — refused `StaleSnapshot` one request\n  earlier — runs a full 16-state campaign to completion, and so does `task.resume` of the\n  continuation pinned to it\n  (`falsification_a_replayed_create_rewinds_the_lineage_and_un_stales_the_old_snapshot`);\n- the snapshot that *was* the head becomes unsealable and unforkable, refused\n  `CapabilityDenied` (the `LineageError::Unknown` arm) — indistinguishable from an artifact\n  that never existed, so the real head is stranded with no recovery\n  (`falsification_a_rewound_lineage_strands_the_snapshot_that_was_current`);\n- a `seal: false` re-create also overwrites the `WorkspaceRecord`, flipping `sealed` back to\n  false and revoking a live continuation with `StaleSnapshot`\n  (`falsification_an_unsealed_re_create_revokes_a_valid_continuation`).\nThe idempotency ledger is the ONLY guard: the same create under the key it already used\nreturns at dispatch step 7 and never reaches step 8, so nothing is rewound\n(`attack_staleness_the_same_create_under_the_same_key_changes_nothing`, the anti-vacuity\ncontrol). A fresh key — which `rule idempotency.replay` contemplates via the retention window\n— or a second actor (keys are per-actor) defeats it.\n\n**D2 — `task.resume` writes a terminal task's budget (1 test).** `daemon/task.rs::resume`\napplies the request's optional budget via `budget::update` BEFORE the `is_terminal` check;\n`update_budget` checks first. The same ceiling on the same cancelled task is refused through\none operation and written through the other, so `resume`'s doc (\"a no-op does neither\") is\nfalse: `TaskRecord.budget` afterwards reports `states: 4096` for a task that ran under 4.\n(`falsification_resume_rewrites_a_terminal_tasks_budget_update_budget_refuses`)\n\n**D3 — `ReplayKey` omits `RequestEnvelope.budget` (1 test).** `daemon/state.rs::ReplayKey::of`\ncarries operation + envelope snapshot + envelope intent + arguments. `budget` is an envelope\nfield, part of the canonical request `rule idempotency.replay` compares — and load-bearing:\n`verification::start_handle` writes `budget_preimage` into the `task_*` identity, so two\nbudgets are two tasks. A `verification.start` replayed under one key with a larger budget is\nneither `IdempotencyKeyReused` nor honoured: it gets the first budget's task verbatim. The\ncontrol (same two requests, two keys) shows they really are two campaigns.\n(`falsification_the_replay_key_omits_the_envelope_budget`). The same omission covers\n`output_policy` and `page`; `budget` is the reachable, semantically load-bearing one.\n\n## Baseline coverage (spike §3 scenario → production test)\ncanonical snapshot identities → `baseline_snapshot_and_task_identities_are_canonical_across_two_fresh_daemons` ·\nidempotent identical task creation → `baseline_an_identical_keyed_start_is_byte_identical_and_mints_one_task` ·\nkey-reuse rejection → `baseline_a_key_reused_for_a_different_request_is_refused` (arguments AND\nenvelope-snapshot dimensions; the budget dimension is D3) · resumable continuation →\n`baseline_a_parked_continuation_resumes_to_the_frozen_die_hard_verdict` (16 states, closed) ·\nrejection of continuation under a different snapshot →\n`baseline_a_resume_naming_a_different_snapshot_is_stale`.\n\n## Files / commits / gates\n- `crates/continuumd/tests/dx03_falsification.rs` (new, 2602 lines, 27 tests). No\n  continuum-workspace test added: every attack that landed is daemon-grain; the\n  snapshot/lineage layer refused every stale handle it was shown.\n- `notes/plan/notes/G0_SPIKE_MATRIX.md` DX-03: Status kept `Evidence (finite spike)` with\n  \"**pass condition falsified at the production grain**\" appended; Evidence names the campaign\n  file, the retained R3 §3 baseline lineage, the attacks and counts; Decision records the three\n  defects, the frozen-`src/` discipline, scope limits, and the concerns. `plan.md` §0.3\n  deliberately NOT touched (DX-03 stays in the carries-evidence set; the validator's derived\n  counts are unchanged). DX-14 row untouched (bn-3p32's).\n- Commits: `5d1ac51` (campaign), `ecde570` (matrix row + regen).\n- Gates in-workspace: fmt clean; clippy `--all-targets --locked -D warnings` clean;\n  `cargo test -p continuumd -p continuum-workspace --locked` 32/32 suites ok with every\n  pre-existing test unchanged; `generate_traceability.py` + `validate_dossier.py` exit 0\n  (`\"status\": \"pass\"`); `just check` exit 0.\n\n## OOM hygiene\nLargest sweep 6 × 5 = 30 whole-campaign runs + 1 control over a 16-state model (~0.07s for the\nwhole file). No threads, no textual doubling, no allocation from a loop bound. Well inside the\nexisting 210×8 / 5,040-run ceilings.\n\nBone left open for the lead to route the three fixes. Attack catalogue and concerns in 2/2."}	blake3:-jROkfC3dj3fHz18rrORaIZhx0GtkfKpUMxdZHIYy-Q
1785700378468826	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy4Qg		item.comment	bn-1kp6	{"body":"G0-DX-03 falsification campaign (2/2) — attack catalogue and recorded concerns.\n\n## Attack catalogue (22 attacks, per-attack outcome)\n\n**Axis 1 — idempotence**\n1. keyed replay after six interleaved hostile operations (a third-actor read, an admission\n   denial, an envelope/body shape disagreement, a refused key reuse, an unshipped lane, a\n   subscription read) — byte-identical. HELD\n2. keyed replay on a *reconnected* daemon (INV-002's `DaemonState`-only transplant into a\n   freshly negotiated daemon) — byte-identical. The ledger is state, not session. HELD\n3. keyed replay *across* a supersession — the ledger neither re-runs the campaign against the\n   new tree nor re-admits the stale snapshot; no second task, no second publication. The\n   anti-vacuity control is the identical request under a fresh key, refused `StaleSnapshot`. HELD\n4. two fresh keys on one campaign (the IDL's declared cached-result lane) — the two cached\n   answers agree byte for byte, name one task identity, publish nothing new. HELD\n5. same key, different envelope `budget` — **FALSIFIED** (D3).\n\n**Axis 2 — stale snapshot rejection**\n6. every snapshot-consuming operation against a superseded handle: `verification.start`,\n   `task.resume`, `workspace.fork`, `workspace.seal` → `StaleSnapshot`; `workspace.diff` →\n   `UnsupportedSemanticFeature` (refuses rather than degrading over stale inputs). No partial\n   effects: the parked task is untouched at `Suspended`, cost 3. HELD\n7. a snapshot superseded so far the `Fork` can no longer place it — `check_current`'s honest\n   `Unknown` arm, reached by sealing and campaigning on the lineage's *second* point and then\n   advancing three more times. Every path still refuses. HELD (records concern 1)\n8. replay `workspace.create` under a fresh key, then reuse the old handle — **FALSIFIED** (D1)\n9. the same rewind seen from the snapshot that *was* current — **FALSIFIED** (D1)\n10. the same rewind with `seal: false`, revoking a live continuation — **FALSIFIED** (D1,\n    fails closed)\n11. anti-vacuity for 8–10: the same create under the *same* key changes nothing and the stale\n    handle stays refused. HELD\n\n**Axis 3 — deterministic resume**\n12. two independently built daemons driven through the identical DX-03 script — byte-identical\n    answers, frame for frame, four answers each. HELD\n13. a hostile suite spliced at every position of the resume script: 6 operations × 5 placements\n    = 30 runs plus the un-interleaved control. The resume answer *and* the verdict that follows\n    it are byte-identical at every placement; a fired-counter assertion makes each run\n    non-vacuous. HELD\n14. resume under every arm of `rule task.update_budget`'s legality table — raised, unchanged,\n    tightened-above-spend, lowered *below* committed spend (B18), and withdrawn entirely. Cost\n    is monotone 3 → … → 16 and no arm un-explores a campaign (INV-009). HELD\n\n**Axis 4 — handle reuse**\n15. double-resume one continuation: one task, one campaign value, identical cost and status,\n    the same frontier naming the same `cont_*`. What *does* grow is the committed-publication\n    list, one per run, each with a distinct content identity — INV-009's \"may add evidence …\n    may not silently replace prior artifacts\" and RFC 0026's \"re-derived artifacts receive new\n    identities\". Recorded rather than asserted away: a no-progress resume still publishes. HELD\n16. resume a continuation a *newer* continuation has superseded — admitted, and it advances\n    the same task to the same place, no second campaign. HELD (records concern 2)\n17. a completed task handle on every observing operation (`task.status`, `task.subscribe`,\n    `verification.result`), twice each — byte-identical, publications unchanged. HELD\n18. a continuation reused after cancellation, carrying a budget — **FALSIFIED** (D2)\n19. the same reuse *without* a budget — a genuine no-op: two resumes agree byte for byte, the\n    record is unchanged, nothing is published. This is what isolates D2 to the budget write\n    rather than the terminal resume path as a whole. HELD\n20. cancelling twice — the second answers `Unchanged` rather than `Cancelled` (honestly\n    different bytes) and leaves the task's record byte-identical. HELD\n21. an unheld `cont_*` (a real handle with its last hex digit rotated) refused byte-identically\n    by a daemon running the campaign and by a look-alike that never ran anything; and the real\n    handle against the look-alike is the same denial. RFC 0027 X2, no existence oracle. HELD\n22. resume after the daemon's epochs advanced under it: a *changed* semantic epoch is\n    `ContinuationEpochMismatch` (P1), an *absent* one is `EpochUnsupported`, neither is confused\n    with staleness, and the anti-vacuity control is the same request on an unmoved daemon,\n    which is served. HELD\n\n## Concerns (recorded, not called defects)\n1. `LineageError::Unknown` maps to `CapabilityDenied` in `daemon/workspace.rs` (argued at length\n   from RFC 0027 X2 — \"inventing a distinguishable not-found is precisely the existence oracle\")\n   but to `StaleSnapshot` in `daemon/task.rs::resume` and `daemon/verification.rs::start`. Every\n   path refuses, so DX-03's pass condition is untouched; but one verdict answering to two codes\n   is how two readings come to disagree later. Pinned by attack 7.\n2. `Continuation::bounds` and `Continuation::frontier` are pinned at creation — RFC 0026\n   requires it and `verification::run_in` does it — and are read by **nothing** on the resume\n   path: `task.resume` re-derives the bound from `TaskEntry::bounds()` and `bfs::explore`\n   restarts from the beginning. So at this grain a `cont_*` handle means \"advance this task\",\n   not \"resume from this point\", which is *why* an older continuation cannot be refused as a\n   second meaning (attack 16). It is sound today only because breadth-first exploration makes\n   the parked explored set a prefix of the resumed one; a real partial-state engine would make\n   it a defect.\n3. `daemon/task.rs::resume`'s documented decision table gives `StaleSnapshot` for \"the pinned\n   snapshot is not held\"; the code returns `Fault::denied()` there. Unreachable through wire\n   operations today — nothing removes a `WorkspaceRecord` — so it is untested and noted only.\n\n## Scope limits stated plainly\nOne thread, one request at a time (`Daemon::dispatch` takes `&mut self`): this campaign makes\nno concurrency claim, which is G0-DX-13's axis. No disk, no crash injection, no restart across\na process boundary — durability is PR 6+ per docs/35. The one engine is the reference engine\nover Die Hard; three of the four verification lanes do not exist yet."}	blake3:h33NB8Rj2ELn9hAjJoAVYW84bbXqmCl6SOTMjIfps3o
1785700526966846	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy4gg		item.create	bn-n1xou	{"description":"# Context\nbn-1kp6 D1 (three pinned tests in crates/continuumd/tests/dx03_falsification.rs): daemon/workspace.rs::create names the lineage ForkName from the content-addressed ws_* handle it derives, then calls DaemonState::put_lineage — an unconditional BTreeMap::insert. A second create with identical components (fresh idempotency key, or second actor) produces the identical handle and fork name and REPLACES the live Fork with a fresh Fork::diverge at the origin snapshot. Consequences pinned: (a) a superseded snapshot — refused StaleSnapshot one request earlier — is served again by verification.start AND task.resume; (b) the snapshot that was the head is stranded as CapabilityDenied with no recovery; (c) a seal:false re-create revokes a live continuation. The idempotency ledger is the only guard and rule idempotency.replay's retention window defeats it (anti-vacuity control pinned).\n\n# Fix design (agent decides against the dossier, with these constraints)\nThe DX-13 precedent is the compass: concurrent identical creation converges on ONE semantic identity with no lost state. A create whose fork name already exists must not rewind — likely put_lineage-if-absent with the existing lineage untouched and the (content-addressed, already-existing) snapshot returned; the seal divergence needs explicit treatment. Whatever the design: the three D1 pins flip to regression guards asserting no-rewind/no-stranding/no-revocation, the localising controls stay green, and any wire-visible change is audited (fields already declared + governing text = no flag; otherwise F-flag).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Identical re-create (fresh key or not) never rewinds a lineage, strands a head, or revokes a continuation; three pins flipped.\n- [ ] Stale-snapshot refusal remains for genuinely superseded handles (campaign's held attacks stay green).\n- [ ] Wire-visibility audited and stated.","kind":"bug","labels":["kernel","phase-a","req:phase-a","req:pr-6","req:g0-dx-03"],"parent":"bn-19u","size":"s","title":"workspace.create rewinds a live lineage — unconditional put_lineage on content-addressed fork name (bn-1kp6 D1)","urgency":"urgent"}	blake3:0rTWP6-xml6QBQ2uZ27u2tyuJwwftjGy3CB1PtqiZIY
1785700526967036	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy4wg		item.link	bn-1n6r	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-n1xou"}	blake3:jQ_Mr0pVmwYAgXUA96RiQQSjHhVRtWkOXPLUlc6raOE
1785700527176869	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5Ag		item.create	bn-h1zqz	{"description":"# Context\nbn-1kp6 D3 (one pinned test in dx03_falsification.rs): daemon::state::ReplayKey omits RequestEnvelope.budget — which is part of the canonical request rule idempotency.replay compares AND is in the task_* preimage, so two budgets are two tasks. A verification.start replayed under one key with a larger budget is neither IdempotencyKeyReused nor honoured — it silently returns the first budget's task verbatim. Same omission covers output_policy and page.\n\n# Fix\nReplayKey covers the full canonical request per rule idempotency.replay (budget, output_policy, page — audit the envelope for any other omitted field). Same key + different canonical request = IdempotencyKeyReused, per the rule's own text. Flip the D3 pin; keyed-replay attacks that held must stay green.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Same key, different budget/output_policy/page → IdempotencyKeyReused; pin flipped.\n- [ ] True replays (identical canonical request) still return the original answer byte-identically.","kind":"bug","labels":["kernel","phase-a","req:phase-a","req:pr-6"],"parent":"bn-19u","size":"xs","title":"ReplayKey omits RequestEnvelope.budget/output_policy/page — one key answers two canonical requests (bn-1kp6 D3)","urgency":"urgent"}	blake3:-6HeJ5u1epf00etEU9UfduMx8kH9StubLZXmWpyZYxM
1785700527177010	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5Qg		item.link	bn-1n6r	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-h1zqz"}	blake3:3I2nFN-ow1SRVf46mI_RPhTtObLIipsaHdh1nWtUvfM
1785700527361496	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5gg		item.create	bn-10wdo	{"description":"# Context — three recorded concerns from bn-1kp6's DX-03 campaign, none a defect\n1. LineageError::Unknown maps to CapabilityDenied in the workspace family but StaleSnapshot in task/verification — one verdict, two spellings. Needs a single documented rule (or a documented reason for the asymmetry).\n2. Continuation::bounds/frontier are pinned at creation and read by NOTHING on the resume path — a cont_* means 'advance this task', not 'resume from this point'. Sound only because BFS makes the parked set a prefix. Needs an RFC 0026 disposition stating which meaning is normative (a future non-prefix engine breaks the current one silently).\n3. task.resume's documented decision table says 'pinned snapshot not held' → StaleSnapshot; the code returns Fault::denied(). Unreachable via wire ops today, but doc and code disagree.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each concern gets a disposition (RFC text, doc fix, or code alignment) with the campaign's tests updated to cite it.","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","req:phase-a","spec-debt"],"size":"xs","title":"Refusal-surface consistency + cont_* semantics disposition (bn-1kp6 concerns 1-3)"}	blake3:eweKwetbmxcgz1Nl5FvUUzYyATJ1512G40Caf_GccQI
1785700543081170	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5wg		item.comment	bn-1kp6	{"body":"Landed @ ac8085e3f0e5. Lead re-verified post-sync in-workspace: all 32 suites green with no pre-existing test changed, validator pass, regen idempotent; frozen-src discipline confirmed by diff (two test-file commits + matrix row only).\n\nFALSIFIED-WITH-PIN ACCEPTED — the strongest campaign result of the program so far: two of three conjuncts broken against the production daemon, on three independent defects, with the workspace snapshot/lineage layer refusing every stale handle it was shown (the defects are all in the daemon's use of it, which is exactly the layering the architecture claims). The row decision is ratified as the agent made it: Status stays Evidence (finite spike) with the falsification stated in-line, so the derived counts and §0.3 are untouched and DX-03 correctly remains freeze-blocking.\n\nFIXES ROUTED, all urgent, all parent bn-19u, all blocking bn-1n6r: D1 → bn-n1xou (lineage rewind — the serious one: StaleSnapshot bypass, stranded head, revoked continuation; DX-13's one-semantic-identity precedent is the fix's compass; carries req:g0-dx-03). D2 → bn-10093 (already routed from bn-3p32's A1 — same defect found independently by both campaigns, which is itself corroborating evidence; this campaign's D2 pin joins A1/A2 as the contract). D3 → bn-h1zqz (ReplayKey omits budget/output_policy/page). Concerns 1–3 → bn-10wdo (refusal-spelling consistency; cont_*-means-advance-not-resume-from needs an RFC 0026 disposition — concern 2 is design-significant and must not be lost). ROW UPGRADE OWED: when all three fixes land and the pins flip, the DX-03 row upgrades to Evidence (reference implementation) with the falsification-and-fix cycle recorded DX-13-style — assigned to bn-10093's wave as the last step.\n\nRATIFIED FURTHER: baseline discipline (all five R3 §3 scenarios re-run at production grain as baseline_* tests before any attack counts); the anti-vacuity controls (fresh-key control isolating the idempotency ledger as the only guard; fired-counter on the interleaving sweep); deterministic resume holding on every axis attacked — frame-for-frame across fresh daemons — is the row's one conjunct that survived and is worth naming in bn-1n6r's exit narrative.\n\nG0-DX-03 coverage carried forward on bn-n1xou (req:g0-dx-03)."}	blake3:X20FY1Ag0Rfx_GHfGQuOQF45ukn6lkHACPextwMFj14
