1785701821822615	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy7wg		item.comment	bn-n1xou	{"body":"FIX LANDED (in-workspace .maw/workspaces/bn-10093, commit abeedda — not merged, not pushed).\n\nThe fix. `daemon::workspace::create` no longer rewinds anything: an identical create CONVERGES. `DaemonState::open_lineage` is a new put-if-absent door (BTreeMap entry/or_insert) and is create's only access to the lineage map, so a lineage this daemon already holds keeps every advance it made; the held `WorkspaceRecord` is not replaced; and `sealed` is monotone. `put_lineage` stays exactly as it was and is now documented as what it is — `workspace.fork`'s ADVANCE, whose guard is the `advance_current` compare-and-set upstream that produced its argument. The design follows the DX-13 precedent named in the bone (identical creation converges on one semantic identity, nothing lost) and, more directly, the daemon's own convergence rule in `DaemonState::append_evidence`: \"a convergence, not an overwrite and not an error\", the stored value untouched \"even when the two appends disagree about everything else\".\n\nThe seal divergence, treated explicitly (a four-row table in `daemon::workspace`'s module docs, plus the comment at the write): unsealed + seal:true SEALS (exactly `workspace.seal`'s effect through the operation that asked for it — the one direction that loses nothing); sealed + seal:true stays sealed; SEALED + seal:false STAYS SEALED and the response reports `sealed: true` — seal:false means \"do not seal it\", never \"un-seal it\": there is no un-seal operation in this protocol, and a seal is what a continuation's staleness predicate reads, so letting a create revoke one would let any caller revoke any continuation by re-creating components it can already name; unsealed + seal:false is unchanged. Also recorded: the held record's descriptor, intent and lineage are untouched, so two creates whose files agree but whose `components.intent` differs converge on the FIRST intent — a re-create cannot re-govern a snapshot that exists (that is `append_evidence`'s \"disagree about everything else\" clause, stated where a reader will hit it).\n\nWIRE-VISIBILITY AUDIT. Byte-identical for every existing flow except one. A first create is unchanged in every field. A converging create returns the same `snapshot`, the same `sealed`, and the same `Created` structural verdict the original create returned — the same convergence-still-reports-Created shape `observe.ingest` already uses for a re-ingest. The ONE changed response is sealed + seal:false: it now reports `sealed: true` where the old code reported `sealed: false` and made that true by making the record false. `WorkspaceCreateResponse.sealed` is a `required` field already declared as whether the snapshot is sealed, and the governing text is `rule task.resume`'s staleness predicate plus the absence of any un-seal operation, so this is conformance to what the field already says rather than a new wire fact => NO F-FLAG raised (bn-23j7s precedent: declared field + governing text = conformance). No IDL, schema, registry or error-union change. Publication behaviour is untouched: seal:true still publishes through the same `publish` call, so the store's receipts are exactly what they were.\n\nPINS FLIPPED — all three, over the IDENTICAL wire sequences, in crates/continuumd/tests/dx03_falsification.rs:\n- attack 8 -> regression_a_replayed_create_converges_and_leaves_the_old_snapshot_stale: after the fresh-key re-create, `verification.start` over the superseded handle and `task.resume` of the continuation pinned to it are BOTH still `StaleSnapshot`, the parked record is still Suspended at cost 3, and a new anti-vacuity control shows the current head still seals and still serves a campaign (so this is a claim about the lineage, not about a daemon that forgot how to run).\n- attack 9 -> regression_a_converged_create_does_not_strand_the_snapshot_that_is_current: the head seals (Ok) and forks (Ok) after the re-create, and its seal answer is BYTE-IDENTICAL with a control daemon that never saw a re-create.\n- attack 10 -> regression_an_unsealed_re_create_does_not_revoke_a_valid_continuation: the seal:false re-create reports `sealed: true`, and the live continuation resumes to Die Hard's frozen verdict (Completed, 16 states).\n\nEvery stale-handle refusal that HELD still refuses: attack 6 (all five snapshot-consuming operations), attack 7 (the `LineageError::Unknown` arm after four advances), attack 11 (same create under the same key changes nothing), and the two baselines about staleness. 22/22 attacks + 5 baselines green.\n\nTESTS: dx03_falsification 27/27, dx14_falsification 13/13, continuumd 314, continuum-task 146, continuum-workspace 256 — all green. cargo fmt --all --check clean; cargo clippy --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings clean; `just check` exit 0 (dossier validator included, exit 0).\n\nONE THING I COULD NOT FIX INSIDE THE FENCE, for the lead. `attack_staleness_the_same_create_under_the_same_key_changes_nothing` (attack 11) is not a pin, so under \"every pre-existing test not a pin stays unchanged\" I left it byte-for-byte alone. Its ASSERTIONS still hold and are still meaningful, but its doc comment now says something false about the fixed daemon: \"swapping only the idempotency key — nothing else about the request — flips the outcome from 'refused, as required' to 'admitted'\". Post-fix the swap flips nothing. The file's module doc and the three flipped tests state the corrected reading; attack 11's own prose is the one place left saying the old thing, and correcting it is a one-line doc edit somebody with authority over that test should make."}	blake3:S_O_OlOtXk5GM6iGsX0LQSn1iSaYbdQ7tAI8b4kgqCU
1785701842912806	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy8Ag		item.comment	bn-h1zqz	{"body":"FIX LANDED (in-workspace .maw/workspaces/bn-10093, commit 84f29b3 — not merged, not pushed).\n\nThe fix. `daemon::state::ReplayKey` now carries the whole canonical request: `budget`, `output_policy` and `page` beside the `operation`, `snapshot`, `intent` and decoded `arguments` it already had. `ReplayKey::of` reads all seven from the envelope. Same key + different canonical request is now `IdempotencyKeyReused` at step 7 of the dispatch, per the rule's own second sentence, with no partial effect.\n\nENVELOPE AUDIT (asked for by the bone: \"audit the envelope for any other omitted field and say what you found\"). `RequestEnvelope` has thirteen fields. Seven are in the key. The other six are out, each for a stated reason, and the table lives in `ReplayKey`'s own documentation so the next reader does not have to re-derive it:\n- `request_id` — the per-ATTEMPT identity. A retry carries a fresh one by construction, so including it would make every genuine replay a conflict; letting it differ is precisely what the rule exists for.\n- `trace` — W3C propagation metadata, per-attempt like `request_id`: a retry carries a new `traceparent`, and a key that conflicted on it would be unusable by any traced client.\n- `idempotency_key` — what the ledger FILES the record under, not part of what is compared under it.\n- `actor` — already the other half of the ledger's map key (\"keys are scoped per actor\"), so it cannot differ between a record and a hit.\n- `capability` — RFC 0027 S5 keeps a `cap_*` out of every derived value, and RFC 0027 T4 binds the capability to the actor that is already the scope; two delegations of one actor's authority are one caller retrying, not two requests.\n- `protocol_version` — a request naming other than the negotiated version is refused at step 2 of the dispatch, so every request that reaches the ledger carries the same value and including it could only add a constant.\nSo: nothing else omitted. The three the bone named were the three that were missing.\n\nWIRE-VISIBILITY AUDIT. Byte-identical for every TRUE replay: an identical canonical request under a used key still returns the recorded outcome verbatim, and the flipped test asserts that beside the refusal rather than assuming it (a repair of the rule's second sentence that broke its first would be a worse defect). What changes is a DIFFERENT canonical request under a used key: it is now refused with `IdempotencyKeyReused` instead of being silently answered with the other request's recorded outcome. That code is already declared on every `@mutation`'s error union and is `rule idempotency.replay`'s own text, so the change is conformance toward a stated rule => NO F-FLAG (bn-23j7s precedent). No IDL, schema or registry change; `ReplayKey` is daemon-internal state and its shape is not on the wire.\n\nPIN FLIPPED: attack 5 in crates/continuumd/tests/dx03_falsification.rs, `falsification_the_replay_key_omits_the_envelope_budget` -> `regression_the_replay_key_covers_the_envelope_budget`. Same sequence: park a 4-state campaign under `idem-shared`, then send the 64-state request under the same key. Now asserts `IdempotencyKeyReused`, `Payload::None` (no partial effect), one task in the table (the refused request started nothing and the recorded campaign is untouched), and then that the identical canonical request under the same key is STILL answered from the ledger byte-for-byte. The two-fresh-keys control — proving the two requests really are two campaigns with two `task_*` identities, since `budget_preimage` is in the preimage — is unchanged.\n\nEvery keyed-replay attack that HELD stays green and true: attack 1 (replay after six interleaved hostile operations), attack 2 (replay on a reconnected daemon, INV-002's transplant device), attack 3 (replay across a supersession, with its fresh-key anti-vacuity control), attack 4 (two fresh keys agree byte-for-byte), attack 11 (same create under the same key), and the two idempotence baselines — including `baseline_a_key_reused_for_a_different_request_is_refused`, whose comment about `budget` being the third dimension that is NOT refused is now covered by attack 5 itself. 22/22 attacks + 5 baselines green.\n\nTESTS: dx03_falsification 27/27, dx14_falsification 13/13, continuumd 314, continuum-task 146, continuum-workspace 256 — all green. fmt clean; clippy -D warnings clean; `just check` exit 0."}	blake3:13ZTkyzmmfuAGAzoq2Rv0fl1LuEpjiqgrtTSCfzRObE
1785701872662128	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy8Qg		item.comment	bn-10093	{"body":"FIX LANDED (in-workspace .maw/workspaces/bn-10093, commit e29f4f0 — not merged, not pushed). This comment also carries the row updates and the overall counts for the three-defect wave.\n\nTHE FIX (bn-10093 / D2). In `daemon::task::resume` the terminal check now sits ABOVE the ledger write. The budget arm of a resume therefore produces `task.update_budget`'s OWN observable for a terminal task: the ledger is untouched, no `task.budget_suspended` milestone and no `TaskEvent` is appended, and the answer is the terminal status — which is exactly what a resume carrying NO budget always produced (A2). The two operations no longer disagree about one rule. Nothing else moved: the engine bound is still read AFTER the write, so a raised ceiling still extends THIS resume rather than the next one, and the non-terminal path is unchanged in every field. `update_budget`'s doc now states that `task.resume`'s optional budget is held to the same rule by the same reading, and `resume`'s decision table says why a terminal task is not a row in it.\n\nDISPOSITION RECORDED IN CODE, where the continuation table lives (`TaskTable` in `daemon::task`): continuations of terminal tasks are KEPT, not pruned, and the two reasons are written there. (1) Handle validity is a protocol-major property — RFC 0026 makes a `cont_*` a durable artifact identity, not a live scope, and pruning would turn a resume of a cancelled task's continuation into RFC 0027 X2's undistinguished denial, byte-identical with \"never existed\", losing the one honest answer (the task is terminal, here is its status). (2) The guard belongs at the write, not at the lookup: `resume` now refuses every write to a terminal task before touching the ledger, so holding the handle confers nothing a terminal task should not grant, and pruning would defend the property one layer away from the thing it protects while leaving the ledger write unguarded for any other door.\n\nWIRE-VISIBILITY AUDIT: BYTE-IDENTICAL for every flow that was not the defect. A resume of a non-terminal task is unchanged. A resume of a terminal task already answered `Ok` with the terminal status and still does — what changes is that the `task.status` record it leaves behind is now the one the cancel left, i.e. the rewrite `task.update_budget` already refused for a stated reason. No IDL/schema/registry change, no new field, no new code. Behaviour moves TOWARD `rule task.status_monotonic` and `update_budget`'s documented refusal => NO F-FLAG (bn-23j7s precedent).\n\nPINS FLIPPED (both, identical wire sequences):\n- bn-3p32 A1, crates/continuumd/tests/dx14_falsification.rs: `falsification_a_cancelled_task_is_still_writable_through_task_resume` -> `regression_a_cancelled_task_is_not_writable_through_task_resume`. Asserts the cancelled record is BYTE-IDENTICAL across `cancel` -> `resume(continuation, budget below spend)` — A2's shape — plus the individual facts (ceiling kept, milestone list unchanged, no `task.budget_suspended`, spend untouched, publications 1, no new continuation, `TaskRegions::is_total`, 2 regions opened) so a regression says WHICH one moved. A2 (`negative_a_resume_without_a_budget_leaves_a_cancelled_task_untouched`) is unchanged and green.\n- bn-1kp6 attack 18, crates/continuumd/tests/dx03_falsification.rs: -> `regression_resume_refuses_the_terminal_budget_write_update_budget_refuses`, which compares the record through the two doors in one sequence: `update_budget(4096)` on the cancelled task, then `resume(cont, 4096)`, and asserts the record is byte-identical across the second.\n\nROW UPDATES (chore commit 9d6e935):\n- G0-DX-03 Status `Evidence (finite spike) — pass condition falsified at the production grain` -> `Evidence (reference implementation)`. Evidence and Decision rewritten DX-13-style: the campaign (bn-1kp6, `src/` frozen per the bn-21dd precedent), the three defects by name, the three fix bones (bn-n1xou / bn-10093 / bn-h1zqz), the per-fix wire audit result, and that all five reproductions now run un-ignored as regression guards. Scope statement and the two recorded concerns (`LineageError::Unknown`'s two wire codes; `Continuation::bounds`/`frontier` unread on the resume path) carried forward unchanged — neither was a defect, neither is fixed here.\n- G0-DX-14 Notes disclosure: cycle completion appended — A1 fixed by bn-10093, the record byte-identical across the sequence, A1 now a regression guard in A2's shape with A2 unchanged beside it, bn-1kp6's attack 18 guarding the same fix from the DX-03 side, and the continuation-table disposition recorded in code.\n- plan.md §0.3 deliberately UNTOUCHED. Its G0 sentence names the SET of rows carrying evidence, which `check_g0_matrix_counts` holds equal to the matrix's `Status ^= \"Evidence\"` set; DX-03 was and remains in that set, so set equality is preserved. No §0.3 sentence names DX-03's evidence CLASS — the same sentence already covers DX-13 and DX-14, both reference-implementation rows — so the text does not demand a change. Nothing the validator derives was touched; `validation-results.json` not touched.\n- Regen: `python3 notes/plan/tools/generate_traceability.py` (PLAN_REQUIREMENTS.json picks up the new `matrix_status`; PLAN_BONE_TRACEABILITY.md unchanged). `validate_dossier.py` exit 0.\n\nCOUNTS AND GATES (final tree): dx03_falsification 27/27 (22 attacks + 5 baselines, five flipped), dx14_falsification 13/13 (A1 flipped), continuumd 314, continuum-task 146, continuum-workspace 256, all green; `cargo fmt --all --check` clean; `cargo clippy --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings` clean; `just check` exit 0 including the Lean gate. Each of the three fix commits was also verified green on both campaign files in isolation (clone + checkout at e29f4f0, 84f29b3, abeedda: 27/27 and 13/13 at every one), so the wave bisects.\n\nFour commits, none touching `.bones/`: e29f4f0 (D2), 84f29b3 (D3, bn-h1zqz), abeedda (D1, bn-n1xou), 9d6e935 (rows + regen). Not merged, not pushed. No bone closed."}	blake3:AEDjC70WNaT8FgQvCM50sHggsqYX4xETsD4S0_OuJ7o
1785701983356029	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy8gg		item.comment	bn-10093	{"body":"Landed @ fd53ea92d919 with bn-n1xou and bn-h1zqz (one workspace, bisectable per-fix commits — each verified green in isolation). Lead re-verified: all suites green across continuumd (314) / continuum-task (146) / continuum-workspace (256), fmt clean.\n\nRATIFIED (D2): terminal check above the ledger write producing update_budget's exact observable — the two operations can no longer disagree; raised-ceiling-still-extends-this-resume preserved (the read stays after the write); kept-with-refusal pruning disposition documented at TaskTable. NO-F-FLAG accepted: behavior moves toward task.status_monotonic + update_budget's stated refusal, byte-identical elsewhere. A1+D2 pins flipped to regression guards asserting A2's byte-identical shape. DX-14's falsification-and-fix cycle is COMPLETE and recorded on the row."}	blake3:YMVILkn7zWmdrAVHjqWi19gFpY0oZBpX7NTIu4sBP_U
1785701983385691	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy8wg		item.move	bn-10093	{"state":"done"}	blake3:RLQqpWvALKb8UmQAuLDt9c00ezsgAAVYBFnK8urL_x0
1785701983420748	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy9Ag		item.comment	bn-h1zqz	{"body":"Landed @ fd53ea92d919 (commit 84f29b3 within the bn-10093 fix wave). RATIFIED: ReplayKey now covers budget/output_policy/page; the six-fields-out audit with per-field reasons recorded AT THE TYPE (request_id/trace per-attempt, idempotency_key is the filing key, actor is the ledger's other map key, capability out of derived values per RFC 0027 S5+T4, protocol_version refused at step 2) is the right way to make an omission a decision rather than an accident. NO-F-FLAG accepted: true replays asserted byte-identical; different-canonical-request-under-used-key = IdempotencyKeyReused, the rule's own second sentence with a declared code. D3 pin flipped; all held keyed-replay attacks still green."}	blake3:K5uwDI1VPYOBBT9hMaEhzwy65-Sknr5V-VOha6-Q4T4
1785701983451928	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy9Qg		item.move	bn-h1zqz	{"state":"done"}	blake3:b243E0h1NyPzBe-wRY8qGYmr-IS7GItMJJWm1LvHqts
1785701983480590	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy9gg		item.comment	bn-n1xou	{"body":"Landed @ fd53ea92d919 (commit abeedda within the bn-10093 fix wave). RATIFIED: convergence via open_lineage put-if-absent as create's ONLY door to the lineage map, put_lineage retained as fork's advance with its guard named; the four-row seal table with sealed+seal:false reporting sealed:true (no un-seal operation exists and a seal is what a continuation's staleness predicate reads) — the one wire-visible change, declared field + governing text, NO-F-FLAG accepted. FIRST-INTENT-WINS RATIFIED WITH REASON: the intent is not in the content-addressed handle, and a re-create silently re-governing an existing snapshot would be an intent change without a revision — INV-001 decides this, not convenience; the refusing alternative (needs a code that does not exist) is recorded as the road not taken, revisitable only through the flag discipline. Three D1 pins flipped with the new anti-vacuity (current head still seals and serves; byte-identical against a control daemon that never saw a re-create). DX-03 row upgrade to Evidence (reference implementation) with the full cycle record verified in the merged matrix. G0-DX-03's falsification-and-fix cycle is COMPLETE — the second full cycle after DX-13."}	blake3:x0NHGjtfVJjlry-VtS3sYGGf7d7BF-O4fy8xR6pISA4
