1785705389238141	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEygQk		item.comment	bn-3dr	{"body":"[1/2 design] Landed in .maw/workspaces/bn-3dr (commit 73453ae). Not merged, not pushed.\n\nDURABLE/VOLATILE SPLIT (designed first; stated at the type in crates/continuumd/src/daemon/recovery.rs).\nDurable: the continuum-workspace ReferenceStore — committed content, the index, the receipt ledger, the abort log, the store's own capability registry. Its crash safety is DX-13's, cited not re-proved (content before index; the in-flight pin from content commit to index commit; receipts appended in the index entry's critical section).\nVolatile: every field of DaemonState — wire capability registry, staged content, model catalog, workspace records, lineage map, intent registry, evidence graph, evidence event log, idempotency ledger, admission log, task table, continuation table, region tree. Thirteen, enumerated as `VolatileFact` with a `Disposition` each: three `reprovisioned` (the IDL §7 out-of-band surfaces a deployment restores at startup) and ten `declared` — named as lost, never reconstructed.\nNO disk-backed daemon-state store was built. Reason recorded at the type: plan §4.5's restart contract is about the store plus \"resume from the last committed continuation or transition to Failed with a typed reason\", and a task table that never reached a disk has no committed continuation to resume from; inferring one from the store is precisely the \"silently reconstructed state\" the same paragraph prohibits. Persisting DaemonState has its own crate-boundary question (plan §20 gives continuumd no storage edge beyond continuum-workspace) and is not this bone's.\n\nCRASH-POINT CATALOGUE. `CrashPoint` — nine boundaries: one before each of the eight dispatch steps plus one after the handler (before the replay record write). `CrashInjector`/`NoCrash` is the seam, modelled on the store's StorageFaults and on how region.rs instruments worker phases. `Daemon::dispatch_or_die(request, injector) -> Result<OperationOutcome, Killed>` is the harness entry point; `Daemon::dispatch` is the same pipeline under NoCrash. `Killed` carries the boundary and NO envelope, deliberately: a process that is gone sends no frame and the protocol fixes no code for one that is not there.\nThe finer grain inside step 8 — between a publication's content commit and its index commit, docs/35's own injection point — is NOT duplicated: it is PublicationPhase through the store's StorageFaults, reached from a daemon via the new `Builder::store_faults`. Two seams, composed; neither re-proved.\n`CrashPoint::reaches_the_store()` is true for exactly one boundary, and that is falsifiable rather than decorative: the nine-boundary sweep asserts the recovery report is byte-identical across the eight boundaries before the handler and different after it. A step that grew a durable side effect shows up as two boundaries disagreeing. That is what makes instrumenting all eight load-bearing.\n\nRECONCILIATION RULES (`recovery::recover(&ReferenceStore, &CapabilityToken)`, authorized at `promote` through ReferenceStore::audit_view so it is audited by the store's own sink; takes &self and calls no mutating method).\n1. Every index entry resolves — content present and identifying to the handle it is filed under (the path is a pure function of the handle, so entry/content/identity are three statements that must agree). Failures are StoreDefect::MissingReferent / IdentityMismatch: reported, never repaired.\n2. Ledger against index, both directions: every published identity has receipts (`receiptless` must be empty) and no receipt names an unpublished identity (`orphan_receipts` must be empty). Reported BESIDE the defect list, not inside it — docs/35 fixes fsck's classification as a closed three about content-versus-index, and this is a ledger-versus-index obligation (DX-13's \"no lost receipts\"). The closed set is not widened.\n3. Unreachable content is quarantined: named in the report, left exactly where it is. Recovery NEVER deletes; collect_garbage stays a separate operator decision under its own capability, and a recovery that silently reclaimed would destroy the evidence that a crash happened.\n4. What did not survive is declared per VolatileFact, not inferred.\n\nRECOVERY-REPORT SHAPE. `RecoveryReport` { classes (per-class published/unreachable/bytes — docs/35's \"results per artifact class\"), reconciled [(path, handle)], receipts [(handle, count)], quarantined, receiptless, orphan_receipts, defects (StoreAudit::fsck verbatim), volatile }. `verdict()` is clean | residue | defective. `render()` is a total canonical projection — one fact per line, fixed section order, every list sorted, every input read under one audit view; no clock, no counter, no insertion-order dependence. Byte-equal across two recoveries of one crashed state AND across two independently crashed daemons that ran the same campaign (the two-fresh-daemons device applied to recovery).\n\nIMPL-02 DURABILITY — CLAIMED, with the scope stated.\nClaimed and tested: (a) a parked task's committed publication survives the crash and comes back reconciled — in `surviving(ArtifactClass::Task)`, index-resolved, receipted, reads back under the identity the task named it by; (b) a staged-but-uncommitted partial is absent post-recovery — not published, not readable, unreceipted, present only as named residue.\nEnabling change: publication_commitment's preimage is now a first-class value, `budget::publication_record`, and verification::advance publishes those exact bytes into the store through stage -> commit_content -> commit_index BEFORE committing the publication onto the task, checking that the store's derived handle equals the daemon's commitment (the SealedWorkspace::seal IdentityDisagreement discipline). Previously the name was derived and the bytes discarded, so \"committed evidence survives restart\" had no referent at all.\nDECLINED, explicitly: the task RECORD does not survive. A restart resolves no `Running` task to a continuation or to Failed, because there is no record to resolve. The report declares that per fact instead of reporting an empty task table as an achievement, and the suite checks the declaration behaviourally (task.status denied, task.resume on the surviving continuation denied, the idempotency key no longer replays).\nPublication identity: epochs still deliberately omitted. The store derives identity from bytes and nothing else, so the binding needs no epoch-scoped identity; widening would break two_daemons_name_one_campaigns_publications_identically whenever two daemons' epoch sets differ, for no store-side gain. The routing comment's single point of change is left as the single point of change, with the decision recorded there."}	blake3:NqIp7sXVifK413I0S-o7FNAIKECWKsx4B6FuVGBjDmY
1785705421030011	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyggk		item.comment	bn-3dr	{"body":"[2/2 audit, evidence, gates, concerns]\n\nWIRE AUDIT. No operation added. No request or response field added, removed, or changed. No IDL edit, no revision record, no F-flag. Recovery is an operator action on a store — the surface RFC 0026/IDL §7 keeps outside the operation registry alongside capability administration and content staging — reached through recovery::recover and Daemon::crash/Builder::over, never through dispatch.\nThe one behaviour a deployment can newly reach: verification.start and task.resume answer PublicationAborted when the storage seam refuses the campaign record's publication. verification.start already declares that code in its `errors` clause and task.resume already carries it in daemon::task::FAULTS via rule errors.common's @mutation clause. With the default NoFaults storage the code is unreachable and every existing flow is byte-identical (all 319 pre-existing continuumd tests unchanged and passing). Declared code + governing text = conformance, so no flag (bn-23j7s precedent).\nOne consequence worth naming: a capability registered through Daemon::state_mut AFTER build is not in the store's registry and therefore cannot publish, so it now gets CapabilityDenied from verification.start. That is state_mut's own documented rule (\"can be admitted and it cannot publish, which is fail-closed in the only direction that matters\") now reaching one more operation. Nothing in the tree registers a capability that way.\n\nHARNESS SHOWN LOAD-BEARING.\n- Withheld-mirror control 1 (control_without_the_fault_the_seal_completes_and_the_verdict_is_clean): every negative assertion of the INV-017 tests re-run against an uninjured daemon, and every one comes out the other way — the composite root IS readable, verdict IS clean, quarantined IS empty.\n- Withheld-mirror control 2 (the_report_distinguishes_the_two_publication_phases): a crash at CommittingContent leaves no residue, a crash at CommittingIndex leaves exactly one, and the reports differ. A report printing a constant could not tell them apart.\n- Control 3 (control_the_receipt_count_is_read_from_the_ledger): sealing identical components twice converges on one identity and leaves 2 receipts per record; a recover that counted index entries instead of receipts answers 1.\n- Mutant (mutant_a_corrupted_identity_seam_flips_the_verdict_to_defective): a ContentIdentifier that stops agreeing with itself after the publication — a deliberate violation of the purity contract, the only way docs/35's `identity mismatch` corruption is reachable, since the store never rewrites content — is caught: verdict flips clean -> defective, the entry drops out of `reconciled`, the renderings differ. The faithful control over the same fixture recovers clean, so the catch is the mutation's and not the fixture's.\n- Anti-vacuity guards inside the determinism tests: the agreed renderings are asserted non-trivial (\"quarantined \", \"verdict residue\", \"class task published=1\").\n\nFILES. New: crates/continuumd/src/daemon/recovery.rs (CrashPoint/CrashInjector/NoCrash/Killed, Disposition/VolatileFact, ClassSummary/Verdict/RecoveryReport, recover, 4 unit tests); crates/continuumd/tests/g1_crash_recovery_evidence.rs (16 tests). Modified: daemon/mod.rs (dispatch_or_die + the nine boundaries, Daemon::crash, DurableSubstrate, Builder::over, Builder::store_faults, BoxedFaults); daemon/budget.rs (publication_record / commitment_of split, epoch decision recorded); daemon/verification.rs (publish_record; advance/run_in/start take store + publisher); daemon/task.rs (resume passes store + publisher).\nDOSSIER. plan §4.5's Crash-safety bullet annotated with the (delivered: bn-3dr — ...) convention (§22's G1 bullet deliberately left alone: validate_dossier's gate_scheme_correspondence compares §22 against docs/52 by token overlap, and an annotation there breaks the mirror; §4.5 is where the criterion is stated normatively). G0_SPIKE_MATRIX's DX-13 row: its deferral sentence is split — daemon-scale CRASH INJECTION now cites this bone with what it establishes, and daemon-scale LINEARIZABILITY is left open and stated as such (Daemon::dispatch takes &mut self and serves one request at a time, so there is no concurrent daemon to linearize; that question arrives with the concurrent daemon, per docs/35). Regen ran (generate_traceability). validation-results.json untouched. No checker tolerance was extended.\n\nCOUNTS. continuumd 319 -> 339 (+16 evidence, +4 recovery unit). continuum-workspace 256 unchanged. continuum-task 146 unchanged. No pre-existing test edited.\n\nGATES. cargo fmt --all --check clean; cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings clean; cargo test -p continuumd -p continuum-workspace -p continuum-task --locked green; full `just check` exit 0 (dossier status pass, boundaries/covenant/governance pass, Lean built, axiom manifest up to date).\n\nCONCERNS.\n1. Daemon::dispatch carries one `expect`. It is unreachable for every input — NoCrash::kills is unconditionally false and a unit test pins that over CrashPoint::ALL — and the alternative was fabricating a wire answer for a daemon that is not there. If zero panics in continuumd is the standard, the shape that removes it is an associated-type CrashInjector with NoCrash::Killed = Infallible, at the cost of object safety.\n2. The crash grain is honest but coarse: the store is in-memory and \"survives\" because the harness moves the value, not because anything was flushed. A real crash between a store write and its fsync is below this model. What is evidenced is the daemon-scale composition over a store whose own durability contract is DX-13's; the disk is later.\n3. advance can leave the store holding a record the task never committed (publish succeeds, a later step in run_in fails, the task-side publication is discarded). That is the docs/35 direction — residue is recoverable, a task claiming an absent artifact is not — and the code says so, but a long-running daemon accumulates residue that only collect_garbage reclaims.\n4. recovery::recover probes receipts only for handles it can name (index values plus unreachable content). A receipt for an identity in neither is unconstructible through this store's API, so the check is complete here and would need widening for a store with a separate ledger enumeration.\n5. VolatileFact::ALL is a hand audit of DaemonState's fields, in the style of ReplayKey's field table. A field added to DaemonState without a variant here would be a fact a restart loses in silence; the correspondence is documented and behaviourally spot-checked, not mechanically enforced.\n6. INV-006 is NOT discharged despite the req:inv-006 label. INV-006 as plan §2 states it is replay stability under a pinned semantic epoch, which crates/continuumd/tests/inv006_replay_stability_evidence.rs already addresses; what this bone delivers is §4.5's recovery semantics. Left unannotated rather than claimed — flagging in case the label should be re-routed.\n7. A retry after a restart converges on the residue as well as on the landed records (the crash-caught record's content was already durable, so the retry's content commit converges onto those bytes and its index commit is the entry that was missing). The store therefore ends `clean` rather than carrying a duplicate. This is the store's ordinary convergence and does not contradict RFC 0026's \"the retry is a fresh publication, not a resumption of a partial one\", which is about protocol state; the test names it explicitly rather than glossing it."}	blake3:XnA1rp6zaYrSqyhx9WVXjCMO0k7mM2uMut7qUe_IAKA
1785705546795107	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEygwk		item.comment	bn-3dr	{"body":"Landed @ 3a321d8895c4. Lead re-verified in-workspace: all three crates green (continuumd 339, workspace 256, task 146), fmt/clippy clean, no pre-existing test edited.\n\nRATIFIED. (1) The no-disk-store decision with its reason AT THE TYPE — plan §4.5's restart contract needs a committed continuation, and inferring a task table from the store is exactly the silently-reconstructed state the same paragraph forbids; declaring thirteen VolatileFacts with typed Dispositions is the honest architecture for this build. (2) The crash-grain composition: nine dispatch boundaries + the store's OWN PublicationPhase seam for the INV-017 instant, with reaches_the_store() falsifiable by the sweep — instrumentation proven load-bearing, not decorative. (3) Killed carrying no envelope (a dead process sends no frame). (4) The real store binding for IMPL-02's durability half: publication_record publishing the named bytes through the two-phase protocol BEFORE task-commit — committed partials now genuinely survive restart and read back by identity; uncommitted absent. The declared-not-reconstructed decline for task records is the right half to decline. (5) Epochs stay out of publication identity — content-keyed store needs none; widening would break process-independence for no gain; the routing question from bn-23j7s is CLOSED. (6) Wire audit accepted: no new surface, PublicationAborted already declared and unreachable under NoFaults. (7) The dossier discipline: §4.5 annotated where the criterion is normative, §22 left alone for the token-overlap mirror, DX-13's deferral SPLIT with linearizability explicitly still open (there is no concurrent daemon to linearize — arrives with one, per docs/35).\n\nCONCERN DISPOSITIONS. (1) The one expect in dispatch: pinned-unreachable accepted as-is; the Infallible-injector alternative costs object safety for no defect — declined. (2) Coarse crash grain (value moved, not flushed): honest scope for a store with no fsync story yet; OS-grain crash testing arrives with a disk-backed build. (6) INV-006 label: agent is right — re-routed. The invariant's plan.md heading is now annotated delivered citing inv006_replay_stability_evidence.rs (bn-i4aem's wave), so this close orphans nothing and the label's history is recorded in the annotation.\n\nG1 crash-safety bullet delivered. The daemon durability story is now: store crash-safe (DX-13 cycle), recovery typed and deterministic (this bone), task-table persistence honestly absent until a disk-backed build is dossier-mandated."}	blake3:P2tpMUe5Gn0PBG1CT9pSdORcpwzVT5y6d2oJ2Cwdpj8
