1785715270503926	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyvwk		item.comment	bn-21iq	{"body":"PR-7 / IMPL-02 — provenance. Delivered at library level in `crates/continuum-evidence/src/{provenance,actor,identity}.rs` (commit 74b76c3; 8008db7 adds the GOV-1-07 rationale entry the new dependency edge needs).\n\n## Design\n\nRFC 0038 has no prose provenance section — it delegates (\"This RFC summarizes; the schemas decide\") — so the record is transcribed member for member from the object both `evidence-graph-node.schema.json` and `evidence-graph-edge.schema.json` carry: `actor`, `created_at`, `inputs` required, `tool` optional, `additionalProperties: false`.\n\nThree decisions carry the bullet:\n\n1. **Structural, not validated.** Three of the four members are plain fields of `Provenance`, and `EvidenceNode::propose` / `EvidenceEdge::new` take one by value. \"The graph refuses provenance-free additions\" is therefore not a branch anywhere in `graph.rs`; it is a thing a caller cannot spell. A `compile_fail` doc test in `node.rs` — which compiles as an external crate — is the evidence.\n2. **Epochs are carried and never rendered.** plan §4.6 makes evidence epoch-scoped (\"a receipt remains verifiable under its pinned epoch indefinitely\") and neither schema has a member for it; the provenance object is closed at four. Both naive readings are wrong — dropping the epochs loses §4.6, rendering them inside `provenance` writes a field the schema forbids — so `Provenance` holds an `EpochSet` that `to_record` omits and `to_preimage` includes. Which envelope epochs ride in on the wire is RFC 0026's question and is not answered here.\n3. **`inputs` is a set.** One derivation described two ways would have two content identities (ADR-0013).\n\nActors are the wire alias's four schemes (`agent|human|service|ci`) with a `ServiceIdentity` narrowing, so docs/44's \"without granting authority based on identity\" and RFC 0038's \"promotion is service-restricted\" can both hold: authority is a function of the *scheme*, never of the name.\n\nIdentity is ADR-0013's, in `identity.rs`: `EvidenceIdentity` is the canonical encoding of the record and its equality and ordering consult no hasher; the `ev_` handle the schemas carry is a *name* for it, minted through a labeled non-certified `HashIdentity` under `evidence-graph-handle`. A handle collision is detectable and never a silent merge. The preimage → token function is the `EvidenceNaming` seam, precisely because `edge_id` derivation is bn-3sypm's F14.\n\nTime is an argument, never a reading — nothing in the crate touches a clock (INV-005, ADR-0003, GOV-1-04) — and `Timestamp` transcribes the daemon scalar's one spelling.\n\n## Clause → test\n\n| Clause | Source | Test |\n|---|---|---|\n| four members, three required | both schemas | `record_is_exactly_the_schema_object`, `a_tool_less_record_omits_the_member` |\n| `additionalProperties: false` | both schemas | `record_is_exactly_the_schema_object` |\n| input handles match the artifact pattern | both schemas | `input_handles_match_the_artifact_pattern` |\n| `created_at` is `date-time` | both schemas | `timestamps_are_the_wire_spelling`, `nothing_here_reads_a_clock` |\n| evidence is epoch-scoped | plan §4.6 | `epochs_are_carried_and_never_rendered`, `the_preimage_names_all_six_epochs` |\n| provenance is structural | PR-7 / IMPL-02 | `node.rs` module doc, second `compile_fail`; `nothing_enters_the_graph_without_a_producer` |\n| one derivation, one spelling | ADR-0013 | `inputs_are_a_set`, `provenance_value_is_order_independent` |\n| four actor schemes, one is a service | RFC 0038, daemon `ActorId` | `the_scheme_set_is_the_wire_alias`, `exactly_one_scheme_is_a_service`, `two_agents_differ_only_by_name` |\n| identity is the encoding, not a digest | ADR-0013, plan §20 | `identity_equality_never_consults_a_hash`, `a_handle_is_a_labeled_non_certified_name` |\n| naming is replaceable | bn-3sypm F14 | `a_second_naming_scheme_renames_without_re_identifying` |\n\nIntegration section 2 of `crates/continuum-evidence/tests/pr7_evidence_graph.rs` restates the same claims from outside the crate: `every_record_in_a_populated_graph_names_a_producer` (five nodes, four distinct producers — credit is kept rather than collapsed), `a_provenance_record_is_exactly_the_schema_object_and_carries_its_epochs`, `a_producer_cannot_append_under_another_actors_name`.\n\n## Counts\n\n27 unit tests — `actor.rs` 8, `provenance.rs` 11, `identity.rs` 8 — plus 3 of the 17 integration tests and 2 of the 7 `compile_fail` doc tests.\n\n## Declined / not claimed\n\n- The wire never learns the epochs from here; no RFC 0026 envelope change was made.\n- No daemon join: `continuumd` writes its own `provenance.actor` from the admitted grant and is unchanged.\n- `tool` is the schema's bare string plus non-emptiness. docs/44's fuller list — \"prompts/tool inputs as policy permits, source retrievals\" — has no schema member, and inventing one would be a schema change."}	blake3:brvt_cnSzykCyYs3vfJzzLjVTDByoRCbHHqfTRsdhEo
1785715304530881	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEywAk		item.comment	bn-2c8l	{"body":"PR-7 / IMPL-03 — trusted status transitions. Delivered at library level in `crates/continuum-evidence/src/authority.rs` and the promotion path of `src/graph.rs` (commit b87e749).\n\n## Design\n\nThe daemon already models INV-004 with a private-constructor witness, and the reasoning transfers without importing it (plan §20 puts `continuumd` at this crate's own tier, so the pattern is mirrored and the code is not shared).\n\n**The witness.** `Promotion` has private fields and no public constructor. The only ways to obtain one are `TrustedService::{promote_to, validate, inconclusive}`, each needing a `&TrustedService`, which needs a `ServiceIdentity`, which refuses every actor scheme but `service:`. The chain from an `agent:` actor to a promoted status therefore has no first step, and `EvidenceGraph::promote` — the only function in the crate that writes a status — takes a `&Promotion` and nothing else. Three `compile_fail` doc tests hold the boundary as a client sees it (a doc test compiles as an external crate): a `Promotion` cannot be built by hand, `StatusWrite::promoted` is `pub(crate)` and unreachable, and a `TrustedService` cannot be declared over an `ActorId`.\n\n**The table.** `ServiceRole::may_promote_to` is docs/44's \"Status authority\" table, one cell per row. Two cells are a *reading* rather than a quotation and are marked as such in the module docs so an auditor knows which two to argue with: `refuted`'s \"valid counterexample/checker\" is read as the three roles that check, and `inconclusive`'s \"producing service\" as the four that run an attempt. `proposed` is authorized for no role at all — creation is not a promotion, and docs/44 gives that row to \"any authorized human/agent\".\n\n**The payloads.** plan §11.4 attaches a mandatory payload to two statuses, and both are structural: `validate` is the only path to `validated` and takes a `ValidationBasis`; `inconclusive` is the only path to `inconclusive` and takes a typed INV-008 reason; `promote_to` refuses both by name with `PayloadRequired`. `service_identity` is written only for the four assurance-bearing statuses the node schema requires it on, because `additionalProperties` is false and a field written where the schema does not admit it is as invalid as one missing where it does.\n\n**The three guards, in order.** `EvidenceGraph::promote` checks that the node is held, then INV-004's producer-is-not-the-checker comparison (before the lattice, as `evidence.verify` orders it and for the same reason), then `claim_status::compare_and_set` — this crate's own decision rather than a second copy, so the graph and any independent linearizability checker share one implementation of \"would this write lower the claim?\". Authority and the lattice stay independent guards: a Lean proof service authorized for `proved` is still refused over a `refuted` claim. A promotion appends a version and keeps the node's identity, which is what makes the per-claim compare-and-set meaningful at all.\n\n**Node creation.** `CreationCapability` is RFC 0038's \"Actor capabilities control node creation\" and docs/44's \"actors may append only allowed node types\", plus the check the daemon gets from the wire for free: a node's `provenance.actor` must be the capability's own actor, so a producer cannot append under someone else's name. It grants no promotion — docs/44 keeps the two columns apart.\n\n## Clause → test\n\n| Clause | Source | Test |\n|---|---|---|\n| only trusted services promote into validated/proved | RFC 0038 | `only_two_roles_reach_validated_and_proved`, module doc `compile_fail`s ×3 |\n| agent votes never change status | RFC 0038 | `an_agent_has_no_path_to_a_promotion` |\n| the authority table | docs/44 | `the_authority_table_is_docs_44` (9 statuses × 5 roles, every cell) |\n| service identity on exactly four statuses | node schema | `only_the_four_assurance_statuses_name_a_service` |\n| `validated` records its basis | plan §11.4 | `a_validated_promotion_records_its_basis` |\n| `inconclusive` carries a typed reason | INV-008, plan §11.4 | `an_inconclusive_promotion_carries_a_typed_reason` |\n| a settled reassertion records what the claim holds | plan §11.7 | `a_write_records_the_settled_status_not_the_asked_one` |\n| a service with no roles is refused | — | `a_service_with_no_roles_is_refused` |\n| creation capability is not promotion capability | docs/44 | `a_creation_capability_grants_no_promotion` |\n| actors append only allowed kinds | docs/44 | `an_actor_appends_only_allowed_node_types` (all 20 kinds) |\n| no appending under another name | daemon `observe.ingest` | `a_producer_cannot_append_under_another_name` |\n| a producer may not promote its own claim | INV-004 | `graph.rs`'s `a_service_cannot_promote_its_own_production` |\n| promotion is a compare-and-set | plan §11.7 | `a_lost_compare_and_set_is_a_status_conflict`, `a_downgrade_is_refused` |\n| authority and lattice are independent | plan §11.4 + docs/44 | `authority_and_the_lattice_are_independent_guards` |\n| a promotion keeps the identity | plan §11.7 | `a_promotion_appends_a_version_and_keeps_the_identity` |\n| the PR-7 exit sentence | START_HERE PR 7 | `the_exit_sentence_holds_over_every_role`, and integration `the_pr7_exit_sentence_holds_from_outside_the_crate` |\n\nAnti-vacuity is explicit in three places: `the_exit_sentence_holds_over_every_role` asserts the graph actually *reached* `validated` once during the five-role sweep, so the sweep is not passing because nothing landed; `a_service_cannot_promote_its_own_production` shows a *different* service promoting the identical node, so the refusal is about the identity match rather than about the node; `an_actor_appends_only_allowed_node_types` runs an unrestricted capability over all twenty kinds beside the restricted one.\n\n## Counts\n\n11 unit tests in `authority.rs`, 8 promotion tests in `graph.rs`, 4 of the 17 integration tests, 3 `compile_fail` doc tests.\n\n## Declined / not claimed\n\n- **Library-level.** The daemon's own promotion path is unchanged; no operation was added to the IDL or the RFC 0027 registry. The PR-7 exit sentence is held here as a property of this crate's public API, asserted from an integration test that links it as a separate crate. Its wire-level reading is already delivered separately by `continuumd`'s `evidence.verify` and is not re-claimed.\n- **`TrustedService` construction is the deployment's trust declaration.** This crate cannot decide who is trusted and does not pretend to; what it guarantees is that a declaration is *required*, that it can only name a `service:` actor, and that the roles bound what the service can reach.\n- **Edge-creation authority is not modelled**, because RFC 0038's Authority section covers only node creation and status promotion — bn-3sypm's F14 says so in as many words.\n- **No evidence-class ceiling.** The daemon refuses to promote past `Observed` when its own check supports no more; that is a statement about a *check*, and this crate runs none, so it does not restate it. A service that claims more than it checked is refused by INV-004's self-certification guard only when it is also the producer."}	blake3:ik2SmfpsLqk7EzNg9EHEcu8fva6MdS59rOxQWGmQ4B0
1785715335676058	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEywQk		item.comment	bn-2acj	{"body":"PR-7 / IMPL-04 — conflict nodes. Delivered at library level in `crates/continuum-evidence/src/conflict.rs` and the conflict path of `src/graph.rs` (commit a7b3101).\n\n## Design\n\ndocs/44's conflict block has four lines and three of them are structure — the parties, the ground on which they cannot both hold, and the experiment or proof that would distinguish them — so `Conflict` carries all three and all three are mandatory. Fewer than two *distinct* parties, an empty ground, or an empty required experiment are refusals: a conflict nobody can act on is exactly how an ambiguity stops being visible (GOV-1-12's \"ambiguity is an error\", INV-018's spirit, docs/44's \"unresolved contradictions remain visible\").\n\n**Never silently choose.** Nothing in `conflict.rs` or `graph.rs` ranks the parties. There is no confidence field to read, no join over their statuses, and no automatic resolution — `ClaimStatus::join` already records in its own doc that it is a lattice operation and *not* a conflict-resolution rule. `EvidenceGraph::contradictions` reports pairs and returns; it never deletes, never promotes, and never picks. `Resolution` is what a coordinator produces instead, and it is refused unless it names **evidence** (an artifact, not a vote) and unless the node recording it is a `decision` — plan §11.2's `ReviewDecision`, which is the thing at the other end of docs/44's \"explicit edge\".\n\n**Resolution is a transition.** docs/44 gives exactly one row for retiring a claim — `any → superseded | policy/owner with explicit edge` — and plan §4.6 says what the edge is (\"linked ... by `SUPERSEDES` edges; nothing is rewritten in place\"). `EvidenceGraph::resolve_conflict` is those two sentences in order: append the explicit `SUPERSEDES` edge from the decision node to the conflict node, then promote the conflict through the ordinary `promote` path — so INV-004 and plan §11.7's compare-and-set both still apply, and only a service holding `ServiceRole::PolicyOwner` can mint the promotion at all. Nothing is removed; there is no removal API in the crate.\n\n**What a conflict can name.** `ConflictSubject` is a node **or** an edge, because a contradiction is often between two assertions rather than two artifacts — a `SUPPORTS`/`REFUTES` pair over one claim is `contradictions`' whole output. Materialization draws what the graph's shape allows and names what it cannot: `CONFLICTS_WITH` once per unordered pair of node subjects in canonical identity order (the relation is symmetric, and the other direction would be a second content identity for one fact), `DERIVED_FROM` from the conflict node to each node subject, and *every* subject's handle — edges included — in the conflict node's `provenance.inputs`, which is the \"from-what-inputs\" member and the schema-conforming place to record what a conflict is about when no edge can point at it. Handles come from the `EvidenceNaming` seam, so this path commits to no `edge_id` derivation either.\n\n**The contradiction table** (`graph::CONTRADICTORY_KINDS`) is deliberately two rows, each read off the dossier — `SUPPORTS`/`REFUTES` from docs/44's integrator outputs and plan §11.7, `SUPPORTS`/`INVALIDATES` from docs/44's \"one patch repairs one failure but invalidates a proof\". A longer table would be this crate inventing semantics for relations the dossier defines only by name, and a wrong contradiction is worse than a missing one: it manufactures conflict nodes nobody can resolve.\n\n## Clause → test\n\n| Clause | Source | Test |\n|---|---|---|\n| at least two distinct parties | docs/44's block | `a_conflict_needs_two_subjects` |\n| ground and required experiment mandatory | docs/44's block | `a_conflict_states_its_ground_and_its_experiment` |\n| contradictions materialize a conflict node | plan §11.7 | `graph.rs`'s `a_contradiction_materializes_a_conflict_node` |\n| the same subject, or the other direction, is not a contradiction | — | `a_contradiction_needs_the_same_subject` |\n| provenance names every subject | docs/44, node schema | `the_conflict_node_names_every_subject_as_an_input` |\n| `CONFLICTS_WITH` written once per pair | ADR-0013 | `conflicts_with_edges_are_written_once_per_pair` |\n| resolution names evidence, not confidence | docs/44 | `a_resolution_without_evidence_is_refused` |\n| resolution is recorded by a `decision` | plan §11.2, docs/44 | `a_resolution_is_recorded_by_a_decision_node` (all 20 kinds) |\n| the explicit edge carries the evidence | docs/44 | `the_supersedes_edge_carries_the_resolutions_evidence` |\n| only a policy owner retires a claim | docs/44 | `only_a_superseding_promotion_resolves` (all 5 roles) |\n| resolution is a transition, not a deletion | plan §4.6, docs/44 | `resolving_a_conflict_deletes_nothing` |\n| unresolved contradictions remain visible | docs/44 | `an_unresolved_conflict_stays_visible` |\n| a conflict's own references must resolve | docs/44 | `a_conflict_about_an_unheld_subject_is_refused` |\n\n`resolving_a_conflict_deletes_nothing` is the load-bearing one: it captures node and edge counts and every held identity before the resolution, then asserts counts (`+1` edge, `+0` nodes), re-reads every identity, pins the surviving `CONFLICTS_WITH` link, and checks the conflict node's own two-entry status history. Integration section 4 runs the same shape end to end from outside the crate, including that both parties stay at version 0.\n\n## Counts\n\n7 unit tests in `conflict.rs`, 5 conflict tests in `graph.rs`, 2 of the 17 integration tests.\n\n## Declined / not claimed\n\n- **`ConflictGround` and `RequiredExperiment` are prose**, deliberately. docs/44's own example is prose and no vocabulary for either exists anywhere in the dossier; a closed enum here would be this crate deciding a taxonomy nobody has written. Refusing the empty string is the whole of what can honestly be enforced.\n- **An edge subject cannot be an edge endpoint.** The graph's edges join nodes, so an edge party is recorded in `provenance.inputs` rather than linked. Named in the module docs rather than papered over.\n- **No wire surface.** Nothing appends a conflict node or a `CONFLICTS_WITH` edge over the protocol; that is the same 73rd-operation question bn-3sypm holds for `CHECKED_BY`."}	blake3:rh3PX7XnjAnrHqAv0YClrXWgJcH-QL8ofgBocCFB2CY
1785715425720417	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEywgk		item.comment	bn-3cyf	{"body":"PR-7 / IMPL-01 — support/refute/depend/refine/explain/repair/check edges. Delivered at library level in `crates/continuum-evidence/src/edge.rs`, with `src/{node,identity,graph}.rs` (commit a940b0a; evidence suite 7b3717d; annotations 3ea8b36).\n\n## Design\n\n**Thirteen, not seven.** plan §11.3 and the edge schema's `kind` enum name thirteen edge types; the PR bullet names seven lower-case words. `EdgeKind` is the thirteen in the schema's own tokens and `PR7_BULLET_WORDS` carries the correspondence as data. Implementing only seven would have been the worse reading of \"closed vocabulary\": PR 7's own conflict and status bullets need `CONFLICTS_WITH` and `SUPERSEDES`, and an enum missing six members of a closed schema enum cannot round-trip a conforming instance. The test pins that each bullet word is a lower-case prefix of its kind's token, and lists the six the bullet does not name.\n\n**`CHECKED_BY` requires a checker as a type.** The edge schema's one `if`/`then` (and plan §2's SD-11, \"`evidence-graph-edge` requires `checker` on `CHECKED_BY` (INV-004)\") is stated as a sum rather than as a validation: `EdgeRelation` is twelve unit variants plus `CheckedBy(CheckerBinding)`, the binding carries a `ServiceIdentity` that refuses every actor scheme but `service:`, and there is no `Option` anywhere on the path. Two `compile_fail` doc tests — which compile as an external crate — hold both halves: `EdgeRelation::CheckedBy` alone is a constructor function and never a relation, and `CheckerBinding::new` does not take a string.\n\n**No stringly edges.** `EdgeKind::from_token` is the only parse and it refuses `supports`, `CHECKS`, `SUPPORTS `, and the empty string. `EvidenceEdge` stores a relation, not a token.\n\n**Content identity.** An edge's identity is the canonical encoding of what it asserts: the relation including the checker, both typed endpoints, the evidence named, and the producer. Provenance is inside the identity deliberately — two agents that independently assert the same `SUPPORTS` edge have produced two pieces of evidence, and collapsing them either loses the credit docs/44 keeps or makes the surviving provenance depend on write order, which plan §11.7 forbids and GOV-1-12 calls ambiguity.\n\n**Typed endpoints.** `NodeRef` carries the node's identity *and* its kind, and can only be taken from a node. The RFC types none of the thirteen relations' endpoints, so no endpoint rule is imposed — carrying the kind is what makes any rule *expressible*, which is exactly what the one open question in this area needs.\n\n**The graph.** Append-only put-if-absent keyed by content identity; no removal, no replacement, no mutable accessor. Refuses an edge whose endpoint it does not hold (docs/44's \"references must resolve\", RFC 0038's whiteboard compiler \"rejects nonexistent references\"), refuses a self-edge on every relation (an artifact that supports, checks or repairs itself asserts nothing checkable; for `CHECKED_BY` it is INV-004 in its purest form), and reports contradictions without ever choosing. Every accessor iterates a `BTreeMap` keyed by content identity, so order is a function of content alone (GOV-1-03, INV-005).\n\n## The bn-3sypm boundary, as drawn\n\nRFC 0038's OPEN F14 asks three questions and this crate answers **none** of them:\n\n| F14 question | How it is left open |\n|---|---|\n| which node kind the `CHECKED_BY` to-handle names | `edge::CheckTargetRule`, a graph-level *policy* defaulting to `Undecided` (admits every kind). `CHECKED_BY_TARGET_CANDIDATES` records bn-3sypm's three candidates, and `every_candidate_answer_to_f14_is_expressible` asserts each is expressible as `one_of` and refuses the other two, plus the union. The day the RFC decides, the answer is one value of an existing type. |\n| `edge_id` derivation | `identity::EvidenceNaming`, a seam. What the crate commits to is the *preimage* — the canonical encoding of the edge record, a statement about what an edge **is** and the same statement under any naming function. The preimage → `ev_…` function is supplied (`DigestNaming`, a labeled non-certified `HashIdentity` per ADR-0013) and replaceable; `a_second_naming_scheme_renames_without_re_identifying` pins that a new naming changes no identity. |\n| edge-creation authority | not modelled at all. `authority.rs` covers exactly what RFC 0038's Authority section covers — node creation and status promotion — and mints no edge capability. |\n\nNothing was added to the IDL, the RFC 0027 registry, or `continuumd`. No operation appends an edge over the wire; `evidence.query` still truthfully answers no edges and `evidence.get` still returns `edge: null`.\n\n## Clause → test\n\n| Clause | Source | Test |\n|---|---|---|\n| thirteen kinds, the schema's tokens | plan §11.3, edge schema | `the_kind_set_is_exactly_the_schema_enum` |\n| the bullet's seven are seven of them | PR 7 bullet 1 | `the_bullets_seven_words_name_seven_of_the_thirteen` |\n| no stringly edges | GOV-1-12 | `an_unknown_token_does_not_parse` |\n| `CHECKED_BY` requires `checker` | edge schema, SD-11, INV-004 | module doc `compile_fail`s ×2; `only_a_check_edge_names_a_checker` |\n| the checker is a service | plan §11.3, RFC 0027 P3 | `a_checker_is_a_service_identity` |\n| edges are content-identified | docs/44, ADR-0013 | `what_an_edge_asserts_is_in_its_identity` (relation, checker, endpoints, direction, evidence, producer) |\n| evidence is a set | ADR-0013 | `evidence_is_a_set_in_canonical_order` |\n| an edge relates two artifacts | INV-004 | `a_self_edge_is_refused` |\n| the record is the schema object | edge schema | `the_record_is_the_schema_object` |\n| F14 stays open, and is expressible | bn-3sypm | `every_candidate_answer_to_f14_is_expressible`, `the_check_target_rule_is_undecided_by_default`, `a_decided_check_target_rule_is_enforced` |\n| twenty node kinds, plan §11.2 names beside them | plan §11.2, node schema | `the_kind_set_is_exactly_the_schema_enum`, `every_kind_names_its_plan_11_2_type` |\n| creation lands at `proposed`, no other constructor | docs/44, INV-004 | `a_new_node_is_proposed_and_nothing_else`, module doc `compile_fail` |\n| identity excludes status and idempotency key | plan §11.7, RFC 0038 | `identity_is_fixed_across_versions`, `the_idempotency_key_is_outside_the_identity` |\n| what a node says *is* in its identity | ADR-0013 | `what_a_node_says_is_in_its_identity` |\n| append-only, replay converges | RFC 0038 | `an_identical_write_converges_and_changes_nothing`, `the_graph_exposes_no_removal` |\n| references must resolve | docs/44 | `an_edge_to_an_unheld_node_is_refused` |\n| deterministic iteration | GOV-1-03, INV-005 | `iteration_order_is_content_order` |"}	blake3:LXF5y9Ry2EAQ6Prt7fF3kcQMpmyI2Aa1xT5U4w6mjQ0
1785715425760384	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEywwk		item.comment	bn-3cyf	{"body":"# Architecture report (PR 7 overall)\n\n## RFC 0038 fidelity\n\nRFC 0038 is 29 lines and says outright that the schemas decide, so every vocabulary in the crate is transcribed from `evidence-graph-{node,edge}.schema.json` token for token and each transcription has a test that spells the enum out. Where the RFC's prose is the only source — the Authority section, the write model — it is quoted at the type that implements it.\n\nDelivered against the RFC section by section:\n\n- **Nodes/edges.** Twenty node kinds and thirteen edge kinds, closed, with plan §11.2's spellings carried beside the wire tokens. \"Typed immutable candidates\" is literal: a node has no setter and no interior mutability, and a promotion produces a *new* value whose identity is unchanged — the history is versioned state hanging off a fixed identity, which is what plan §11.7's per-claim compare-and-set requires. \"Edges name checker/evidence when applicable\" is the `CheckedBy(CheckerBinding)` variant and the `evidence` set.\n- **Authority.** docs/44's table cell by cell, with the two readings marked. Node creation is `CreationCapability`; promotion is the `Promotion` witness. \"Confidence is metadata\" is honoured by there being no confidence anywhere in the crate.\n- **Write and concurrency model.** Append-only put-if-absent; a replayed write returns the original identity; promotion is a per-claim compare-and-set that cannot regress the lattice; contradictory claims materialize a `Conflict` node rather than resolving by write order; `idempotency_key`, `claim_id` and `service_identity` are all carried, the last on exactly the four statuses the schema conditions on. **Not delivered:** actual concurrency. There is no lock, no linearization point, and no cross-writer serialization here — plan §11.7 itself flags enforcement as a frontier lane (§24.5, research/08), and what this crate provides is the *decision* those enforcement mechanisms will be written against, plus `verify_promotion_history` as the acceptance predicate.\n- **Queries.** Delivered: the traversal and selection layer — by kind, by status, by claim, by producer, incident edges — plus `contradictions` (\"conflicting candidates\"), `conflicts`, and `unresolved_conflicts`. **Not delivered:** missing obligations, proof frontier, repair frontier, semantic duplicates, task generation. Those are semantic queries needing artifact content this crate does not hold; they are named as not-delivered in the module docs rather than approximated over handles.\n- **Whiteboard compiler.** Not delivered, and not in any PR-7 bullet. Its one rule that *is* enforceable at this layer — \"rejects nonexistent references\" — is `add_edge`'s endpoint check.\n- **Acceptance (\"parallel swarm with conflicting abstractions and adversarial false consensus\").** Not delivered. It needs a swarm and a daemon.\n\n## The bn-3sypm boundary as drawn\n\nThree F14 questions, three non-answers, each visible at the type: `CheckTargetRule::Undecided` (with all three candidates expressible and tested to refuse each other), `EvidenceNaming` (with the identity being the preimage, so a naming decision is not an identity decision), and no edge-creation capability at all. Both module docs quote bn-3sypm directly, so a reader who reaches these types learns whose decision is pending and where it lands. Nothing in the IDL, the RFC 0027 registry, or `continuumd` changed.\n\nThe library-level `check` edge does deliver what the bone asked of it: the edge kind exists, is typed, and INV-004's rule — a check edge REQUIRES a checker binding — is enforced by construction with no constructor that omits it.\n\n## Daemon-join status\n\nNot started, and deliberately. The crate declares one workspace edge, to `continuum-value`; it does not depend on `continuumd` (plan §20 puts them at the same tier, and the reverse edge already exists). Concretely, what a join would need:\n\n1. an operation that appends an edge — the 73rd operation, which is bn-3sypm's coordinated plan §10.2 + RFC 0027 registry + IDL + daemon change;\n2. reconciling two node-identity derivations. `continuumd`'s `observe.ingest` derives an evidence handle from `(trace commitment, instrumentation profile)`; this crate derives a node identity from the whole record minus status and idempotency key. RFC 0038 fixes neither. They are two derivations for two surfaces today, and a join has to pick one — see concerns.\n3. moving `daemon::state::EvidenceNode`/`StatusWrite` onto these types. The shapes already line up (the daemon's `Promotion::write` and this crate's are the same function), so it is a substitution rather than a redesign, but it is a `continuumd` change and this bone did not make it.\n\n## Concerns\n\n1. **Two node-identity derivations exist.** The daemon's is narrower than the library's; they will disagree on the same logical node. Neither is wrong — RFC 0038 fixes no node derivation — but a join must choose, and the choice is observable (it decides when two ingests converge). Worth putting on bn-3sypm's plate beside `edge_id`, since it is the same class of question.\n2. **Two cells of the authority table are readings.** `refuted` ← \"valid counterexample/checker\" and `inconclusive` ← \"producing service\" are the only cells docs/44 does not name a role for outright. They are marked in the module docs, but if the dossier later names them differently, `ServiceRole::may_promote_to` is the single place to change.\n3. **`CONTRADICTORY_KINDS` is two rows.** Deliberately conservative; a real contradiction between, say, `REFINES` and `INVALIDATES` would go unreported today. Under-reporting was chosen over inventing semantics, because a spurious conflict node is unresolvable by construction.\n4. **An edge cannot be an edge's endpoint**, so a conflict between two assertions is recorded in the conflict node's `provenance.inputs` rather than linked. It is schema-conforming and tested, but a graph traversal will not reach it.\n5. **`ConflictGround`/`RequiredExperiment` are prose.** No dossier vocabulary exists; only non-emptiness is enforced.\n6. **Concurrency is a decision, not an enforcement.** See above — plan §24.5's ratified criteria (writer counts, zero-tolerance bounds, deterministic conflict artifacts) are untouched by this PR.\n7. **The PR-7 exit line is not annotated.** The exit sentence is held here as a property of the crate's public API, from an integration test that links it as a separate crate. Its wire-level reading belongs to a daemon claim, and annotating the exit from a library bone would overstate it.\n\n## Counts\n\n- Commits: 6 (`74b76c3` bn-21iq, `a940b0a` bn-3cyf, `b87e749` bn-2c8l, `a7b3101` bn-2acj, `7b3717d` bn-3cyf evidence, `3ea8b36` annotations + regen, `8008db7` GOV-1-07 rationale).\n- Files: 8 new modules + `lib.rs` + `Cargo.toml` + 1 integration suite; 7,447 insertions, 21 deletions.\n- Tests: 129 unit + 17 integration + 3 doc + 7 `compile_fail` doc = **156** in `continuum-evidence`, against 40 before. Workspace total under `just check`: **2,074 passing** (baseline 1,958; +116, all in this crate). 0 failures, 0 ignored.\n- Gates: `cargo fmt --all --check`, `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings`, `cargo test --workspace --locked`, and full `just check` — including boundaries, kernel covenant, governance, dossier and Lean — all exit 0. `notes/plan/validation-results.json` untouched."}	blake3:cVZhFPnVLWPUVhNX7ASN3xygol26eKUFfm4yedkAQ0w
1785715457595678	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyxAk		item.comment	bn-3cyf	{"body":"Correction to the architecture report above: the commit count is **7**, not 6 — the list that follows it is right and the number was wrong. `74b76c3` (bn-21iq), `a940b0a` (bn-3cyf), `b87e749` (bn-2c8l), `a7b3101` (bn-2acj), `7b3717d` (bn-3cyf, evidence suite), `3ea8b36` (annotations + regen), `8008db7` (GOV-1-07 rationale). Every other figure in that section — files, insertions, 156 crate tests, 2,074 workspace tests, gates at exit 0 — stands."}	blake3:n0e5NddjPeodQyn7JT56XfkOJqZ1ezUYby2NNiPlHco
1785715540700006	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyxQk		item.comment	bn-3cyf	{"body":"Landed @ 5179094da4c4. Lead re-verified: continuum-evidence 156 tests green (129 unit + 17 integration + 3 doc + 7 compile_fail), workspace 2,074/0, merge clean.\n\nRATIFIED — the quartet and its four load-bearing choices. (1) THIRTEEN edges not seven: the closed vocabulary is the schema's token set (seven alone cannot round-trip a conforming instance, and the quartet's own later bullets need CONFLICTS_WITH/SUPERSEDES), with PR7_BULLET_WORDS recording the bullet mapping — the honest reading of a bullet that under-enumerates its own schema. (2) CheckedBy(CheckerBinding) with no Option on the path and compile_fail doc tests holding the external view — INV-004's checker-required rule as a property of what compiles. (3) Provenance UNSPELLABLE-without rather than rejected-without (three by-value fields), epochs carried-never-rendered per the schema/plan §4.6 split. (4) Promotion private-fields-no-public-constructor reachable only through TrustedService — the daemon's witness pattern mirrored without importing the daemon; conflicts with mandatory parties/ground/required-experiment, nothing ranked, nothing deleted, resolution = docs/44's one retirement row asserted by counts and re-reads.\n\nTHE bn-3sypm BOUNDARY as drawn is exactly right: CheckTargetRule::Undecided expressing all three candidates and refusing the other two under each; EvidenceNaming as a seam because identity-is-the-preimage makes naming a separate decision; authority unmodelled. CONCERN 1 ROUTED to bn-3sypm (comment following): two node-identity derivations now exist (daemon observe.ingest = trace-commitment+profile; this crate = whole-record-minus-status-and-key) and RFC 0038 fixes neither — the daemon join must choose observably.\n\nHonest declines accepted: readings-not-quotations marked at the type (two docs/44 cells); CONTRADICTORY_KINDS two dossier-sourced rows (under-reporting over invention); six of seven RFC 0038 queries named not-delivered; edge-subject-of-conflict in provenance.inputs with the traversal gap stated. The mid-run GOV-1-07 staleness catch (inv005's live re-run firing twice on the new crate edge) is the freshness-tripwire design working as built — and the cargo-aborts-at-first-failing-binary counting caveat is noted for future baselines."}	blake3:e2fUonS1-k55aw-cP_qs5cahO-yjfQ40dmwyySQuqsg
1785715540739302	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyxgk		item.move	bn-3cyf	{"state":"done"}	blake3:S6dyXlsEj6acyeJivvx0SV4tlIWYeogxoY3MmN1Nfi8
