1785722465949846	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy_gk		item.comment	bn-1h158	{"body":"Delivered @ ea1de06, in .maw/workspaces/bn-1h158. NOT closed, not merged, not pushed.\n\nTHE RULE. New `rule handshake.bootstrap_encoding` in the IDL's §7, right after `rule handshake.negotiation`: `ClientHello`, `ServerWelcome`, and `ServerReject` MUST be `canonical_json` regardless of `ClientHello.encodings` and regardless of what negotiation selects; the negotiated encoding governs every frame from the first `RequestEnvelope` onward, both directions, life of the connection — no third, mixed state.\n\nidl_version 1.4 -> 1.5, revision-history entry naming bn-1mhcr's finding and this bone. `version` stays \"3.3\": none of `rule versioning.compatible_change`'s five triggers fires (no operation/field/enum member/relaxed constraint/error code), so no minor bump and no F-flag — bn-23j7s precedent, stated in the IDL's version-doc comment and in a new RFC 0026 \"Versioning and revision\" bullet: \"a declared rule over an already-conforming wire is conformance, not a flag.\"\n\nTRANSPORT-BEHAVIOR VERIFICATION (read before writing, not invented). `encode_hello` is literally `codec::to_bytes` = `write_in::<Json,_>`, hardcoded, no branch on `hello.encodings` or on anything negotiated. `Server::open` calls the same `to_bytes` for both `welcome` and `reject`; neither has ever read `Negotiated`. `Server::answer` matches `self.negotiated.encoding()` and dispatches to `answer_in::<Json>`/`answer_in::<Cbor>` (bn-1mhcr's landed work, unchanged). The rule states exactly this split.\n\nRFC 0026 ALIGNMENT. \"Connection lifecycle\" item 2 (\"Encoding\") gets the sentence-level addition citing the rule, plus a new \"Versioning and revision\" bullet for 1.5 (matching the existing per-revision bullet pattern). No numbered \"Corrections recorded by this RFC\" entry — that section resolves RFC/IDL disagreements, and this is new prose filling a gap both left open, same category as 3.3's `evidence.link`, which also landed without one.\n\nCONFORMANCE PIN. New transport_local.rs test `the_bootstrap_frames_are_canonical_json_however_the_connection_negotiates`:\n- `ClientHello` offering `canonical_cbor` first (adversarial case) — pins `encode_hello`'s literal bytes: `{\"actor\":\"service:continuumd\",\"capability\":\"cap_root\",\"client\":\"continuumd-transport-test\",\"encodings\":[\"canonical_cbor\",\"canonical_json\"],\"protocol_versions\":{\"high\":\"3.2\",\"low\":\"3.0\"}}`. `Json::parse` ok, `Cbor::parse` fails.\n- Negotiates to `canonical_cbor` (confirmed that's actually what lands), builds `ServerWelcome`/`ServerReject` over that outcome, asserts both frames still parse as JSON / fail as CBOR and round-trip correctly, while the *value* `ServerWelcome.encoding` correctly names CBOR — frame bytes are JSON, what it says is CBOR.\n- Post-negotiation half cited, not re-proven: `a_cbor_connection_serves_a_whole_exchange_in_cbor` (`codec_canonical_cbor.rs`, bn-1mhcr) already drives a real CBOR-negotiated exchange and asserts both frames are CBOR-not-JSON; doc comment names it as the other half of the same claim.\n\n`transport/mod.rs`'s module doc rewritten to cite the rule instead of \"the gap ... belongs in the IDL, not here.\"\n\nPRE-EXISTING TEST CHANGED — ONE, MECHANICAL. `idl_conformance.rs`'s `document.rules.len()` moves 38->39 (same bookkeeping the file's comment block already tracks per revision); comment extended with an IDL-1.5 paragraph. `IDL_VERSION` in `registry.rs` (a `pub const` the conformance test reads against the parsed IDL, not a test literal) moved 1.4->1.5 with `PROTOCOL_VERSION`'s doc explaining why it doesn't move alongside.\n\nWIRE AUDIT. No operation/field/enum member/error code added; no presence marker moved; no wire byte differs before/after. Recorded in three places (IDL version-doc, RFC bullet, `registry.rs` doc) so a reviewer finds the reasoning wherever they look.\n\nCOUNTS. continuumd: 395 -> 396 (+1, verified by stashing the diff and re-running against the pre-change tree). Full workspace (`cargo test --workspace --locked`, incl. doc-tests): 2,197 passed, 0 failed post-change; did not reconcile against the brief's quoted 2,164 baseline — the continuumd stash-diff is the delta I can vouch for precisely (+1, nothing removed).\n\nGATES. fmt clean. clippy `--all-targets -D warnings` clean. `cargo test -p continuumd --locked` green (396/396). `cargo doc -p continuumd --no-deps`: new intra-doc links all resolve, no new warnings. Full `just check` exit 0 (fmt/clippy/full workspace test/boundaries/covenant/governance/dossier/Lean). Traceability regen run — no-op (RFC/IDL prose isn't plan.md/START_HERE). `validate_dossier.py` pass. `validation-results.json` untouched.\n\nCONCERNS.\n1. Judged the RFC's \"Corrections recorded\" section out of scope (see above); if the lead wants discoverability there, it's one more entry under a \"3.3\" subsection that doesn't exist yet (3.3's own `evidence.link` didn't get one either).\n2. Full-workspace baseline arithmetic (2,164 vs 2,197) unreconciled; verified the delta precisely at continuumd level instead, per the bn-24i/bn-bou09 precedent of not chasing a baseline discrepancy once the delta is independently confirmed.\n3. `ServerWelcome`'s bootstrap bytes are asserted structurally (parses-as-JSON, fails-as-CBOR, round-trips) rather than literal-pinned like `ClientHello`'s — no golden literal existed to reuse, and the bone's wording named `ClientHello` specifically for the literal pin. Can add one for `ServerWelcome` too if wanted."}	blake3:r-jbfiROdp5kpSnS7YYBI_JXxqCmqkGtMHPSKCUnCWM
1785722521274800	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy_wk		item.comment	bn-1h158	{"body":"Landed @ 510fb9e22fc6. Lead re-verified: continuumd 396 green, merge clean.\n\nRATIFIED. (1) The rule states exactly the landed split (encode_hello/Server::open hardcoded canonical JSON verified in code; Server::answer on Negotiated::encoding()) — described, never invented. (2) The three-frame scope (ClientHello, ServerWelcome, ServerReject all bootstrap — the reject of a failed negotiation obviously cannot ride the encoding that was never agreed). (3) idl_version 1.5 with no protocol bump, the versioning.compatible_change triggers checked by name — the bn-23j7s rule applied precisely. (4) The conformance pin covering the adversarial half (CBOR offered FIRST, bootstrap still JSON) with the post-negotiation half cited to bn-1mhcr's existing coverage rather than duplicated. (5) transport/mod.rs now cites the rule instead of apologizing for its absence — the finding's lifecycle closed end to end.\n\nPR-5's protocol surface is now, to my knowledge, free of recorded unwritten rules."}	blake3:hWXweWeWyhmej7lJueaHKLWNJ_nA7IJQNiOK8I4k7G8
1785722521312454	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEygAo		item.move	bn-1h158	{"state":"done"}	blake3:-LPuu7fNid_oVaCXoOF9anm_3y7u2MvrsQPhS7G1WPc
1785723100678772	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEygQo		item.comment	bn-dtg61	{"body":"Delivered in-workspace at d114791. `just check` exit 0.\n\n## 1. §20 / boundary audit — the edge is PERMITTED\n\n§20 states one rule about this crate: \"proof/certificate checker does not depend on search\nengines\". It constrains what continuum-certificate depends on, not who depends on it, and\n`certificate-checker-not-search` walks the closure *outward from* it and the four kernels — so\n`continuumd -> continuum-certificate` cannot enter it. continuumd's engine-reference edge stays\non the far side for the reason its own rationale gives: the forbidden engine edge is the\n*checker's*. KCOV-06 fixes the direction from the other end (ADMITTED_KERNEL_DEPENDENCIES empty).\ncontinuumd is not an adapter; §20's crate list is unchanged. After the edge: boundaries self-test\n15/15, check `pass`, 27 internal edges (was 26), 0 violations; GOV-1 twelve/twelve pass.\n\nGOV-1-07 entry added: origin `workspace`, tcb_class `trusted-checking-base`, kinds\n`[\"dev\",\"normal\"]`, used_by `[\"continuumd\"]`. The class is not a self-assessment — it *is* the\nchecking base's entry point, and `checker-dependencies-are-tcb` enforces it for everything the\ncrate links. One entry inserted; nothing else in that HOT file touched.\n\n## 2. Byte source — no gap, the store is real\n\n`EvidenceNode.artifact` is a `Commitment`; `DaemonState::staged` resolves it to\n`StagedFile{path,content}`. The lane reads `staged.content` — the same held bytes the observation\nlane re-derives — and hands the whole slice on, magic included. The daemon reads none of it: not\na magic, not a length, not an epoch.\n\nOrdering: `check()` steps 2 and 3 (reference resolves; bytes re-derive to the commitment; node\nre-derives to the identity it is filed under) now run on **both** lanes, before the kernel. A\nkernel cannot see a misfiling — a well-formed certificate is well-formed wherever it is filed —\nso skipping them here would have opened the retargeting attack the observation lane refuses.\nConsequence: `values` is honestly produced on this lane too.\n\nScope: only `EvidenceKind::Certificate` routes. The other ten keep the `InsufficientEvidence` arm\nverbatim; `finite-exact`/`inductive` name a *result* class, not a certificate artifact.\n\n## 3. Verdict fidelity — all four expressible, NO FLAG\n\nAudited the declared shape (IDL 3291-3317 + `rule errors.common`). It carries all four with no\ndistinction collapsed, so this is conformance and no flag is raised (bn-23j7s). Nothing added to\nthe IDL: no field, no code, no rule.\n\n- `Checked(Verified)` -> `ok`, promoted to `validated`, `validation_basis`, `established` /\n  assurance_class `validated`. The only path in this daemon reaching `validated`.\n- `Checked(Rejected)` -> `error CertificateRejected` — the code's own sentence is \"An independent\n  checker rejected a certificate or proof artifact\".\n- `Checked(Unsupported)` -> `ok`, nothing promoted, `inconclusive` + reason `Unsupported`.\n  **Decisive**: `ResultEnvelope.verdict` is null on `status = error`, so\n  `SemanticVerdictValue.inconclusive_reason` is the *only* channel INV-008's typed reason has; an\n  error would have kept \"inconclusive\" and lost which of the six reasons.\n- `Unroutable` -> `error EpochUnsupported`: \"An artifact […] declares a schema or semantic epoch\n  unknown or incompatible with this daemon. Typed rejection, never best-effort decoding (docs/09\n  T13)\" — T13's control is literally \"no 'best effort' decode for evidence\". Already in this\n  operation's own `errors` clause and unreachable until now. `UnsupportedSemanticFeature`\n  rejected: it names a *deployment* lane that has not shipped, which would be false.\n  `CertificateRejected` rejected: nothing decoded the bytes, so nothing may call them invalid.\n  The two `RoutingFault` variants share the code — one fact, one recovery — and get two distinct\n  static details.\n\nExtra fidelity fix found in the audit and taken: continuum-kernel-smt is the one kernel whose\n`CheckedClaim` carries its own `AssuranceClass`, and ADR-0017 / docs/03 §6.2 keep\nCHECKED_CERTIFICATE and TRUSTED_SOLVER apart so a theory-lemma-backed refutation is not rendered\nas a fully replayed one. The wire's `validation_basis` has exactly those two members, so the SMT\narm *reads* the claim's class rather than hard-coding `checked-certificate`.\n\n`proof_status` names the **kernel crate**, not `service:continuumd-verifier` — crediting the\ndaemon with a check it routed rather than ran is the one thing INV-004 is about. `values` still\nnames the daemon, because that re-derivation is the daemon's.\n\n### Flag candidate (recorded, not squeezed)\n\n*Which* outcome occurred and *which* checker reached it both travel; the kernel's reason *within*\n`Rejected` does not. `Fault::detail` is a non-interpolated `&'static str` (`rule\nenvelope.no_prose`), and the typed channel — `Error.data`, \"shape determined by `code`\" — is\nabsent daemon-wide because no schema is declared for any code (`daemon/result.rs:22-27`, `rule\nencoding.opaque_payloads`, IDL open item 3). Transcribing four kernels' `Rejection` enums into\ndaemon-side statics was declined: a second authority over vocabularies those crates own, which is\nwhat continuum-certificate itself refuses (\"there is no unified claim accessor\"). A client learns\n*continuum-kernel-core rejected this certificate*, not *TrailingBytes{extra:1}*. Closing it needs\na declared `data` shape for `CertificateRejected` — a wire addition, hence the flag discipline.\nPre-existing daemon-wide gap, not opened here.\n\nAlso declined: promoting `Unsupported` to `ClaimStatus::Inconclusive`. `Inconclusive` sits\n*below* `Observed`, so an honest \"I cannot answer\" over a node holding anything higher would\nreturn `StatusConflict` — a failed write reported in place of the verdict the checker gave. Same\nposture as the redaction lane.\n\n(2/2 follows: clause->test map, counts, gates, INV-004, concerns.)"}	blake3:rgRFqaxgw6gQ-Uh6Yw-EKCSTxJppoqlkxWzj4gDnv9E
1785723105429860	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyggo		item.comment	bn-dtg61	{"body":"bn-dtg61 report 2/2 — tests, counts, gates, concerns. (1/2 has the three audits.)\n\n## 4. Clause -> test map\n\nAll via `Daemon::dispatch`, in `crates/continuumd/tests/daemon_evidence.rs`. Certificates are\nreal: continuum-engine-reference explores Die Hard and emits the CONTCERT wire form (the\n`certificate_kernel_differential.rs` recipe), so producer and checker share no code. Each test\nalso asserts `check_certificate` on the *same bytes* first, holding the daemon to relaying rather\nthan to agreeing with the test's guess.\n\n- real CONTCERT -> `validated`, `checked-certificate`, `established`/`validated`, history names\n  service+basis, `proof_status` = continuum-kernel-core:\n  `a_real_certificate_is_validated_by_the_kernel_that_owns_its_family`\n- mutated -> `CertificateRejected`, detail names the kernel, nothing promoted, and a green\n  certificate under the same fixture still verifies:\n  `a_mutated_certificate_is_rejected_because_the_kernel_rejected_it`\n- kernel `Unsupported` -> ok + inconclusive + `InconclusiveReason::Unsupported` (explicitly not\n  the redaction lane's `InsufficientTelemetry`), `trusted-solver`, nothing promoted:\n  `a_certificate_naming_a_contract_the_kernel_does_not_implement_is_typed_inconclusive`\n- unroutable typed, never `CertificateRejected`, `verdict` null — both `UnknownFamily` and\n  `TooShortForMagic`: `bytes_no_checker_owns_are_typed_as_unroutable_and_never_as_a_verdict`\n- one code two details: `the_two_routing_faults_are_one_code_and_two_details`\n- absent artifact still `InsufficientEvidence`:\n  `a_certificate_class_node_whose_artifact_is_absent_is_still_insufficient_evidence`\n- **bn-3sypm's negative held live** — a *successful* verify appends no edge, no receipt node, no\n  `EdgePublished` event, no `artifacts`: `verifying_a_certificate_appends_no_edge_of_its_own`\n\n**One pre-existing test changed**, the one this delivery falsifies:\n`a_class_whose_independent_checker_has_not_shipped_is_never_promoted` used\n`EvidenceKind::Certificate` to assert that lane unreachable. It now reaches a checker, so the\nclass moved to `EvidenceKind::Inductive` (Lean, still out of process). Name, structure and claim\nunchanged; the reason is in the test and cites bn-dtg61. Module-doc item 4 updated to match. No\nother pre-existing test touched; no count assertion moved.\n\n## 5. Counts and gates\n\nWorkspace tests **2,196 -> 2,203** (+7, all new), 0 failures. The bone's stated 2,164 predates\nbn-1mhcr and bn-3tz60 landing on this branch; I measured 2,196 by stashing this branch's changes\nand re-running. fmt clean; `clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings` clean;\n`cargo test -p continuumd -p continuum-certificate --locked` green; **`just check` exit 0**.\nFiles: continuumd/Cargo.toml, daemon/evidence.rs, tests/daemon_evidence.rs,\ntools/governance/dependency-rationale.toml, Cargo.lock. **No dossier edit**, so no regen;\nvalidation-results.json untouched.\n\n## 6. INV-004 / bn-bou09 concern 5\n\nClosed: the crate has a consumer. `validated` is reachable by exactly one path, through a crate\nthat cannot link search, whose `CheckedClaim::new` is `pub(crate)` per kernel so this process\ncannot fabricate a `Verified`, and which shares no decoder with the producing engine. I did\n**not** add bn-dtg61 to plan.md:319's delivered annotation — no dossier line claims the\nno-consumer concern, so per the bone's item 5 this comment is the record; the heading is already\n`satisfied` and extending its bone list is the lead's call at close.\n\n## 7. Concerns\n\n1. The `Rejection`-reason gap is the one real flag candidate; worth a bone if a client needs\n   \"truncated at field X\" vs \"trailing bytes\" without holding the artifact.\n2. **SAT / SMT / temporal arms are wired but untested end-to-end through the daemon.** All four\n   families route and all twelve verdict arms are written out (a fifth kernel or a fourth verdict\n   arm is a compile error, not a silent default), but only CONTCERT has an in-tree fixture —\n   nothing emits LRAT, Alethe or ranking witnesses. continuum-certificate's `family_routing.rs`\n   covers all four at the composition layer; untested is the *daemon's* projection of the other\n   three, specifically the SMT `TRUSTED_SOLVER` basis arm, the one that differs from its siblings.\n3. `Unsupported` promotes nothing, so such a node sits at `proposed` forever with only the\n   response carrying the fact; see the lattice-regression reason above.\n4. `evidence.verify` publishes no artifact but does write a status — already true before this\n   bone; noted because this lane makes that write reach `validated`, the strongest status a\n   client-triggered operation can now cause."}	blake3:U55tN5AvgGg6ZQMWTFao4VOA5YstlklLNe9irGeWsHg
1785723200987703	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEygwo		item.create	bn-1dj05	{"description":"# Context\nbn-dtg61's verdict-fidelity audit: all four continuum-certificate outcomes reach the wire typed, but the kernel's reason WITHIN Rejected does not travel — Fault.detail is a non-interpolated &'static str and Error.data is absent daemon-wide (no code declares a data schema). Closing it is a wire addition (an Error.data schema for CertificateRejected, or a typed field), i.e. flag-discipline territory.\n\n# The work\nRecord the flag in RFC 0026's flags preamble per the F15-F18 ledger convention (candidate designation, what it would add, what it waits on — likely the next protocol-minor bundle), with the reference to bn-dtg61's audit. If the next bump takes it: the schema, the codec, the conformance vectors, and the CLI rendering ride together.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Flag recorded in RFC 0026's ledger with direction; bone closeable once recorded (implementation rides a future bump).","kind":"task","labels":["protocol","spec-debt","req:phase-a"],"size":"xs","title":"Flag: kernel rejection reasons do not travel — Error.data schema for CertificateRejected (bn-dtg61 candidate)"}	blake3:-VGkB8LFlctjtfaCkIXL3f-7xN0SLXHkL27z5XgwPOE
1785723201034915	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyhAo		item.comment	bn-dtg61	{"body":"Landed @ 520c82ac0276. Lead re-verified post-sync: continuumd + continuum-certificate green (2,203), boundaries 27-edge pass, GOV-1 twelve/twelve.\n\nRATIFIED. (1) The §20 audit's reading — the crate's one rule constrains ITS dependencies, not its dependents, with the checker walking outward so the new edge cannot enter the forbidden closure; direction already fixed from the other end by KCOV-06. (2) Identity re-derivation on BOTH lanes before the kernel — a kernel cannot see a misfiling; skipping it would have reopened the retargeting attack the observation lane refuses. Exactly right. (3) The verdict mapping with the DECISIVE observation that verdict-is-null-on-error makes ok+inconclusive+InconclusiveReason::Unsupported INV-008's only honest channel for Unsupported; Unroutable→EpochUnsupported chosen with reasons against the two tempting alternatives. (4) The SMT assurance-class non-flattening (reading the claim's own AssuranceClass rather than hard-coding) — ADR-0017 held at the wire. (5) Relaying held to the composition (every daemon test asserts check_certificate on the same bytes first). (6) bn-3sypm's verify-appends-nothing negative held live. (7) The pre-existing test's class swap (Certificate→Inductive) preserves the claim with the reason in-test — the correct handling of a test whose fixture the delivery obsoleted.\n\nFLAG CANDIDATE ROUTED: bn-<next comment> records the Rejected-reason-does-not-travel gap in RFC 0026's ledger. SMT TRUSTED_SOLVER arm's untested-through-daemon status accepted as honest — nothing in-tree emits Alethe; the daemon-level test arrives with the producer (Phase D).\n\nbn-bou09's no-consumer concern is CLOSED — the certificate checker now has its daemon consumer with the kernel vocabulary intact end to end."}	blake3:ErYH5ECR4UeVJDIrtz1no7o5NwDQFvVBAp8blf9yioU
1785723201067255	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyhQo		item.move	bn-dtg61	{"state":"done"}	blake3:TG-gCvZ1aNMrsxezX2fiRbWWruVS5pEj3JEzg4htMo0
1785723211285854	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyhgo		item.comment	bn-dtg61	{"body":"Flag bone: bn-1dj05."}	blake3:NYgZGfLtGrKUmCI1HZhjxwtAOaXpJEJeC0F67lXqy9o
1785724347268809	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyhwo		item.comment	bn-28jj	{"body":"PR-11 / IMPL-04 delivered in-workspace (61080c3). `just check` exit 0; 2,261 workspace tests, 0 failures.\n\n**The omission model.** `crates/continuum-context/src/omission.rs`: the closed five-member `OmissionReason` (the IDL's and the schema's tokens, in schema order), and a record shape in which the schema's two `if`/`then` conditionals on `expandable` are *unrepresentable* rather than validated. `Retrievability` has two arms: the expandable one carries the `ExpansionQuery`, so a record silent about how it is retrieved has no spelling (INV-007's second half); the irretrievable one carries `IrretrievableReason`, a two-member type holding exactly the `redaction`/`unsupported` the schema admits under `expandable: false`, so a `budget` omission declared irretrievable has no spelling either. There is no constructor taking a bool and a reason independently, which is why there is no validation to forget. `kind` is narrowed from the schema's free string to the closed eleven-member `SelectionKind` — inside what the schema admits, so a pack written here still validates — on IMPL-03's own reading of `SourceRef`'s `file`: a partition keyed by free-form phrases (the shipped example writes \"observer-independent causal predecessor\") cannot be checked for the exactness RFC 0028 demands of it, and a free-string `kind` is a place source-derived prose could reach the artifact (INV-016). Recorded as a documented reading, not a schema edit.\n\n**The closed-accounting argument.** `accounting.rs` starts from the candidate set and records one *disposition* per candidate; `Accounting::close` refuses to produce a `ClosedAccounting` while any candidate has none, and the manifest is **derived** from the dispositions rather than supplied beside them. So \"candidate set = selection + Σ manifest counts, partitioned exactly by kind and reason\" is a property of the only constructor there is — not arithmetic a compiler could get wrong in three places and notice in one. That is `continuum-task`'s budget `MeterSet` discipline (one ledger both halves are read off) applied to the thing INV-007 is about. `ClosedAccounting::reconcile` is the independent leg RFC 0028's Validation section asks for: it recomputes the candidate count from the two *published* halves and compares it against the size the candidate set declared before any disposition existed — two numbers from different places, so agreement is evidence rather than a tautology (`EvidenceBook::reconcile`'s own argument). Anti-vacuity is explicit rather than implied: an undispositioned candidate, a doubly-dispositioned one, a selection containing a non-candidate, and RFC 0028's two named mutants (a shrunk manifest count, an inflated one) are each exercised as a refusal. The same conservation law is then asserted *over the wire*: expanding one anchor at depth 1 and at depth 2 moves items between the pack and the manifest and changes neither `|selected| + Σ counts` nor the membership — expanding further creates nothing, expanding less loses nothing.\n\n**Handle semantics.** Content-derived per ADR-0013: `ExpansionHandle::derive` hashes the ID5 canonical encoding of `{anchor, depth, parent, relation}` and spells the digest as a `ctx_` identity. Two consequences, both tested. RFC 0028's idempotency rule (\"a replay with a byte-identical canonical request MUST return the same `ctx_*`\") holds *without the ledger* — two calls under two **different** idempotency keys, which the ledger therefore cannot collapse, return one handle and byte-identical packs. And RFC 0027 C2 (\"two expansions selecting the same items are distinct artifacts answering different questions\") holds because every term of the question, plus the parent the question does not carry, moves the digest. That is the sense in which G0-DX-01's expansion handles are **exact**: the `ctx_*` a parent's manifest promises in `Omission.recoverable_by` is derivable from the record alone, and `the_handle_the_manifest_promised_is_the_handle_the_expansion_returns` follows the promise and gets that pack (with a different-question anti-vacuity leg beside it). The parent is in the preimage and not in `question`, because RFC 0028 fixes `question` as the triple and two parents' expansions along one triple are two packs.\n\n*(continued in the next comment: the daemon family, the CLI flip, count moves, gates, and concerns.)*"}	blake3:FS0z2bz3b2r3NX6HwADDGFWrpuhoaIerCpk1DpuJX_A
1785724347310216	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyiAo		item.comment	bn-28jj	{"body":"**bn-28jj, part 2 of 2** (continues the delivery note above).\n\n**The daemon's context family.** `codec::operations::decode_arguments` implements both context request shapes; `daemon/context.rs` serves the namespace. `context.expand` resolves the query against a **registered pack** and answers with the child pack and the residual manifest — the groups reachable from what it selected and beyond the depth asked for — while the envelope's `omissions[]` carries the same records reason for reason and record for record (RFC 0028: \"the wire projection of the same facts\"). There is no arm of the handler that answers manifest-free. `context.compile` decodes and is refused `UnsupportedSemanticFeature` (the `observe.classify`/`observe.result` precedent): RFC 0028's ten-stage compiler is wired nowhere in this workspace, and a compiled-looking pack with no compiler behind it is the degradation `rule errors.unsupported_surface` names. Every row of RFC 0028's typed-outcome table is the code it says and each is tested: unheld pack → `CapabilityDenied` (X2, never a distinguishable not-found); anchor that does not resolve in the *published parent* → `MalformedRequest`, identically for five hostile spellings (the anchor is compared for exact equality against the pack's own advertised anchors and nothing else — INV-016's closed-vocabulary shape, with a second constant detail for anchors no canonical identifier can spell, and the choice between the two decided by the caller's own bytes); relation undefined for a resolvable anchor → `UnsupportedSemanticFeature`; purged anchor → **success** with the `redaction` record and the parent's own `redactions[]` stub (\"an error carries no manifest and no commitment\"); exhausted relation → success with an empty manifest, the assertion of completeness; over-budget → `BudgetExhausted` with a typed `non_resumable_reason` (SD-13) and nothing published. The child document is derived, not compiled: `snapshot`/`intent`/`semantic_epoch` and five more keys are copied verbatim, a parent missing one is refused rather than defaulted, and `guarantees` is `[]` because C5 forbids inheritance and C1 forbids echoing an unchecked claim.\n\n**Snapshot staleness, and the two tests it deliberately does not run.** `task.resume` runs three (envelope-disagreement, held/sealed, current-in-lineage). Only the first applies here: a continuation is *resumed* into the world it pinned, but a pack is *read* — \"the pack is readable as history\" — so refusing to expand one because its snapshot was superseded would make the history the pack exists to explain unreadable, and RFC 0028's table has no such row. What is a real disagreement is an envelope naming one snapshot and a pack stated against another; that is `StaleSnapshot`, and `rule errors.common` admits it \"where a snapshot is named\", which there it literally was. Both directions tested.\n\n**CLI flip.** bn-3tz60's three refusal tests in `tests/context_expand.rs` are replaced by live counterparts over a real frame and a real admitted answer: the family is registered and one pack is seeded through `DaemonState::put_context_pack`. The success renderer that bone shipped renderer-first now runs end to end (the child's two spans and its own manifest are readable in the JSON envelope), the `omissions` key is still asserted present on both arms, and one refusal test is kept on its new cause (an anchor the pack does not carry). Its unit-level success test is unchanged beside them — it exercises an omission with a `recoverable_by` handle that a completed expansion has no occasion to carry. The fixture's relation moved `causal_predecessors` → `source_span`, because `SelectedItem`'s only typed constructors are `SourceRef`'s and `ModelActionRef`'s (IMPL-03) and I did not widen them; the request shape exercised is identical.\n\n**Count-assertion moves** (count-assertions-force-visibility, all citing this bone): landed operations 26 → 28 in `inv016_untrusted_source_evidence.rs` (test renamed `exactly_twenty_eight_…`, expected list +2, the file's 26/47 prose → 28/45) and in `codec_canonical_cbor.rs` (`served`); `inv003_no_prose_only_evidence.rs`'s freshness tripwire follows the rename. The INV-016 free-text inventory gains the three positions the two context requests declare (`ContextExpandRequest.anchor`, `ContextCompileRequest.{question,guarantees}`), with a comment naming which of leg 1's three shapes each is and pointing at the probes, which are homed in `daemon_context_operations.rs` with the family they exercise. bn-1mhcr's 48 goldens are **unchanged**: the body vectors are synthesized from the registry (one per namespace, `context.compile` already among them), so they do not move when a family lands. `idl_conformance.rs` counts registry rows, not landed families — untouched.\n\n**Counts.** +65 tests: 43 unit in `continuum-context` (omission 12, expansion 11, accounting 9, pack 11), 21 in the new `crates/continuumd/tests/daemon_context_operations.rs`, +1 in the CLI's flipped file. Workspace 2,261 passing / 0 failing. The brief's 2,164 baseline is stale relative to my branch point (bn-1mhcr and bn-3tz60 landed after it was written); the pre-bone total on this base was 2,196.\n\n**Gates.** `cargo fmt --all --check`, `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings`, `cargo test --workspace --locked`, and full `just check` (boundaries, covenant, governance ×5, dossier, Lean) all exit 0. Dossier regenerated: `PR-11-IMPL-04` flips `active` → `satisfied`, active requirements 677 → 676. `validation-results.json` untouched.\n\n*(concerns in the next comment.)*"}	blake3:it3VKMGFE8PFGfrRyukwWkoJqZxUXiuPBb28BxLYb4U
1785724347341916	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyiQo		item.comment	bn-28jj	{"body":"**bn-28jj, part 3 of 3 — concerns / for the lead.**\n\n1. **Two forced one-liners outside my fence.** `src/transport/mod.rs`'s `encode_arguments_in` match is exhaustive over `Arguments`, so two new variants force two arms there (bn-1h158's file — minimal, expect a rebase). `daemon/mod.rs` gains one `pub mod context;`.\n2. **New dependency edges.** `continuumd → continuum-context` (normal) and `continuum-cli → continuum-context` (dev, for the CLI fixture's pack). `dependency-rationale.toml` gains one entry classed **`engine`** — the class is a judgement: a pack is an explanation, \"publishing a pack MUST NOT change any claim's status\" (INV-015), the crate is linked by no certificate checker, and RFC 0028 C6 requires every pack guarantee to be re-checked by a checker independent of the compiler that claimed it. The entry records for the reviewer what that class does not discount: the omission manifest is a transparency claim a consumer has no second reading of. Flag if you want it at `trusted-checking-base`.\n3. **Two IDL/schema readings documented rather than edited** (per the wire-audit rule — implementing declared operations is conformance, no F-flag): (a) `omissions[].kind` narrowed to the closed `SelectionKind`, which the shipped `context-pack.example.json` diverges from with a free-form phrase — the example is already recorded as non-conformant in RFC 0028 correction 2 for a different field, and this is a second instance for the schema/example sweep, not an edit I made; (b) `content_budget.bytes` is written as the **ceiling in force** (the request's when it names one, else the parent's), not a measurement of the bytes written — the schema calls it \"byte budget of the pack\" while the ratified FR-01 metric sums it over a pack family, which reads more like actual cost. Both readings are stated in `pack.rs`'s module doc. If the sweep decides `bytes` is a measurement, the change is one line here.\n4. **`depth: 0` is `MalformedRequest`** — the IDL types it `U32 optional`, absence means 1, and neither RFC gives zero a meaning. Recorded as this bone's reading in `expansion.rs`.\n5. **Declined, deliberately.** The child pack is **not published into the reference store**: a store handle is the content identity of the bytes and a `ctx_*` is the identity of its question, and reconciling the two spellings is a pack-assembly decision (IMPL-03 already declined whole-pack assembly for the same reason), so the pack travels on the wire and the envelope names no `ArtifactRef`. A byte-budget **packer** — RFC 0028's second branch, publishing a smaller child that records the shortfall — is IMPL-06's, so an over-budget expansion takes the first branch and publishes nothing. Typed construction for the nine other selection kinds stays IMPL-01/02/05/06's: an expansion carries items of any kind and constructs items of none. And the compiler that would populate a pack's groups in production is unbuilt, so a deployment registers packs out of band through `DaemonState::put_context_pack` — the surface content staging and model registration already use (IDL §7). When the compiler lands it writes through the same surface and `daemon/context.rs` does not change.\n6. **`inv006_replay_stability_evidence.rs`'s prose counts** (\"25-variant enum\", \"47 of the 73\") were already stale at 26/47 before this bone and are doc comments rather than assertions; I left them untouched rather than widen the blast radius. Its actual test (`program.replay` is declared-but-undecodable) is unaffected and still true."}	blake3:e44IJA_Fr9TtRJNAVV93EK3_DV0AK30ULVf8YHmx5oY
1785724487034223	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyigo		item.create	bn-6ekf7	{"description":"# Context — two schema readings bn-28jj documented rather than edited (wire-audit rule)\n1. omissions[].kind narrowed from the schema's free string to the closed SelectionKind vocabulary — the shipped context-pack.example.json diverges from this reading (a second instance for the schema/example sweep; the W7 pinning-test precedent applies if the example is fixed).\n2. content_budget.bytes written as the CEILING IN FORCE rather than a measurement — the ratified FR-01 summing rule may want the other way.\n\n# The work\nDecide each reading against the schema/RFC 0028/FR-01 texts: fix the example (epoch-governed edit discipline) and/or bless the readings in the schema's own prose; align crates/continuum-context's documentation with the outcome.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Both readings dispositioned with text; example conformant or reading revised; just check green.","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","req:phase-a","spec-debt"],"size":"xs","title":"Context Pack schema readings — example divergence on omissions[].kind + content_budget.bytes semantics (bn-28jj)"}	blake3:gRgcuIUp8pjTcY-_sZUSCIrsz5z3uPL4CAuysOh9WU8
1785724487072328	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyiwo		item.comment	bn-28jj	{"body":"Landed @ 8b0b130736ca. Lead re-verified post-sync: continuumd/context/cli suites green with --locked after a union resolve of the Cargo manifests (bn-dtg61's certificate edge + this bone's context edge both present, lock coherent), boundaries and GOV-1 pass, workspace 2,261.\n\nRATIFIED. (1) Unrepresentable-over-validated for the schema's conditionals — Retrievability's arms carrying exactly what each admits is the strongest reading of a JSON-schema if/then. (2) Closed accounting as a CONSTRUCTOR property (candidates = selection + Σ counts with no other way to build a manifest) with reconcile recomputed from the published halves — independent, not tautological; RFC 0028's two named mutants exercised as refusals. (3) Handle derivation over the ID5 canonical question — idempotency WITHOUT the ledger and C2 by construction; DX-01's 'exact expansion handles' pass condition now holds end to end (the ctx_* promised in recoverable_by is the one returned). (4) The CLI flip preserving one refusal test on its new cause. (5) Count moves bone-cited; bn-1mhcr's goldens correctly unmoved (registry-synthesized).\n\nROUTED: the two schema readings → bn-<next comment> (example divergence + content_budget.bytes semantics). The engine classification for the new edges accepted as argued (Context Packs assemble evidence; they check nothing) — revisit only if the tier table disagrees. Forced one-liners in sibling files resolved cleanly at sync (union on the manifests).\n\nPR-11 IMPL-04 satisfied via bullet annotation. The context family is live: 28 landed operations."}	blake3:LqWic6F3h-9a5sgJajpSiXrAOajfpKMFBeUEqkHvWRg
1785724487103193	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyjAo		item.move	bn-28jj	{"state":"done"}	blake3:9PsvrjZJnpcAFiR-SwYOGgLkm_-ZLlAeNTdzas00cws
