1785867000257293	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAQ		item.comment	bn-6ekf7	{"body":"Both readings dispositioned; no schema shape changed. just check exits 0 (fmt, clippy -D warnings, dossier, boundaries, covenant, governance, lean all pass); cargo test -p continuum-context --locked: 65 unit + 7 integration + 1 doctest = 73 passed, 0 failed.\n\nREADING 1 — omissions[].kind: RATIFIED as the closed SelectionKind vocabulary (mirrors selected[].kind), not a free descriptive string.\n- Schema: \"the candidate set equals the selection plus the sum of the manifest counts, partitioned exactly by kind and reason\" (omissions description) — a partition keyed by free-form phrases cannot be checked for exactness.\n- RFC 0028 \"Omission manifest\": \"the partition of the unselected candidates by kind and reason is exact.\"\n- crates/continuum-context::omission already implements this (OmissionRecord.kind: SelectionKind), for the same reason plus rule ordering.deterministic and INV-016.\n- The shipped example diverged: \"kind\": \"observer-independent causal predecessor\" is not a SelectionKind member. Fixed to \"kind\": \"event\" — the 196 omitted items behind e_ack are its causal-predecessor events (RFC 0028 correction 15 already calls them \"196 noise events\"). No Rust test pins the example's bytes (checked: only notes/plan/tools/validate_dossier.py validates it, schema-conformance only, and the current schema still types kind as a string so both old and new values validate) — no W7-style pin flip was needed.\n- Recorded as RFC 0028 correction 16, citing bn-6ekf7. Schema's omissions[].kind gained a description blessing the reading (prose only).\n- CANDIDATE SHAPE CHANGE for the lead / schema sweep: omissions[].kind should become a closed enum mirroring selected[].kind, exactly as RFC 0028 F5/correction 2 already did for expansions[].relation (paid by bn-1lfek). Raised as new flag RFC 0028 F13. Not made here per the wire-audit/shape-change discipline.\n\nREADING 2 — content_budget.bytes: RATIFIED as the pack's own MEASURED byte size (post-packing), not \"the ceiling in force.\" bn-28jj's crates/continuum-context::pack reading was the wrong way.\n- RFC 0028 \"Budgets and packing\": \"Budget is not key material... consumed budget is Cost provenance\" — ties the pack's recorded budget to the IDL's Cost (actual spend), not Budget/OutputPolicy (the requested ceiling).\n- F4's tie-in reinforces it: content_budget.tokens's tokenizer identity is recorded \"as Cost.tokenizer_id provenance,\" directly mapping content_budget's shape onto Cost's (bytes, tokens, tokenizer_id all mirror Cost's fields, not Budget's or OutputPolicy's).\n- Decisive: content_budget.nodes IS documented as a ceiling (\"Graph-node ceiling of the IDL's OutputPolicy.max_nodes\") — the schema's own asymmetry — while bytes is not tied to OutputPolicy.max_bytes anywhere; it's tied to \"the graded cost denominator of the ratified FR-01 sentence.\"\n- FR-01 (plan §24.5 quote-id=context-compilation-win-margin, research/32): \"...delivering at least a 10x median per-task reduction in context bytes counting every expansion...\" RFC 0028's \"Expansion accounting\" says this is graded by *summing* content_budget.bytes over a pack family. Summing a requested ceiling (typically a fixed config value on both conditions) cannot show any \"reduction\" and makes the ratified metric uncomputable; only a per-pack measurement does.\n- Recorded as RFC 0028 correction 17, citing bn-6ekf7. Schema's content_budget.bytes description sharpened (prose only) to state the measurement reading and the nodes/bytes asymmetry explicitly.\n- No shape change needed here (bytes was always typed integer; only the semantics were ambiguous/misread).\n- Doc-only fix applied in crates/continuum-context::pack (module doc \"three keys\" bullet, ChildPack.budget_bytes field doc, budget_bytes_of doc): states the ratified measurement reading, and honestly flags that this crate does not yet compute it — assembling the true post-packing size is PR-11/IMPL-06's undelivered bullet, so ChildPack still threads an inherited/requested ceiling through content_budget.bytes as an interim placeholder pending IMPL-06, checked (not enforced) via ChildPack::within. No behavior changed.\n- FOLLOW-UP FOR THE LEAD (not a shape change, a behavior gap, out of scope here per the task's doc-comments-only instruction): crates/continuumd/src/daemon/context.rs's `ceiling()` helper and context::expand handler implement the same \"ceiling in force\" behavior end-to-end (reads envelope.budget.bytes else the parent's content_budget.bytes as a ceiling, writes it back into the child's content_budget.bytes, checks ChildPack::within against it). That crate was in bn-28jj's delivered scope too but is outside crates/continuum-context, so it was not touched by this bone. When IMPL-06 lands real byte packing, both continuum-context::pack and continuumd::daemon::context need to start writing the child's actual measured size into content_budget.bytes instead of the inherited ceiling.\n\nFiles touched: crates/continuum-context/src/omission.rs, crates/continuum-context/src/pack.rs, notes/plan/rfcs/0028-context-pack-format.md, notes/plan/schemas/context-pack.schema.json, notes/plan/schemas/examples/context-pack.example.json. Committed in-workspace as fix: context-pack schema readings dispositioned — omissions[].kind + content_budget.bytes (bn-6ekf7). notes/plan/tools/generate_traceability.py was run; no diff (RFC/schema prose doesn't feed traceability). Bone left in doing state; not merged, not closed, not pushed."}	blake3:11lYM1e96bqHhFmZJNi-jo3WyEEW5sISMZe_WD-spQs
