1785883885946220	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEymgs		item.comment	bn-b8ru	{"body":"PR-12 / IMPL-01 (exact equality) delivered in workspace bn-b8ru, committed (not merged). just check exits 0 in-workspace (fmt, clippy -D warnings, full workspace test suite, crate boundaries, kernel covenant, governance x6, dossier, Lean) — 128 continuum-semantic-diff crate tests, all green.\n\nWHAT \"EXACT EQUALITY\" MEANS, DERIVED FROM THE RFC TEXT (not the two-word bullet title):\nThe bullet carries no prose of its own in START_HERE, unlike its five siblings. Read against RFC 0031's normative text rather than guessed from the title, \"exact equality\" is the RFC's own \"unchanged shortcut\" (\"Inputs, preconditions, and determinism\": \"If before_intent and after_intent are the same in_* identity, intent_changes MUST be empty. Identity is over the canonical encoding of semantic content (RFC 0037), so equal identity entails every field unchanged. The converse does not hold: distinct identities MUST be classified field by field and MUST NOT be reported as a whole-contract change.\"). I cross-walked this against plan §5.3's ten-item list, whose first nine are the seven G3 directional dimensions plus abstraction merge/split (outside PR-12's six bullets) and \"unsupported semantic change\" (the fail-closed rule every sibling already implements); the tenth and last is \"unchanged intent\" — the one item with no directional order, no per-field unit, and no PR-12 sibling bone. \"Exact equality\" = \"unchanged intent\" under PR-12's own vocabulary, and this shortcut paragraph is the only place in the dossier that gives it a normative rule. This is also R2's \"otherwise\" branch (\"RFC 0037's canonical contract encoding otherwise\", as opposed to CPNF-1 for property ASTs) taken at the coarsest possible unit: the whole contract.\n\nWHAT WAS BUILT — crates/continuum-semantic-diff/src/equality.rs:\n- `Equality` (Unchanged/Distinct, 2 variants) and `classify(before: &IntentIdentity, after: &IntentIdentity) -> Equality`: total comparison over continuum_intent::contract::IntentIdentity — the ID1/ID2 canonical preimage bytes.\n- `unchanged_classification(before, after) -> Option<[ClassificationRecord; 15]>`: Some(...) only when the shortcut fires, giving one Relation::Unchanged record per PolicyField — the complete-classification input PolicyTable::verdict itself demands (its own doc: \"a verdict is computed from a complete classification, so the caller supplies the unchanged records explicitly\"). This is NOT correction 14's mistake repeated: it's verdict-input, not the wire intent_changes[] shape, which stays empty on the wire either way — that emission choice belongs to whichever later bone assembles the diff_* artifact, same gap bounds/observers already leave for their own one-field records.\n- No per-field logic, no directions guessed when Distinct — that's the other five bullets' job (property AST edit / assumption add-remove still open; bound change / observer event change / fault-fairness-assurance already landed).\n\nJUDGMENT CALL, ANNOTATED RATHER THAN SILENTLY DECIDED:\nThe shortcut's own sentence says \"the same in_* identity\", and in_* is literally a hash digest (IntentContract::mint_intent_id). A literal reading could compare the minted wire strings. I compare IntentIdentity (canonical bytes) instead, because ADR-0013 — this crate's own upstream discipline — says identity is canonical bytes and a digest only indexes/partitions, collisions resolve by exact comparison; IntentContract::identity() is itself typed &IntentIdentity for exactly this reason. I read this as RFC 0031 using \"identity\" for the semantic content the handle names (its very next sentence glosses \"Identity is over the canonical encoding of semantic content\"), not as a contradiction — but flagging it since a literal four-character reading of \"in_*\" could go the other way. Full reasoning is in the module doc (\"Why canonical bytes, not the in_* handle string\").\n\nEVIDENCE — tests/pr12_impl01_exact_equality_evidence.rs (10 tests) + 6 unit tests in src/equality.rs, all against the real replicated-register-contract.json fixture, no hand-authored contracts, all mutations bounded/linear .replacen or a typed-tree reformat (never raw-text edits, which would corrupt the fixture's own in-preimage free-text fields like optimization.non_vacuity):\n- Positive: fixture decoded twice closes into PolicyTable::verdict's Allow under two of the fixture's own `locked` verbs (properties, completion_policy) — the load-bearing case, not an all-unlocked softball.\n- Adversarial (dangerous direction — near-identical documents that must NOT read as Unchanged): a policy-only edit (bounds' verb no-decrease -> unlocked, zero content fields touched) classifies Distinct, per RFC 0037 ID2's explicit \"the policy table, policy_reviewers ... are in the preimage\" — this is the sharpest disguise case for this module, since a plausible bug is to treat policy as \"governance metadata\" the way name genuinely is.\n- Adversarial (safe direction — cosmetic variants that must still read as Unchanged): name-only edit (ID2 exclusion) and a full reorder/repad of the whole document via the typed Json tree (adapted from continuum-intent's own pr4_exit_evidence.rs technique, preserving AST/condition array order) both classify Unchanged.\n- Anti-vacuity, both directions: a mutant that drops policy/policy_reviewers from the comparison wrongly calls the policy-only edit Unchanged (dangerous-direction bug, shown getting it wrong); a mutant that compares raw un-canonicalized input bytes wrongly calls the reformatted document Distinct (R2's explicit \"Textual match ... MUST NOT be used\", shown getting it wrong the other way).\n- schema_epoch (also named by ID2) is structurally checked present in the preimage rather than compared live: IntentContract::decode's W10 header check refuses any schema_epoch other than the schema's own const before identity is ever computed, so a two-value comparison isn't exercisable through the public API today — noted rather than faked.\n- Determinism: two independent calls produce byte-for-byte-equal record sets.\n\nlib.rs: added only `pub mod equality;` in alphabetical position plus one short addendum paragraph after the existing shared block (matching the bounds/faults precedent of appending rather than editing shared prose, to avoid the three-way-conflict hazard the brief warned about).\n\nSTART_HERE: bullet at (now) line 277 annotated in the style of its siblings; notes/plan/tools/generate_traceability.py regenerated — the requirement's status flipped from active to satisfied in PLAN_REQUIREMENTS.json/PLAN_BONE_TRACEABILITY.md.\n\nReleasing claims now."}	blake3:01o6GMeEz1Qn9qVw_6oSZ8CHLN23u6vNa_czzLZX6FY
1785884001700169	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEymws		item.comment	bn-b8ru	{"body":"Lead: merged @ epoch ae77d6d70f84. equality.rs = RFC 0031's whole-contract unchanged shortcut over canonical ID1/ID2 preimage bytes (ADR-0013), bridged into the fifteen Relation::Unchanged ClassificationRecords PolicyTable::verdict requires — correctly distinct from correction 14's wire intent_changes[] which stays empty either way. The flagged judgment call (canonical bytes vs literal in_* handle string) is RATIFIED: RFC 0031's own gloss makes identity the canonical-encoding content, and comparing preimages keeps the classifier independent of the hash function — the right side of the content-identity boundary. Cross-walk to plan §5.3's tenth bullet (unchanged intent) recorded. Adversarial sweep polarity correct: policy-only edit (ID2) → Distinct; name-only + document-reformat → Unchanged. Lead resolved the expected rebase contacts at merge (lib.rs addendum paragraphs kept both + separator restored; traceability keep-epoch + regen; 159 crate tests green post-combination). Closing."}	blake3:QR-pWxbdmYK7ZYdqNeJRjoZ2yXbE6VKxhByj4Z9v5pw
