1785887862649748	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEywQs		item.comment	bn-cho5	{"body":"PHASE-A-DEL-03 concurrency campaign delivered. All three acceptance criteria met; design decisions, coverage table, sanitizer status, and residuals follow.\n\nDESIGN DECISION 1 — loom: NOT adopted, with the honest reason. The daemon's concurrency is entirely capability-mediated and single-threaded-per-dispatch: `Daemon::dispatch` takes `&mut self` (crates/continuumd/src/daemon/mod.rs:384), the region calculus is `&mut` throughout, and neither continuumd nor continuum-task contains an atomic, a lock, or a thread. The one genuinely concurrent surface is continuum-workspace's `ReferenceStore` (`&self` + `Mutex<StoreState>`, `Sync`), whose concurrency is coarse-grained critical sections with the two-phase machine reified as VALUES (`StagedPublication` / `CommittedContent`) — so every interleaving at the semantic grain is a permutation of whole critical sections, which the deterministic sweeps enumerate exhaustively on one thread, no loom needed. Loom would model Mutex-acquisition orders of sections that are atomic by construction (decorative), and would be this workspace's first external dev-dependency (T12: audit entries for loom plus its transitive closure). The memory-model residual — that the Mutex really provides the atomicity the sweeps assume — is the TSan lane's job instead. Decision: no loom; the repo's own schedule-as-data discipline (continuum-task region/schedule.rs) is the coverage.\n\nCOVERAGE (AC-1) — deterministic schedule tests, both grains, all landed and green:\n- crates/continuum-workspace/tests/publication_schedule_matrix.rs (6 tests): CAS two-phase machine stepped through enumerated shuffle products. Publication races / duplicate coalescing: 3 identical publishers x all 1,680 interleavings -> one identity, no lost receipts, one summary across the space. INV-017 visibility: reader woven through 560 schedules, readable exactly from the index commit, monotone. GC vs the in-flight pin (4 weavings, fsck classifies the between-commits window as UnreachableContent and collection leaves it) + abandoned-residue reclamation (4 weavings). Byte-different collision under a total-collision identifier: 20 schedules, schedule-derived winner, typed IdentityCollision loser, both publishers win somewhere (anti-vacuity). Determinism: matrix renders byte-identical across two runs.\n- crates/continuumd/tests/task_lifecycle_schedule_matrix.rs (5 tests): daemon grain, dispatch order as the enumerated value. Commutation: dpor+proof lanes, all 20 interleavings -> byte-identical finished daemon, regions total, opened == declared. Cancellation: task.cancel woven at every position of the 2-lane shuffle (140 schedules) -> typed refusal + no scope before the task exists, INV-009 publication monotonicity through the whole schedule, surviving lane byte-identical to its unspliced baseline, regions total; >1 cancel status reached (anti-vacuity). Duplicate coalescing: duplicate verification.start woven at every position (140) -> same task_ content identity, no third task, no extra scope, finished daemon byte-identical to the unspliced fixed point; creator-swap, live-task, and cached-result lanes all reached. Restart boundaries: crash at every one of bn-3dr's nine CrashPoints at every lifecycle position (27 runs) -> recovery verdict Clean with zero defects/quarantine at every cell (no half-publication from any dispatch-boundary crash at any position), per-position durable-image step function (8 pre-handler images byte-identical, AfterHandler differs), and a restarted daemon re-runs the whole lane to the byte-identical cold record. Composes with, does not duplicate: dx14 matrices (single-lane phase sweeps; this adds the interleaving axis), g1/bn-3dr (single-dispatch step function; this sweeps boundary x position and adds the restart-re-run claim), dx13 (rendezvous-forced; this enumerates).\n\nDESIGN DECISION 2 — sanitizer lanes (AC-2): new `just sanitizers` recipe, deliberately NOT in `just check` (nightly -Zsanitizer vs the pinned 1.97.0, INV-014 — the default gate stays green on the pinned toolchain). ASan + TSan over -p continuum-workspace -p continuumd -p continuum-task with -Zbuild-std (instrumented std, so TSan has no uninstrumented-std false positives), --locked on the cargo lines (T12 locked-gates scanner sees and passes them). Anti-vacuity for the lane itself: each sanitizer first compiles and runs a canary with a known defect (heap overflow for ASan, data race for TSan) and the recipe FAILS unless the sanitizer catches it — a mis-spelled RUSTFLAGS cannot produce a green no-op. VERIFIED LOCALLY, actually run: nightly 1.98.0 (6bdf43094 2026-06-01), both canaries caught, then 106 test binaries / 1,708 tests passed across the two lanes, zero sanitizer findings. Residuals, typed honestly: (a) this machine's `nightly` floats — the recipe takes a toolchain argument (`just sanitizers nightly-2026-04-29`) and the recorded convention is to pin a dated nightly for a lane identity; (b) doctests excluded (--tests) — they run under the pinned gate, and rustdoc x build-std is not this lane's claim; (c) TSan over continuumd/continuum-task is trivially quiet (single-threaded by construction) — the meaningful TSan surface is continuum-workspace's threaded suites, which ran; (d) no CI exists in-tree (no .github/ anywhere), so per house practice the \"CI lane\" is the documented invocation in the Justfile header (the same posture as check_revision_delta's --require-base note); (e) one narrow waiver: the TSan canary links prebuilt std under -Cunsafe-allow-abi-mismatch=sanitizer (nightly now hard-errors otherwise) — sound because the canary's race lives entirely in its own instrumented crate; the suites never use the waiver.\n\nDESIGN DECISION 3 — seeded defect detection (AC-3): crates/continuumd/tests/task_lifecycle_mutation_campaign.rs (6 tests), the dx13_mutation_campaign pattern at the lifecycle grain. Checks written ONCE over a LifecycleUnderTest seam; the real daemon and a Faithful re-implementation control pass all three; each defect class is owned by a named check and each mutant is caught WITH ITS REASON STRING asserted, plus off-diagonal passes asserted (targeted, not indiscriminate): lost-update <- StaleRecordUpdate (budget update writes back the record it read at arrival, clobbering the interleaved commit) caught by check_no_lost_update; orphan-worker <- AbandonWorkerOnCancel (cancel answers, never drains) caught by check_no_orphan_worker; half-publication <- NameBeforeCommit (record names the artifact before its bytes are durable; the crash-shaped exit never flushes) caught by check_no_half_publication. src/ untouched — mutants are separate re-implementations, so there is nothing to revert and no window in which shipped code was wrong (house doctrine). A summary test pins fail-closed: every mutation caught by at least one check.\n\nSCOPE / PLAN: plan.md §21 Phase A bullet \"task/continuation lifecycle\" annotated (delivered: bn-3tz60, bn-cho5 — ...) in the workspace copy; bn-3tz60 (done) is the client half and these are the only two bones under req:phase-a-del-03. Traceability regenerated in-workspace: PHASE-A-DEL-03 now \"satisfied\" (registry 846, 664 active, all mapped).\n\nVERIFICATION: `just check` exit 0 from the workspace root (fmt-check, clippy -D warnings, full workspace tests incl. the 17 new ones, boundaries, covenant, governance incl. T12 locked-gates over the new recipe, dossier, lean). Committed in workspace bn-cho5; not merged."}	blake3:EsAblFw7Cn1za8gS5jA28Jm8fmFH7qy_yV3QSlCGeZk
