1785904122785256	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy_Qs		item.comment	bn-3jrtz	{"body":"Delivered in-workspace @ b2d02e8 (4 commits, synced onto epoch c8d728d). just check exit 0 twice — pre- and post-sync. Protocol 3.3 -> 3.4, IDL 1.5 -> 1.6, one bump taken once at the end, covering both riders (report 1/2: riders + mechanics; 2/2: sweep table, pins, counts, concerns).\n\n== F19 PAID — CertificateRejected Error.data ==\nThe declared shape is CertificateRejection {checker, reason, field?} (@since 3.4), the FIRST declared Error.data shape. The design decision, from the wire's own vocabulary discipline: the kernel's reason travels as the KERNEL'S OWN stable tokens, relayed verbatim — each continuum-kernel-* crate gains Rejection::reason() and Rejection::field() (const, static tokens, the same diagnostic register as their existing Field::as_str), so the FIRST authority speaks and the daemon never transcribes (the second-authority move bn-dtg61 declined stays declined; the wire deliberately does NOT close the reason vocabulary because it does not own it — the checker_profile echoed-verbatim precedent). checker is Family::checker_crate()'s token, the identity detail/proof_status already spell. Threading mirrors the payload seam exactly: Fault.data (ErrorData enum, closed with a None arm) -> OperationOutcome.data -> transport Server::answer encodes in the NEGOTIATED encoding (an Opaque is bytes of the negotiated encoding, so the encoding-free dispatch cannot fill it); codec gains encode/decode_error_data_in resolved by the carrying object's own required code field — the same function-of-the-message-alone property as the envelope payloads, stated in the rewritten Error.data clause of rule encoding.opaque_payloads. Residual stated in the F19 entry rather than hidden: numeric specifics (offsets/counts/indices) still do not travel — recoverable by re-running the named kernel over the same bytes (bn-dtg61's own recovery sentence); every other code's data stays undeclared.\n\n== F20 PAID — verification.start terminal short-circuit ==\ndaemon::verification::terminal, exactly the flag's minimal fix: entry.is_terminal() ahead of the cached/started split; Completed keeps the cached-result lane (ok + result); Cancelled / non-resumable-Failed answers ok on the OBSERVING lane, task present (envelope and body), result absent, no continuation — mirroring task.resume's short-circuit, so one fact ('this identity names a task that will never run again') is now spelled one way by both operations that can reach it. Deliberate subtlety caught and stated everywhere it matters: a task THIS call created still answers task_started whatever status it reached before the answer was written (a synchronously-completing fresh start genuinely began work here; the result is the NEXT call's cached answer) — the IDL doc, started()'s doc and the RFC bullet all state the precise post-3.4 reading rather than the overclaim 'task_started = can still run'.\n\n== Version mechanics ==\nWhy a minor can carry F20's lane flip (stated in the IDL 1.6 revision note, the version doc, and RFC 0026's 3.4 bullet): no declaration moves — both answer shapes declared since 3.0, 'a client MUST handle both shapes' normative as long, and the status lane is 'a fact about what the operation did' (correction 45), not a field a version defines; exact precedent is 3.2's item 9, which flipped a parked campaign's answer ok -> task_suspended when it made the lanes reachable at all. F19's compatibility argument was CORRECTED mid-delivery: my first draft claimed 'a pre-3.4 connection is never sent the field' — false, this daemon has no per-field version-gated emission anywhere (the @since-3.1 audit field precedent) — the honest ground is that data has been a defined optional field since 3.0 and 3.4 declares its SHAPE for one code; a pre-3.4 reader does what the pre-3.4 text already obliged (carry the Opaque verbatim, never guess). Mechanics: version 3.4 / idl_version 1.6 / revision-history entry; PROTOCOL_VERSION + IDL_VERSION consts; version_window implemented() gains (3,4), declared-version assert flipped, range-spanning selection 3.3->3.4 (major window [3,2] untouched — the bn-3ayom catch re-verified: 2.x still served, pinned-minor test unchanged); idl_conformance struct count 45->46 (CertificateRejection after Error, registry NAMED_STRUCTS in IDL order), operations stay 73, every RFC 0027 count table unmoved (RFC 0027's currency line updated to say exactly that)."}	blake3:yXN23nSjoorBUShpFQ1a9WGZqVAMtelXwWc3EGMngrQ
1785904164225845	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy_gs		item.comment	bn-3jrtz	{"body":"Report 2/2 — F13, the ledger sweep, pins, counts, concerns.\n\n== RFC 0028 F13 PAID ==\ncontext-pack.schema.json gains $defs.selection_kind (the eleven selected[].kind tokens, ONE definition) and both selected[].kind and omissions[].kind $ref it — the F5/correction-2 precedent verbatim; recorded as correction 18. schema_epoch stays 1 for the reason F5's payment set: correction 16 had already made the closed vocabulary the contract in force, so this corrects the schema to the reading, not the contract. Executed negative evidence: jsonschema refuses the example's old free phrase ('observer-independent causal predecessor' is not one of [event, ...]) and an unknown selected[].kind, while the shipped example still validates; committed evidence: continuum-context::omission now reads the REAL schema file and pins the closure (both $ref sites + def enum == SelectionKind::ALL token-for-token in order) with a reconstructed pre-F13 mutant control proving non-vacuity. Sweep bycatch: RFC 0028's F5 and F12 were found paid-but-unmarked (the schema's own $comments cite them); their entries now carry the payment records.\n\n== Riders paid / deferred (all three ledgers swept at execution time) ==\nPAID: RFC 0026 F19, F20; RFC 0028 F13 (+F5/F12 marked). DEFERRED, each reason recorded in its owning ledger:\n- 0026 F1 grammar change to the IDL notation, not payable by the bump mechanism (unchanged from 3.1).\n- 0026 F2 repair changes a declared field's type — breaking.\n- 0026 F3 + F10 undecided open questions this RFC declines to answer yet; F19 narrows F3's neighborhood without deciding it.\n- 0026 F8 statements about what a daemon must NEVER emit; an IDL declares what may be.\n- 0026 F9 waits on the Continuation-struct open question.\n- 0026 F11 the interesting one: F19 gives it the MECHANISM (per-code declared shape resolved by code) but not a PRODUCER — no daemon fact exists for a backoff hint to report honestly; stays open for a producer, noted in both the F11 entry and the open question.\n- 0026 F15/F16/F17/F18 unchanged from the 3.3 sweep (nothing their entries wait on moved).\n- 0028 F1/F2/F3/F9 undecided schema shapes (schema-sweep owners); F4/F11 breaking halves (required-tightening; closed verdict vocabulary); F6 type change breaking; F7's own entry ratifies the pack-content route; F8 crashpack's owners; F10 = IDL open item 3.\n- 0031 F6 DEFERRED with correction 17's blessing kept in force: minting 'equivalent' is the breaking half of the F15/F18 pattern — major semantic-diff schema_version + classifier family + assembler + P2 valence must move together, wider than a minor bundle's mechanism; nothing held hostage (unknown never under-reports; a future major narrows an existing unknown arm without reinterpreting records). Recorded in the F6 entry itself.\n\n== Pins flipped, deliberately, each citing this bone ==\ndx14 A12 (doc + campaign row + NEW status assertions: ok, envelope task, no continuation, result absent); NEW two-branch test negative_a_terminal_identity_and_a_live_identity_answer_on_different_lanes (the A12-style shape assertion the brief owed — same body, same identity, live branch task-lane vs terminal branch observing-lane, differing exactly where F20 said and nowhere else; fails on the terminal branch's status without the short-circuit). CLI: check_verdict/debug_repair_surface/evidence_show/explain_pack/snapshot_family inline pins + 14 pr13 golden files gain the data block ('error.data none' x4 lines; JSON data:null — always-rendered per the redaction-stub convention, so a parser never branches on key existence). Anti-vacuity for F19: daemon_evidence extended (typed data on the outcome, tokens byte-equal to the kernel's own answer over the SAME bytes — relaying held, not guessed) + NEW client-frame test through a real Server (error.data present on the wire, decoded by code back to the kernel's tokens; fails without the shape — the field was absent daemon-wide before). Conformance: certificate.rejected.result golden added in BOTH encodings (canonical_form 7th vector at 3.4; canonical_cbor exchange set 6->7 with pinned hex; mutation sweeps cover both; the new struct also enters the registry synthesis sweep automatically).\n\n== PHASE-A-DEL-02 ==\nAnnotated delivered on plan §21 (the bone's own statement: this bundle was the deliverable's remaining known work), absences stated inline per the annotate-vs-decline rule: transport is local/in-process (the §10 'local IPC or authenticated HTTP/QUIC' socket half has NO carrier bone — lead may want one), 45/73 operations typed-unsupported pending their subsystems (the registry's own discipline), docs/36+46+55 banners still cite 3.3 (owed to the next doc regen). Traceability regenerated in-workspace; PHASE-A-DEL-02 active -> satisfied; validator pass.\n\n== Counts and gates ==\nWorkspace 2,904 tests green post-sync (2,895 pre-sync on the old epoch; +9 over baseline from this bundle: dx14 two-branch, daemon_evidence client-frame, 2x continuum-context schema pins, cbor golden growth + extended asserts). fmt clean; clippy -D warnings clean; boundaries/covenant (kernel line budget held with ~230 added TCB lines across the four Rejection impls)/governance/dossier/Lean all pass; just check exit 0 pre- AND post-sync (trunk advanced under me: bn-wco1; maw ws sync replayed 4 commits cleanly). Commits 91a8a23 (F19+F20 wire), d31cf32 (F13), c4fde63 (RFC sweep), b2d02e8 (DEL-02 annotation + regen). NOT merged, NOT pushed, no .bones change in-workspace.\n\n== Concerns for the lead ==\n1. MCP adapter fidelity: continuum-mcp's Refusal does not carry error.data — an MCP client cannot see F19's typed reason. Left out deliberately (the F19 entry names CLI rendering as the rider; adapters translate); worth a small follow-up bone.\n2. The daemon emits data regardless of negotiated minor (no per-field version gating exists anywhere — pre-existing posture, audit-field precedent). rule versioning.compatible_change's 'MUST NOT emit fields the negotiated version does not define' is satisfied only under the field-defined-since-3.0 reading I recorded; a lead who wants strict per-version emission gating should know it would be a NEW mechanism, not a restoration.\n3. F19 carries reason + field tokens but not the numeric payload — stated as the residual in the entry; if a client someday needs offsets without holding the artifact, that is a new flag, not an extension of this one.\n4. terminal() reports the task's held omissions/artifacts (started()'s treatment); defensible (the answer names held state) but the least-forced choice in the new branch.\n5. docs/36, /46, /55 regenerated-at-3.3 banners are now stale in version only; nothing in them is falsified by 3.4."}	blake3:bBTEshz5dUZkNkP2NQPnk7IsuyDj-u6W6oYRv7-lyaw
1785904227979816	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy_ws		item.create	bn-ulhg3	{"description":"Flagged at bn-3jrtz's 3.4 bundle: the MCP client's Refusal shape drops error.data, so F19's CertificateRejection {checker, reason, field?} — the first declared Error.data shape — does not reach typed-client callers even though the CLI renders it. Carry the data block through continuum-mcp's Refusal (typed, not stringified; the kernel's own vocabulary relayed verbatim per bn-dtg61's declined-transcription discipline), with a test proving a real kernel rejection travels to a client-visible typed value. Check RFC 0027's refusal contract for where the block belongs.","kind":"task","labels":["req:pr-10","implementation"],"size":"s","title":"continuum-mcp: Refusal drops Error.data — carry the typed data block through the client surface"}	blake3:gNB3lICablEUUjfk7JyGGdZ4gwI4v616OedX2mm4-r8
1785904228020822	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEygAw		item.comment	bn-3jrtz	{"body":"Lead: merged @ epoch a71c3cfae92b. Protocol 3.4 RATIFIED under the deferred-and-bundled discipline — fourth execution, one bump at the end, IDL 1.6. F19's shape decision is right: the kernels' own Rejection::reason()/field() speak first and the daemon relays verbatim, keeping bn-dtg61's declined-transcription boundary; goldens in both encodings; 14+5 CLI pins flipped deliberately. F20's caught subtlety (a task THIS call created answers task_started even when synchronously complete) is exactly the two-branch shape discipline holding. F13 pays the schema half on the F5 precedent with schema_epoch honestly unmoved, and the paid-but-unmarked F5/F12 annotations are good hygiene. F6 deferral RATIFIED — the breaking half of the F15/F18 pattern, correction 17's blessing in force. DEL-02 annotation with inline absences accepted (in-process transport only, 45/73 typed-unsupported, docs banners at 3.3 — docs regen rides the next docs pass). MCP Refusal error.data gap routed to a new req:pr-10 bone. Closing."}	blake3:xhPyztk9yFWhk3j5RrMcfm0aTZPJqzKF6O-rUE3EOQs
