1785905064243377	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyjQw		item.create	bn-3ovwq	{"description":"Successor carrier for R14 after the Phase A checkpoint (bn-33iv, evidence at notes/plan/notes/R14_ALIEN_MATH_THEATER_EVIDENCE.md). The Phase A checkpoint found all controls live but two with empty extension: the topology-confinement guarantee is exercised VACUOUSLY (no heuristic/topology code shipped — only OmissionReason::HeuristicCutoff, non-verdict), and publish-negative-results has no live REFUTED instance. This checkpoint re-runs when either gains content: frontier-engine code landing (the confinement claim becomes falsifiable — verify no correctness verdict derives from heuristic topology IN THE CODE THAT EXISTS) or a §24.5 kill firing (verify the negative result is published per ADR-0020). Risks are standing; each phase gets a checkpoint.","kind":"task","labels":["req:r14","risk","governance"],"size":"s","title":"R14 — next checkpoint (Phase B entry): re-verify alien-math controls once frontier code exists"}	blake3:FJh2JvyLvy4GoihiBMLizOKdrrQ6UVDX3Xmb3Pkp0Mw
1785905196033470	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyjgw		item.comment	bn-1os6	{"body":"R01 risk checkpoint executed (workspace bn-1os6, commit 2e55f57, `just check` exit 0: fmt, clippy, 226 green test blocks, crate boundaries, kernel covenant, governance, dossier, Lean T0/T1). Evidence artifact: `notes/plan/notes/R01_RISK_CHECKPOINT.md` (not merged; lives in-workspace for the lead's review/merge).\n\nCONTROL -> EVIDENCE (full citations in the artifact; summary here):\n\n1. gate-driven delivery — LIVE.\n   - Gate scheme: 11 gates, mechanically bidirectional-reconciled `plan.md` Sec22 <-> `docs/52` by `check_gate_scheme_correspondence` (wired into `just check` via `dossier`). Live run: `{'gates': 11, 'plan_bullets': 68, 'docs_bullets': 68}`, zero orphans.\n   - PR exits: 14 of 15 Phase A PR slots (0,1,2,3,4,4a,5,6,7,8,9,11,12,13) are `state:done` exit-evidence bones with merged commits; PR 10 (`bn-38i`) alone remains open, blocked on freeze-blocking G0-DX-10 — matches \"fourteen PRs exited\" exactly, and the one gap is the gate scheme working as designed, not a defect.\n   - `just check` composition: `fmt-check lint test boundaries covenant governance dossier lean` — 8 gates in one command, the same command this checkpoint itself passed.\n   - Phase-barrier wiring: `traceability.py`'s `phase_barrier_failures`/`phase_exit_failures`/`gate_failures`/`risk_checkpoint_failures` all pass (0 failures) per `validate_checked_traceability`. bn-1os6 itself is a live instance of the `risk_checkpoint_failures` rule: `phase-a` + `risk` labels, wired as a blocker of `bn-2ofd` (Phase A integration).\n   - Divergence recorded: brief cites \"69 criteria\"; mechanically verified is 68 bullets + G0's own holistic criterion (reconciles to 69 under that reading, but \"69\" is not found asserted verbatim anywhere).\n\n2. first vertical slice — LIVE.\n   - Engine->kernel->daemon: PR 8 (`bn-1jg0`). Die Hard (16 states/96 transitions/depth-6) explored by `continuum-engine-reference`, emitted as CONTCERT wire bytes, checked by `continuum-kernel-core` from wire form only, proven through the daemon by `continuumd/tests/pr8_exit_evidence.rs::positive_die_hard_returns_sixteen_states_through_the_wire_and_the_depth_six_solution_one_layer_down`.\n   - Daemon->CLI: PR 13 (`bn-2hmk`). `continuum-cli/tests/pr13_exit_evidence.rs` + golden fixtures `pr13-01-snapshot-create--sealed-die-hard-base`, `pr13-03-check-result--die-hard-refuted`.\n   - Daemon->agent client, both corpus members: PR 10 (`bn-134i`/`bn-3awq`). `continuum-benchmark/tests/pr10_impl03_task_completion.rs` runs both Die Hard and Dining Philosophers (573 states/2,365 transitions/depth-10 deadlock, exact deadlock state) through both the native-protocol and shell-baseline arms.\n   - Gap: the five-hop path runs on both corpus members; whether the agent-client hop *wins* its ablation (PR 10's own umbrella, `bn-38i`) is still open — same root cause as control 1's PR-10 gap.\n\n3. no frontier engine before G2 — LIVE.\n   - `plan.md` Sec20's 42-crate list matches `crates/` exactly, bidirectional diff empty.\n   - `tools/check_crate_boundaries.py` (self-tested, wired into `just check`): `{\"members\": 42, \"plan_20_crates\": 42, \"status\": \"pass\", \"violations\": []}` against 7 rules including `forge-not-imported-by-verifier` and `certificate-checker-not-search` — exactly the edges a smuggled frontier engine would have to cross.\n   - Frontier-adjacent crates (`continuum-incremental`, `continuum-engine-{symbolic,liveness,dpor}`, `continuum-forge`, `continuum-refinement`, `continuum-repair`, `continuum-debugger`, `continuum-security`, `continuum-corpus`, `continuum-cir`, `continuum-observer`, `continuum-proof-client`) are all 1-file, 19-25-line doc-comment-only scaffolds, each ending in the identical self-declared \"PR-1/IMPL-01 scaffold\" sentence. `continuum-incremental` independently confirmed empty at `bn-7ek41` (\"a PR-23 scaffold with no types\").\n   - By contrast `continuum-kernel-{sat,smt,temporal}` (4,000+ lines each) and `continuum-semantic-diff` (7,600+ lines) are already-exited Phase A deliverables (PR 9, PR 12), not frontier engines under R01's failure mode.\n\nAlso checked (register lists 5 controls; brief detailed 3 — the artifact gives the other 2 a lighter, still-cited pass rather than silently skipping them):\n\n4. explicit non-goals — mostly live, one typed gap. `docs/00_EXECUTIVE_SPEC.md` Sec\"Scope boundaries\" + `plan.md`'s 1.0-era non-goal notes (federation, weak memory) cited. Gap: docs/00 is titled \"Revision 2\" with no mechanical text-correspondence checker (unlike the gate scheme's) holding it against `plan.md`'s Rev-3 non-goal mentions — no drift found, but nothing guards against future drift either.\n\n5. separate core/product/research lanes — mostly live, one typed gap. Real mechanisms (Sec0.3's DESIGN/HYPOTHESIS claim lattice + Sec24.5 frontier lane register, `research/` directory separation, crate dependency-boundary rules) independently verified. Gap: no single named \"core/product/research\" enforcement site — this document's Sec5 synthesizes the claim from three mechanisms rather than citing one; flagged as this checkpoint's weakest-cited control and a candidate for a future dedicated crate-tier labeling proposal.\n\nPRECEDENT DECISIONS (this is the first R0N checkpoint — recorded in the artifact Sec7 for reuse by R02-R21):\n- Artifact home: `notes/plan/notes/R0N_RISK_CHECKPOINT.md`, sibling to `G0_SPIKE_MATRIX.md`/`PR0_EXIT_EVIDENCE.md` (doc-adjacent, not `tools/` — this document cites existing checkers, it does not add a new self-testing one).\n- Register row left untouched: `docs/08_RISK_REGISTER.md` carries no per-row status column (verified all 21 rows) — the bone comment + artifact carry the checkpoint, per the checkpoint's own brief. Do not invent a status column.\n- Scope discipline: when a brief names a subset of a risk's registered controls for deep citation, still address every control the register lists, at whatever depth the evidence supports, and mark the difference in treatment explicitly (see controls 4-5 above).\n- Gaps typed, not hidden: \"live\" does not mean zero gaps; every discrepancy found got its own severity judgment.\n- No traceability regen needed here: adding the evidence doc changed no text `generate_traceability.py` scans; confirmed via `validate_dossier.py`'s `plan_bones_traceability` check before and after (identical: `active_bones: 765, active_requirements: 652, coverage: complete`, no failure keys). Future checkpoints should re-verify rather than assume from this precedent alone.\n\nKill signal check: register's R01 kill signal is \"after G0 effort, no replayable real-code demo or no project willing to migrate its DST\" — not applicable; control 2 finds a replayable real-code demo already exists (two corpus members, five hops, frozen re-derived facts). No kill/narrow/defer/continue decision is taken here — that privileged program decision is routed to the human lead per the bone's own contract (\"Agents do not take the privileged program decision\").\n\nClaims released after this comment. Workspace bn-1os6 left uncommitted-to-main, unmerged, unclosed for the lead."}	blake3:99_7LCIqpje-gsst2pI1S6psvAggCl9vUT2AaDdi8vs
1785905268542474	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyjww		item.comment	bn-ulhg3	{"body":"Delivered in-workspace @ 7aaf2ce (base epoch 3104b092e60b). just check exit 0. Diff scoped to crates/continuum-mcp only — no Cargo.toml change anywhere, so no new dependency edges of any kind (lib keeps its single continuumd edge; tests use only existing dev-deps).\n\n== The carry ==\nRefusal gains data: Option<RefusalData> (field placed after detail, the wire Error's own position). RefusalData is two arms: CertificateRejection(continuumd::protocol::envelope::CertificateRejection) — the declared F19 shape, the kernel's checker/reason/field tokens relayed verbatim, typed not stringified (bn-dtg61's declined-transcription boundary holds: the client re-exports the wire struct, it never re-spells the vocabulary) — and Undeclared(Opaque), data under a code this client's version declares no shape for, preserved verbatim and uninterpreted. classify() resolves through the codec's own decode_error_data — resolution stays a function of the message alone (rule encoding.opaque_payloads) and the declared-shape table stays in ONE place, so a future code's declaration reaches this client by recompilation, not by a second table. A DECLARED shape whose bytes do not decode remains a ClientError::Codec: the answer is not the shape the protocol declares, the same refusal-to-guess classify already made for the envelope halves.\n\n== The decode-tolerance decision, and the one divergence worth recording ==\nThe codec's posture on an undeclared code's data is a hard refusal (CodecError::UndeclaredErrorData — it will not invent a reading), and the CLI's refusal_of inherits that severity (data under an undeclared code = malformed, ConnectError). This client mirrors the GROUNDS (never guess) but not the severity: RFC 0026's 3.4 revision states the obligation for a reader whose version does not declare the shape — 'carry the canonical value verbatim, never guess at it' — and bn-3jrtz's own concern #2 records that this daemon emits data with no per-field version gating, so an external typed client that hard-errored would break against the first daemon minor newer than itself. The CLI's strictness is defensible AT ITS STATION (version-locked to the daemon build, same crate tree — undeclared data there violates the version in force); an adapter client is not at that station. Not a text contradiction — a difference in station — but flagged in case the lead wants the two surfaces aligned.\n\n== Evidence (tests/typed_surface.rs, 12 green) ==\n(1) REAL kernel rejection over the client's own wire: fixture daemon gains EvidenceFamily; a genuine Die Hard CONTCERT (same emit_finite_closure construction as continuumd's F19 evidence) mutated by one trailing byte is staged and filed as a certificate node out of band via Server::daemon_mut (IDL §7 administration), then evidence.verify is driven through AgentClient::invoke over LocalPair. The refusal arrives code=CertificateRejected with data typed, held EQUAL to the daemon's in-process typed outcome captured over the same node before any frame existed (that relay is itself pinned byte-equal to the kernel's own tokens by continuumd's daemon_evidence F19 tests) — checker/reason/field intact end to end; checker literal continuum-kernel-core; trailing-bytes names no wire position and none is invented. (2) Forward-compat: a hand-built frame (this daemon CANNOT be made to emit data under an undeclared code — rule encoding.opaque_payloads forbids it, hence the conformance-vector posture) stages F11's retry-after candidate under QuotaExhausted; the client answers a typed refusal with data = Undeclared(bytes) verbatim, no error. (3) Anti-vacuity verified by MUTATION, not just by construction: reintroducing the drop in classify (data = None) fails test (1); restored, all green.\n\n== Notes for the lead ==\n- The mcp fixture still negotiates 3.2 (its pre-3.4 pin); the daemon emits F19 data regardless (the audit-field precedent bn-3jrtz recorded). The tests are honest about this: they exercise exactly the posture the 3.4 revision obliges of any reader. If the lead wants the fixture bumped to 3.4 that is a one-line change I left out of scope.\n- RefusalData::Undeclared preserves canonical-JSON bytes (the client's negotiated encoding); a CBOR-negotiating future client would preserve CBOR bytes — same posture, encoding-relative by construction since Opaque is bytes of the negotiated encoding.\n- lib.rs 'every refusal is a typed value' bullet updated to name the block; RefusalData exported at crate root beside Refusal. NOT merged, NOT pushed, no .bones change in-workspace."}	blake3:ZDgGDBMrxTSTpIMeg9Sfzrzlg_sLJP-QATQy5zUKLUU
1785905534207986	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEykAw		item.comment	bn-3j4g	{"body":"R16 risk checkpoint delivered. Workspace: .maw/workspaces/bn-3j4g, commit e264b97. New `tools/governance/check_r16_evidence.py` binds all five register controls (`notes/plan/docs/08_RISK_REGISTER.md:241`) to live enforcement, following `check_t12_evidence.py`'s binding-checker convention; wired into `just check`'s `governance` recipe.\n\nCONTROL -> EVIDENCE\n\n| Control | Live status | Citations |\n|---|---|---|\n| pinned toolchain | pass | `rust-toolchain.toml` exact `1.97.0` channel + workspace edition inheritance (GOV-1-01, live); `Cargo.lock` committed + every non-fmt cargo gate `--locked` + `overflow-checks = true` (T12's \"pinned lockfile\"/\"reproducible build metadata\", live); the nightly sanitizer lane (bn-cho5) mechanically confirmed outside `check:`'s recipe chain — its dated-pin residual (floating `nightly` toolchain arg, parameterized for a dated pin) is bn-cho5's, cited not re-litigated. INV-005's determinism suite (bn-jme9) is the adjacent ambient-nondeterminism half; cited, not duplicated here. |\n| narrow compiler-internal usage | pass | Mechanical grep over every `crates/**/*.rs` + `crates/*/Cargo.toml` + root `Cargo.toml`: zero `#![feature(...)]`, zero `rustc_private`/`rustc_*` compiler-internal crate refs, zero `#[rustc_*]` attrs, zero `proc_macro::` (compiler ABI), zero `RUSTC_BOOTSTRAP`, zero `cargo-features` unstable tables, zero `proc-macro = true` libraries, zero `build.rs` anywhere in the workspace (proc-macro/build-script surface inventoried at zero). GOV-1-02 (unsafe-forbidden) cited as the adjacent stable-metadata discipline. |\n| extraction adapters isolated | pass | Mechanical scan: zero workspace crate declares a Lean-named dependency edge, zero source file spawns `lake`/`lean`/`elan` as a subprocess. `continuum-proof-client` (plan §20's reserved Lean proof-service-client boundary crate, PR 28/Phase D) is still the PR-1/IMPL-01 docs-only scaffold with zero dependencies. `check_kernel_covenant.py` KCOV-03/04/07 (bn-2he: no-async/no-unsafe/no-plugins-or-dynamic-loading over the trusted checking base) and `check_crate_boundaries.py`'s overall pass cited. Honest gap: this is a zero-edge count true *because* PR 28 hasn't landed, not a positive boundary rule that survives it landing — recorded as a residual in the checker's own docstring. |\n| compatibility CI | pass | `.github/workflows/check.yml` (bn-28ur, landed same day) mechanically confirmed to trigger on `push`/`pull_request` against `main` and run `rustup show` (selects the pinned toolchain) then `just check`. First CI this repo has ever had. Read as \"the pinned build is validated pre-merge,\" not multi-toolchain compat testing — consistent with the one-pinned-toolchain (INV-014) posture; recorded as a scope choice, not a gap. |\n| prefer stable metadata over rustc internals where possible | pass | Same zero count as \"narrow compiler-internal usage\" — the preference is exceeded, not merely honored. |\n\nSelf-test proves all 11 fixture classes across the four new direct checks are caught (planted `#![feature(rustc_private)]`, `proc_macro::` use, `proc-macro = true`, `build.rs`, `sanitizers` folded into `check:`, `sanitizers` recipe removed, a `lean-sys` dependency, a `Command::new(\"lake\")` call, and three CI-workflow mutations), and every delegate's own self-test (`check_code_policy`, `check_t12_evidence`, `check_kernel_covenant`, `check_crate_boundaries`) still passes live — none of the five controls passes vacuously.\n\nRisk register R16 heading annotated `(delivered: bn-3j4g — five controls bound in tools/governance/evidence/r16.json)`, following the T12 threat-model precedent (`09_THREAT_MODEL.md:195`, `(delivered: bn-nio3 — ...)`) since no risk-register row had set its own precedent yet. Traceability regenerated in-workspace (`generate_traceability.py`): R16 flips active -> satisfied, registry 846/651 active (was 652). `validation-results.json` intentionally untouched — per `VALIDATION_REPORT.md`, that regen is a lead-loop/merge-time step, not a per-commit worker one.\n\nDivergence from the brief, recorded honestly: the brief's control list in the bone description also names \"compatibility CI\" and \"prefer stable metadata over rustc internals where possible\" only implicitly (the task text elaborates the first three at length); I treated the register's full five-control list as authoritative per \"if texts contradict this brief, follow the texts\" and delivered mechanical evidence for all five, not just the three elaborated in prose.\n\n`just check` exit 0 from the workspace root (fmt/clippy/tests/boundaries/covenant/governance incl. the two new check_r16_evidence.py self-test+real-run lines/dossier/lean all green, 19/19 Lean jobs). Committed as e264b97; not merged/closed per protocol. Claims released."}	blake3:ogmWzmHW1FjXwl-eJ6UeIBm2KK7zTeYlELQ8YiPHB9A
