1785954281491927	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyqQw		item.comment	bn-1ptb	{"body":"T04 evidence delivered in workspace .maw/workspaces/bn-1ptb (commit 1211d2a), not merged.\n\nShape chosen: Python binding checker (tools/governance/check_t04_evidence.py + evidence/t04.json), wired into just check's governance recipe — the T12/R16 shape, not a Rust evidence test. Reasoning: T04's substance is scattered across five crates (continuum-value, continuum-workspace, continuum-kernel-core, continuum-certificate, continuumd) plus two Python governance checkers. A single-crate Rust test (T02's home) can't cite behavior in four sibling crates without duplicating it; the T12-style checker can cite/re-run each site where it already lives, plus honestly encode \"no live producer\" as data rather than either a false pass or an invented Rust fixture for code that doesn't exist. Rust delegates are still real: each cited test is re-run for real (cargo test -p <crate> --test <file>|--lib --locked -- <name> --exact), not read from a stale evidence file, so the test's own baked-in adversarial fixture (a b\"impostor\" payload, a wrong-family-magic certificate, a mutated byte past the magic) is what proves the control, not this file's own logic.\n\nControl -> enforcement (4 of 6 live, re-run every invocation):\n- content-addressed manifests: ADR-0013 text pinned directly; continuum-value identity::tests::blake3_is_a_pure_function_of_the_bytes (digest is a pure function of canonical bytes, never of host/clock/process); continuum-workspace pr3_exit_evidence.rs's snapshot-manifest-survives-disk-changes test\n- build identity: continuum-kernel-core receipt::tests::the_trusted_inputs_are_exactly_the_seam + the_envelope_supplies_the_hashes_the_certificate_carries (INV-014 Seam: source_digest, binary_digest, toolchain); check_kernel_covenant.py KCOV-09\n- reject digest mismatch: continuum-workspace publication::tests::an_identity_collision_aborts_rather_than_conflating_two_artifacts (impostor fixture); continuum-certificate family_routing.rs's three tests (wrong-family magic refused, single-byte mutation past magic still independently checked, garbage never verifies/never panics); continuum-kernel-core pr9_exit_evidence.rs's unrecognized-wire-epoch-rejected test; check_crate_boundaries.py (independent re-verification, not self-certification)\n- preserve redaction commitments: redacted.schema.json's required commitment field; continuumd daemon_evidence.rs's two tests proving Redacted.commitment survives redaction and a later re-verification byte-identical\n\nTyped-absence gaps (2 of 6), each with its own mechanical absence-check that fails loudly if it stops holding, not silently:\n- hash chain/Merkle root over events — no production-trace ingestion pipeline exists in this Phase-A tree (no schema, no Rust producer under crates/*/src). Not the same claim as bn-15gj's workspace-content Merkle root, which is a real citation for a different control over a different asset — conflating them would be an unearned claim. Absence-check: no notes/plan/schemas file names a trace/event artifact, no canary producer identifier (ProductionTraceLog etc.) exists anywhere under crates/*/src.\n- optional signing/attestation — docs/09 marks this one optional, unlike its five siblings. No signing/attestation dependency is pinned anywhere; the other four controls jointly deny an adversary a forged-pass path without a signature. Absence-check: no known signing-crate name in Cargo.toml [workspace.dependencies], reinforced by GOV-1-07 (dependency-rationale.toml is the whole workspace's dependency inventory — today exactly one external crate, blake3).\n\nT04 heading annotated (delivered: bn-1ptb - ...), no nested parens (learned the hard way — traceability.py's delivered-strip regex is \\(delivered:[^)]*\\), so a nested paren truncates the summary; fixed before regen). Regenerated PLAN_REQUIREMENTS.json + PLAN_BONE_TRACEABILITY.md in-workspace: T04 flips active -> satisfied (threat category 12->11 active, 3->4 satisfied), dossier validator (plan_bones_traceability) reports coverage: complete. just check exit 0 twice (full run) plus just governance/just dossier/just traceability re-verified independently after the final edit (added check_code_policy as a third delegate for the GOV-1-07 reinforcement).\n\nClaims released."}	blake3:64C_l-yZ9XSSxoPjqSts2Ic0uF-hRBX-EC7MZRNWjPw
1785954448914407	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyqgw		item.comment	bn-1ptb	{"body":"Lead: merged @ epoch e59ad00e325b. T04 delivered as check_t04_evidence.py (8th governance checker, T12/R16 binding-checker shape — the right call: T04's substance spans five crates + two checkers, so a single-crate Rust test would duplicate logic while the checker RE-RUNS each site's own adversarial test — b\"impostor\" payload, wrong-family-magic certificate, mutated-byte-past-magic — as the actual proof). 4 of 6 controls bound to live re-run enforcement (content-addressed manifests/ADR-0013, build identity/INV-014 Seam+KCOV-09, digest-mismatch rejection/IdentityCollision+wrong-family+unrecognized-epoch-from-bytes, redaction-commitment survival); 2 honest typed-absence gaps each with a self-tested absence-check not a fabricated citation (hash-chain/Merkle over EVENTS — no production-trace pipeline in Phase A, distinct from bn-15gj's workspace Merkle; optional signing — docs/09-marked optional, no signing dep pinned reinforced by GOV-1-07). The nested-parens catch (broke traceability.py's delivered-strip regex, fixed before regen) is a good validator-hygiene find — worth noting in briefs. governance green on trunk. Closing."}	blake3:0DkN6ZJU2um6QU1FhUGVVxrSBIgStIJr5mLdULHhxr4
