1786291942902601	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyxww		item.comment	bn-1hg4o	{"body":"Delivered in workspace bn-1hg4o (commit 10e2c6e, base epoch 7d46ad2). All four scoped items shipped; `just check` exit 0.\n\nVERIFIED OPERATION COUNT — the bone's 72/72 is stale. All three sources agree at **73 operations in 18 namespaces** (the registry grew to 73 at protocol 3.3 when RFC 0038's `evidence.link` landed — RFC 0027 says so in its own revision note). Derived authority histogram: execute 41, read 18, propose 8, revise_intent 4, promote 2 — matching RFC 0027's prose (\"the table is 73 rows, `execute` is 41\").\n\nITEM 1 (PRIMARY) — sd01 mechanized. notes/plan/tools/validate_dossier.py gains `check_protocol_registry_agreement()`, registered as its own gate entry (`protocol_registry_agreement`) and now the body of `sd01`. Three independent parses with no precedence between them, exactly as `rule conformance.registry_agreement` demands: `^operation ns.verb {` + `authority <tok>;` from schemas/continuumd-native-protocol.idl; the plan §10.2 ```text fence expanded from its `ns.verb / verb` shorthand (continuation lines joined by 'does not open a new ns.' rather than by trailing slash); and the RFC 0027 one-row-per-operation table. It enforces: set-equality IDL↔plan §10.2 and IDL↔RFC 0027 (reported in BOTH directions), authority token equality per operation (folding only the licensed `revise-intent`/`revise_intent` spelling the RFC itself licenses), no duplicate plan entry, and RFC 0027's prose '**N operations in M namespaces**' against the derived counts. `sd01`'s old predicate was `glob('*idl*')` — it proved a file existed, not that the registry agreed.\n\nFailure messages tested by temporarily breaking each class, then restoring (sources verified clean afterwards via git status):\n  - dropped `evidence.link` from plan §10.2 → 'declared in schemas/continuumd-native-protocol.idl but absent from plan.md §10.2: evidence.link'\n  - invented an RFC row `task.telepathy` → 'listed in rfcs/0027-agent-tool-protocol.md authority registry but not declared in schemas/continuumd-native-protocol.idl: task.telepathy'\n  - flipped `proof.slice` to promote in RFC 0027 → 'proof.slice: authority \\'execute\\' in schemas/continuumd-native-protocol.idl, \\'promote\\' in rfcs/0027-agent-tool-protocol.md'\n  - edited the prose count to 72/17 → both count lines, with the 18 derived namespaces listed\n  The header names the rule and says 'no source is preferred — fix whichever artifact moved alone'. SD-01 correctly flipped to `open` under each break (spec_debt reported 'SD-01: plan says paid, validator derives open'), which is the ledger's own signal.\n\n  Observation, NOT enforced (out of the rule's text, recorded for the RFC 0026 expansion bone): RFC 0027's Annotations column is ALSO currently token-identical with the IDL's per-operation annotations — I diffed all 73 and the only difference is `@since(\"3.3\")` on `evidence.link`, a versioning marker the RFC does not reproduce. A future bone could mechanize the annotation column too.\n\nITEM 2 — crate fixtures wired in. New `EXTERNAL_SCHEMA_PAIRS` (paths relative to the repo root, via new `PROJECT_ROOT = ROOT.parents[1]`) registers crates/continuum-intent/tests/fixtures/{die-hard,replicated-register}-contract.json against schemas/intent-contract.schema.json. `check_schemas` now reports `{\"pairs\": 21, \"external_instances\": 2}` (was `{\"pairs\": 19}`). Both fixtures validate with zero errors. A missing/moved instance is an explicit assertion, not a silent skip — tested by moving die-hard-contract.json aside: 'schema instance is registered against schemas/intent-contract.schema.json but does not exist; a moved or deleted instance loses its conformance check silently'.\n\nITEM 3 — IETF RFC false positive fixed. tools/governance/check_code_policy.py: `RFC_CITATION` is now `(?<!IETF )(?<!IETF-)\\\\bRFC[- ](00\\\\d{2})\\\\b` — narrowed to the dossier's 00NN range AND guarded against an explicit IETF prefix (both remedies the comment suggested, since either alone leaves a hole). Verified behaviour: `RFC 3339`, `RFC 2119`, `RFC 8949`, `IETF RFC3339`, `IETF RFC 3339`, `IETF RFC-3339`, `IETF RFC 0026` → no match; `RFC 0026`, `RFC-0007`, `RFC 0099` → match. The three existing `IETF RFC3339` workaround sites (crates/continuumd/src/protocol/scalar.rs, crates/continuumd/tests/adversarial_construction.rs) still pass, and the plain spelling would now pass too. Anti-drift: a new `continuum-rfc-range` sub-check fails the rule if any file in notes/plan/rfcs/ ever falls outside 00NN, so the first RFC 0100 cannot silently make its own citations invisible. Anti-vacuity: new fixture tools/governance/fixtures/code/gov-1-08-dangling-rfc-citation (RFC 0026 → RFC 0099 in crates/continuum-value/src/lib.rs) proves the narrowed pattern still fires on `citations-resolve`; self-test 56 → 57 fixtures, pass. Note the same constant feeds check_revision_delta.py's `citations()` — narrowing is strictly more correct there too, since an IETF number was never a decision record. GOV-1-08's `enforces` string and tools/governance/evidence/gov-1.json regenerated to match.\n\nITEM 4 — SHIPPED. Decision: ship. notes/plan/tools/traceability.py `_clean` now stashes code-span contents behind \\x00N\\x00 markers before the `[*_]+` emphasis strip and restores them after (link-text unwrapping still runs in between). Evidence for the safety rule: ran `python3 notes/plan/tools/generate_traceability.py` in-workspace and diffed both artifacts against HEAD.\n  - notes/plan/notes/PLAN_BONE_TRACEABILITY.md — BYTE-IDENTICAL (`cmp` clean).\n  - notes/plan/notes/PLAN_REQUIREMENTS.json — 11 changed leaves, ALL text: 7 `summary` strings and 4 `metadata.controls[]` strings. ZERO key changes. Proven mechanically, not by eye: all 846 `id` values identical in the same order; top-level keys identical; the whole document with `summary`/`controls` blanked to a sentinel compares EQUAL to HEAD's. So no req:* label binding can move.\n  - Every one of the 11 is an underscore restored inside a backticked identifier that the old code mangled: dx01falsification→dx01_falsification, inv008typedinconclusivenessevidence.rs→inv008_typed_inconclusiveness_evidence.rs, INCONCLUSIVEOBSERVATION→INCONCLUSIVE_OBSERVATION, INCONCLUSIVERESOURCELIMIT→INCONCLUSIVE_RESOURCE_LIMIT, TRUSTEDSOLVER→TRUSTED_SOLVER, query.explaininvalidation→query.explain_invalidation, schemaepoch/schemakind/schemaid/schemaversion/encodingversion→schema_*/encoding_version, defect→defect_*, notyetenforced→not_yet_enforced, task.updatebudget→task.update_budget, notapplicable→not_applicable, CHECKEDBY→CHECKED_BY. Grepped the whole repo for the mangled spellings: no consumer anywhere (only my own new comment mentions MAXSTATES).\n  - `_clean` is never used to build an id — all 9 call sites feed summary/bullet text only; ids come from PR/gate numbers and ordinals.\n\nGATE — `maw exec bn-1hg4o -- just check` run twice in the foreground, exit 0 both times: fmt-check, clippy (-D warnings), cargo test --workspace --locked, crate boundaries, kernel covenant, all 8 governance checkers with self-tests, dossier validator (status pass, spec_debt open []), and lean (19 jobs, axiom manifest up to date, T0/T1 axiom-free).\n\nFOR THE LEAD — two notes. (1) The workspace is `stale`: trunk advanced by bn-3l5la (0bb0040/7fb19ea) after my base 7d46ad2. That commit touched RFCs 0028/0031/0032/0037, PLAN_BONE_TRACEABILITY.md, and validation-results.json — none of the files I changed, and neither the IDL, plan.md, nor RFC 0027, so no conflict is expected. (2) I deliberately did NOT regenerate notes/plan/validation-results.json (VALIDATION_REPORT.md / .github/workflows/check.yml call it a lead-loop/manual step, not CI-gated). On the next lead regen it will gain a `protocol_registry_agreement` block and `schemas` will read pairs 21 / external_instances 2."}	blake3:lYF9Rf2_3A4wyVgR9elEicNmmP2gjFFhiRRPVA1EGDU
