1786292761380297	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy0Qw		item.comment	bn-3080b	{"body":"Delivered the evidence.subscribe notification channel — one of the three pieces bn-3080b carries. Whiteboard compiler and the remaining query set are OUT, and go to successor bn-35l6g.\n\nWHAT WAS ACTUALLY MISSING\nThe daemon-side half was already built: every committed delta has been recorded in DaemonState::evidence_events since the graph landed. evidence.subscribe refused with UnsupportedSemanticFeature, and the refusal was right — @streaming has said since 3.0 that an operation \"delivers `events` frames on the same connection\" and the operation's `events EvidenceEvent` clause named the struct, but NO normative source said what an event frame is, how a client tells one from a ResultEnvelope, or which committed deltas a declared scope selects. A frontier answered into that gap would have been the empty success rule errors.unsupported_surface forbids. The missing thing was the wire spelling, and INV-003 says schemas decide it.\n\nNORMATIVE EDIT — please review, this is the part a lead may want to check\nIDL 1.6 -> 1.7, adding ONE rule and no declaration: `rule subscription.delivery`. protocol `version` stays \"3.4\". Three clauses:\n1. An event frame IS the declared event struct, framed and encoded like every other frame, told apart from a ResultEnvelope by required members (request_id/status vs at/kind) — the ServerWelcome/ServerReject device of rule handshake.rejection. No event envelope, so no new struct.\n2. An event carries no subscription identity, which decides two things: a delta selected by two subscriptions on one connection is delivered ONCE, and a subscription delivers only what was committed after it opened — everything earlier is the frontier, so frontier and deltas partition.\n3. A delta is in scope exactly when the scope's EvidenceQuery selects the artifact the delta's own `kind` names, by evidence.query's own predicate, evaluated against the graph when the frame is written — ONCE per delta per subscription, and an unselected delta is not reconsidered. The limit is stated out loud: a scope a later write would have brought an earlier delta inside may be silent about it, and the client recovers by re-reading.\nNo protocol-minor bump: rule versioning.compatible_change lists five triggers and this fires none — the IDL 1.5 / bn-1h158 precedent, with the one honest difference recorded in both files: 1.5 wrote down what daemons already did, while nothing had ever delivered an events frame, so 1.7 decides rather than reports. No F-flag: rule errors.common as relaxed at 3.2 makes \"whether a lane has shipped\" a property of a deployment, not of an operation. RFC 0026 gains the matching versioning bullet and a superset sentence on its subscription bullet; registry.rs IDL_VERSION 1.6 -> 1.7; idl_conformance rule count 39 -> 40.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION\ndaemon/evidence.rs — subscribe() is served. selected() factored out of query() so the frontier and evidence.query are ONE predicate, nodes then edges, one list on the wire. New pub fn in_scope(scope, event, state): the delta's `kind` decides which member names its artifact, so a mislabeled event cannot reach a scope by filling the other member; an event whose kind's member is absent names no artifact and is in no scope. FAULTS drops the evidence.subscribe row with the reason inline; the IDL errors clause KEEPS UnsupportedSemanticFeature, because a deployment without the transport half still owes that refusal.\ntransport/mod.rs — the channel. Server holds a per-connection Vec<Subscription>{scope, delivered}, registered in answer_in only when the dispatch answered ResultStatus::Ok (a denial is not a subscription), cursor opening at evidence_events().len() — exact, because subscribe is @readonly and a dispatch holds &mut DaemonState for its whole extent. Server::deliver() iterates the LOG rather than the subscriptions, so one delta is one frame however many scopes select it, gated on delivered <= index, then advances every cursor to the log end whether or not each delta was in scope. serve() writes the result frame then the event frames. New Server::close() -> Daemon makes \"a dropped subscription changes nothing\" a signature. Client half: ServerFrame{Result(Box<ResultEnvelope>, Box<Payload>), Event(EvidenceEvent)}, decode_server_frame[_in], client_receive_frame[_in]; classification is on the ENVELOPE, not the whole result, so a payload/operation mismatch is a payload error and not a misclassification as an event.\nA subscription is a per-CONNECTION delivery cursor into the daemon's append-only log, deliberately not daemon state — that is what keeps RFC 0026's \"no session state\" and INV-002 true. Argued at the module header, on Subscription, and on DaemonState::evidence_events.\n\nTESTS — new crates/continuumd/tests/evidence_subscription.rs, 17 tests, all end-to-end across the real byte boundary\npositive (5): a_delta_committed_after_a_subscription_arrives_as_a_frame_on_the_same_connection; the_frontier_covers_what_came_before_and_the_frames_what_came_after; a_check_edge_and_its_receipt_arrive_as_two_deltas; canonical_cbor_delivers_the_deltas_canonical_json_delivers; a_scope_clause_is_read_against_the_graph_when_the_frame_is_written.\nnegative (6): a_delta_outside_the_declared_scope_reaches_no_frame; a_scope_naming_another_node_kind_reaches_no_frame; a_connection_that_never_subscribed_is_sent_no_frames; a_refused_subscription_opens_no_channel; an_event_frame_and_a_result_frame_never_validate_as_each_other (both directions, real frames); a_frame_of_neither_shape_is_a_typed_decode_failure.\nboundary (6): a_delta_two_scopes_select_is_delivered_once; a_later_subscription_sees_as_frontier_what_an_earlier_one_saw_as_frames; a_dropped_connection_loses_the_cursor_and_no_artifact (re-reading yields a superset of what the stream delivered); a_delta_the_scope_did_not_select_when_examined_is_not_reconsidered (the stated limit AND its recovery); a_delta_naming_no_artifact_is_in_no_scope (injected; unreachable through any operation); a_second_drain_yields_nothing.\ndaemon_evidence.rs: the refusal test is replaced by a_subscription_answers_the_scope_its_query_would_answer and a_scope_that_selects_nothing_answers_an_empty_frontier_rather_than_a_refusal.\n\nINVARIANTS: INV-003 — the delivery decision is in the IDL, not in Rust prose. INV-004 untouched — subscribe is @readonly, writes no status, mints no Promotion, relays only deltas already committed by the operations that own them. INV-008 — the scope predicate branches on a closed typed vocabulary, not on member presence. INV-002 — argued three times and pinned by the dropped-connection test. No unsafe_code.\n\nGATE: `maw exec bn-3080b -- just check` exit 0, run twice (the second after the final doc edit) — fmt, clippy -D warnings, 237 green test-result lines workspace-wide, boundaries, kernel covenant, governance, dossier validator with spec debt still empty, Lean T0/T1 with an empty axiom manifest.\n\nCOMMITTED in-workspace as 8996a79, 9 files, +1743/-46. NOT merged, NOT pushed, bone left open, workspace left in place. Successor bn-35l6g labelled implementation + req:phase-a-del-04. PHASE-A-DEL-04 deliberately NOT annotated as delivered; the deliverable is not complete.\n\nDELIBERATELY LEFT OUT\n- task.subscribe's TaskEvent frames. The rule covers both @streaming operations and TaskEntry::events is recorded the same way, but delivering them is task-lane work with its own hints-only semantics. Nothing here has to be redone when it lands: the same Server::deliver shape takes a second cursor kind.\n- Backpressure and any bound on how many frames one exchange may write. The transport is in-process and synchronous; a socket transport will need output_policy or ServerLimits to bound a burst, which is a transport-lane decision.\n- conflict_materialized deltas are handled by in_scope but never produced — no operation materializes a Conflict node yet. The branch is written rather than omitted, because the vocabulary is closed and a future producer must not find a hole.\n- Unsubscribe. There is no wire verb and the rule gives an event no subscription identity to name in one; a client drops the connection, which the dropped-connection test asserts costs nothing."}	blake3:ZVS-CHeTz7jTWq76zmCMaBV_jUdNEmom-ZJ7sKXNgpQ
1786292996728049	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy0gw		item.comment	bn-3080b	{"body":"Lead ratification: merged @ 7949ad753f2b (workspace destroyed, snapshot retained). Normative edit REVIEWED AND ACCEPTED: IDL 1.6→1.7 adding rule subscription.delivery only — no declaration moves, registry stays 73/46, protocol stays 3.4 (the relaxed-constraint trigger does not fire because errors.common at 3.2 already made lane-shipping a deployment property), no F-flag per the bn-23j7s line with the difference from the 1.5 precedent stated honestly in the revision note. Implementation review: frontier and evidence.query share one predicate via selected(); per-connection cursor keeps INV-002; once-per-delta evaluation limit stated in-rule and tested with recovery-by-re-read. 17 e2e tests + refusal test replaced by frontier/query-agreement pins. Successor bn-35l6g carries PHASE-A-DEL-04 (correctly NOT annotated delivered). Out of scope, recorded: TaskEvent frames for task.subscribe, backpressure bounds, unsubscribe verb. Closing."}	blake3:2mjayvhtvqVvSCcL9vsyVbExI4WZnlsvwhF7H-GQqzw
