1786293409429432	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy4ww		item.comment	bn-35l6g	{"body":"SPLIT AT PICKUP — assessment and decision.\n\nTwo remaining pieces were assessed against RFC 0038 and the IDL:\n\n(A) Whiteboard compiler (plan §11.5, docs/44 'Whiteboard', RFC 0038 'Whiteboard compiler').\nNo wire surface exists: there is no declared operation, and `EvidenceQuery` has no field\nfor it. Delivering it means (1) a NEW normative schema for the whiteboard note format —\nplan §11.5 gives seven section headings as prose in a code block, and INV-003 says schemas\ndecide, so prose cannot be the input format; (2) a NEW operation, which under the sd01\nregistry-agreement check moves IDL + plan §10.2 + RFC 0027 together and raises the protocol\nminor 3.4 -> 3.5 (`rule versioning.compatible_change`, first trigger). That is size L and\nit is exactly the 'new normative text' the pickup cautions say to avoid when declared\nsurface is still unserved. DEFERRED to the successor carrier.\n\n(B1) The seven RFC 0038 'Queries' forms (missing obligations, conflicting candidates, proof\nfrontier, repair frontier, semantic duplicates, provenance, task generation). Six of the\nseven are semantic and need artifact content the graph does not hold —\n`continuum-evidence/src/graph.rs` already argues this and names them as not-delivered\nrather than approximated, and inventing them over handles alone is the empty-success shape\n`rule errors.unsupported_surface` forbids. They also have no wire representation. DEFERRED.\n\n(B2) CHOSEN: `EvidenceQuery`'s declared-but-unserved traversal — `roots` as traversal\nroots and `max_depth` as its bound.\nThe IDL declares `roots: list<EvidenceHandle> optional` documented 'Roots to traverse\nfrom' and `max_depth: U32 optional`. The daemon serves `roots` as exact-handle membership\nand IGNORES `max_depth` entirely, and the reason written in the code —\n'`max_depth` bounds a traversal and this graph has no edges to traverse, so it selects\nnothing away' (daemon/evidence.rs) — WAS true when written and is now FALSE: `evidence.link`\n(protocol 3.3, IDL 1.4, bn-3sypm) made the graph hold edges. So this is declared surface the\ndaemon under-serves, with an expired justification, and it is the complete testable\nincrement at size s/m. It also composes with what just landed: `rule subscription.delivery`\nbinds a subscription's scope to 'the same predicate `evidence.query` answers with', so\nserving the traversal in `selected()`/`matches()` serves `evidence.subscribe` scopes in\nthe same move — the bn-3080b one-predicate idiom.\n\nNormative consequence, flagged for lead review: direction and depth are genuinely open, so\nthis follows the bn-3080b precedent and declares `rule evidence.traversal` in the IDL with\nan idl_version bump and no protocol-version bump. Full argument in the closing comment."}	blake3:iNtWO5jQ-gEJd4955oQgfsxKC1fh9q9T73jV6xtzirE
1786294423665150	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5Aw		item.create	bn-2qa0u	{"description":"Successor carrier for PHASE-A-DEL-04 after bn-35l6g landed the EvidenceQuery traversal (rule evidence.traversal, IDL 1.8: roots + max_depth served as declared, over the edges evidence.link gave the graph at 3.3).\n\nRemaining substance, both assessed and deferred at bn-35l6g's pickup:\n\n(1) The whiteboard compiler (plan §11.5, docs/44 'Whiteboard', RFC 0038 'Whiteboard compiler'). No wire surface exists — no declared operation, and no EvidenceQuery field for it. Delivering it needs BOTH a new normative schema for the whiteboard note format (plan §11.5 gives seven section headings as prose in a code block; INV-003 says schemas decide, so prose cannot be the input format) AND a new operation, which under the sd01 registry-agreement check moves the IDL, plan §10.2, and RFC 0027's authority table together and raises the protocol minor 3.4 -> 3.5 (rule versioning.compatible_change, first trigger). Two of docs/44's six compilation rules are already structurally enforced elsewhere — continuum-evidence/src/graph.rs refuses an edge endpoint the graph does not hold ('references must resolve'), and no evidence operation writes a status ('status words in prose do not promote status') — so the genuinely new work is the note format, the parser, and the proposal lane. Size L; split at pickup.\n\n(2) The seven RFC 0038 'Queries' forms: missing obligations, conflicting candidates, proof frontier, repair frontier, semantic duplicates, provenance, task generation. continuum-evidence/src/graph.rs already ships the selection-and-traversal layer these are written in, plus EvidenceGraph::contradictions ('conflicting candidates'), and names the other six as not-delivered because they need artifact content the graph does not hold — a proof frontier needs proof obligations, semantic duplicates need semantic equality. They also have no wire representation: EvidenceQuery has no field that names any of them, and adding one raises the protocol minor. Inventing them over handles alone is the empty-success shape rule errors.unsupported_surface forbids. Any slice here should start by deciding whether these are wire operations at all or library queries over content a later PR supplies.\n\nScope from RFC 0038 + the IDL + plan §11.5. May split at pickup. Do NOT annotate PHASE-A-DEL-04 as delivered from this bone alone.","kind":"task","title":"Evidence graph remaining surface: whiteboard compiler, the seven RFC 0038 semantic queries"}	blake3:i6L12rkJPQwx208-2OsjOi-0e26gTD24pVEv3-vRXm4
1786294427179710	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5Qw		item.update	bn-2qa0u	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"implementation"}}	blake3:QNbOEjh8Q4JsPpCi5d2scmT0YiwLTIv7sxE55EkWbOY
1786294427212876	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5gw		item.update	bn-2qa0u	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"req:phase-a-del-04"}}	blake3:e92jP3bxZguAogR-t8QLHVYeLjR6phdLX_LD-sMsL-M
1786294543629087	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5ww		item.comment	bn-35l6g	{"body":"DELIVERED (1/2) — decision, defect, and the normative edit.\n\n`rule evidence.traversal` (IDL 1.8) and the daemon that serves it. Committed in-workspace .maw/workspaces/bn-35l6g (4358644); NOT merged, NOT pushed, bone left open for the lead.\n\nSUCCESSOR CARRIER: bn-2qa0u (labels: implementation, req:phase-a-del-04). PHASE-A-DEL-04 is NOT delivered and is not annotated as such.\n\n## The defect this closes\n\n`EvidenceQuery.roots` has read 'Roots to traverse from' since protocol 3.0 and `max_depth: U32 optional` has been declared beside it just as long. The daemon served `roots` as exact handle membership and ignored `max_depth` outright, with the reason written into daemon/evidence.rs: '`max_depth` bounds a traversal and this graph has no edges to traverse, so it selects nothing away'. That was true when written and stopped being true at protocol 3.3, when `evidence.link` (bn-3sypm) gave the graph its first edges. Silently dropping a declared bound is the 'degrading, guessing' `rule errors.unsupported_surface` forbids.\n\n## NORMATIVE EDIT — FLAGGED FOR LEAD REVIEW\n\nnotes/plan/schemas/continuumd-native-protocol.idl: new `rule evidence.traversal` (six clauses); `idl_version` 1.7 -> 1.8 with a revision note arguing the versioning consequences; two `EvidenceQuery` field doc comments pointing at the rule. `protocol.version` stays \"3.4\".\n\nVersioning argument, stated so it can be disagreed with: `rule versioning.compatible_change` names five triggers — adding an operation, an `optional` field, an `@open` enum member, an error code, or relaxing a server-side constraint. This revision pulls none. No field, member, code, struct, or operation is added, removed, or moved: 73 operations, 46 structs, 9 aliases, 34 enums, 2 unions; rules 40 -> 41 is the only count that moves. This is the IDL 1.7 shape and 1.6's F20 argument: what moves is not a declaration but WHICH of an operation's already-declared answers a scenario lands on. It differs from 1.5 honestly and the revision note says so: 1.5 wrote down behaviour every conforming daemon already had, whereas here a daemon's answers DO move — from a strict subset of what `roots` selects to what the declared word 'traverse' says. A client that meant the old subset writes `max_depth = 0` and gets it exactly; a client that never sends `roots` cannot observe the revision at all. If the lead judges an observable answer change to be itself a compatible change, the fix is a one-line `version` edit plus `@since` on the rule — I did not take it because none of the five named triggers fires and taking a minor for a rule would contradict the 1.5 and 1.7 precedents.\n\nRFC 0038 is deliberately UNTOUCHED: the decision belongs in schemas/ (INV-003), and the RFC's 'Queries' section lists the seven semantic forms this slice does not serve.\n\n## The six clauses, and why each was decided that way\n\n1. An edge is walked in EITHER direction. An edge's direction is what it ASSERTS, not which way relevance runs. Every SUPPORTS, REFUTES, and COUNTEREXAMPLE_TO edge names the claim as `to`, so a forward-only walk from a claim reaches none of the evidence offered to it; reverse-only fails the mirror case, since `certificate CHECKED_BY receipt` points away from its subject. RFC 0038's own reason for a graph ('one counterexample refutes several candidates; one invariant supports many properties') and docs/44's 'each task contains only relevant graph slice and handles' are both neighborhood language. Picking one arrow would be inventing an answer no source took.\n2. Depth counts edges; a root is 0; a node is at d+1 when an edge incident to a depth-d node names it (least such value = shortest undirected path); an EDGE takes the GREATER of its endpoints' depths, or 0 when named in `roots`, in which case both endpoints are 0 too.\n3. `max_depth` absent is unbounded; 0 is exactly the pre-1.8 membership reading, so nothing a client could previously ask became unaskable.\n4. An answer names no dangling edge. This falls out of clause 2 rather than being a filter: max(from,to) <= bound implies both endpoints are within the bound. It is 'references must resolve' — the graph's own structural refusal — holding for a query ANSWER and not only for a write.\n5. The traversal walks the whole graph; the other clauses filter what it reached. A walk restricted to nodes matching `statuses` would mean 'paths through validated nodes', which is one clause changing another's meaning rather than conjoining with it.\n6. Absent or empty `roots` is the whole graph and `max_depth` then selects nothing away — the field's own declared sentence. With no root there is no distance to bound."}	blake3:5bhYA-3ufelAeCdrzq9ItLW9R6Z2_Iqxwy0giz7pgUY
1786294571800996	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6Aw		item.comment	bn-35l6g	{"body":"DELIVERED (2/2) — implementation, test inventory, gate, exclusions.\n\n## Implementation\n\ncrates/continuumd/src/daemon/evidence.rs — new private `Reach` enum (Whole | From(BTreeMap<EvidenceHandle,u32>)), computed once per query by `Reach::of` (symmetric adjacency built from the edge set, BFS from the roots, then edge depths assigned as the max of their endpoints'), consulted per artifact by `Reach::holds`. `matches`/`matches_edge` take it; `selected` builds it once. This follows the bn-3080b idiom exactly: ONE predicate, so `evidence.query`, `evidence.subscribe`'s frontier, and `in_scope`'s delta filter cannot disagree. `in_scope` recomputes Reach per delta — that is the rule's own 'evaluated once per delta per subscription, against the graph as it stands', read for a scope whose selection now depends on the edge set — and it short-circuits to Whole for the common rootless scope, so the cost lands only on scopes that asked for a traversal.\n\nAlso: crates/continuumd/src/protocol/registry.rs `IDL_VERSION` 1.7 -> 1.8; crates/continuumd/src/protocol/shared.rs field doc comments mirroring the IDL.\n\n## Test inventory\n\ntests/daemon_evidence.rs (operation layer, +8 tests). The fixture gains one capability, `cap_checker_smt`/`service:kernel-smt`, so a two-edge chain is constructible without violating INV-004 (kernel-core cannot check the receipt kernel-core produced), plus a `link_under` helper naming the checker profile — a receipt node's identity is f(content, profile), so one staged blob yields two distinct receipt nodes and no new staging is needed. Graph under test: a -E1- r1 -E2- r2, plus b connected to nothing.\n- POSITIVE  a_traversal_crosses_an_edge_in_either_direction — with and against the arrow on one edge; forward-only or reverse-only each fails a half.\n- BOUNDARY  max_depth_bounds_the_traversal_in_edges — 0, 1, 2, 9 (saturating), and absent.\n- INVARIANT a_rooted_answer_names_no_dangling_edge — clause 4 checked at every bound 0..3 from each of the four artifacts as root.\n- NEGATIVE  an_artifact_no_edge_connects_to_a_root_is_out_of_scope — both directions, unbounded.\n- NEGATIVE  a_root_the_graph_does_not_hold_reaches_nothing — alone, and beside a held root, so an unheld handle is neither a wildcard nor a probe for graph contents.\n- BOUNDARY  an_empty_roots_list_is_the_whole_graph_and_a_depth_bound_selects_nothing_away — absent and Present(vec![]), both with max_depth 0.\n- BOUNDARY  a_root_naming_an_edge_seeds_the_edge_and_both_its_endpoints — at 0 and at 1.\n- CLAUSE 5  the_traversal_walks_the_whole_graph_and_the_other_clauses_filter_what_it_reached — `a` promoted to observed; a scope rooted at r2 with statuses:[observed] and max_depth:2 still answers `a` THROUGH r1, which the status clause excludes. At max_depth:1 the same scope answers nothing, so the bound is real and the test cannot pass vacuously.\n\ntests/evidence_subscription.rs (REAL BYTE BOUNDARY — the client writes request frames, the server reads bytes it did not construct, and frames come back decoded by shape with no tag and no typed hand-off; +2 tests):\n- a_rooted_scope_delivers_the_deltas_its_traversal_reaches_and_no_others — frontier is the root; an unrelated ingest reaches no frame; the evidence.link one edge out delivers exactly NodePublished + EdgePublished.\n- a_zero_depth_scope_is_membership_and_a_new_neighbor_reaches_no_frame — the link delivers nothing, while a promotion of the ROOT still delivers a StatusTransition, so the bound is shown to exclude the neighborhood and not the root.\n\ntests/idl_conformance.rs — document.rules.len() 40 -> 41 with the revision-ledger entry beside 1.4-1.7; the existing IDL_VERSION/idl_version agreement assertion covers the bump, and the count assertions cover 'no declaration moved'.\n\n## Gate\n\n`maw exec bn-35l6g -- just check` — EXIT 0. Run in the foreground to completion (fmt, clippy -D warnings, crate boundaries, the full workspace test suite, the dossier validator including the sd01 IDL/plan §10.2/RFC 0027 registry agreement, `lake build`, and the ADR-0035 axiom manifest). daemon_evidence 66/66, evidence_subscription 19/19. Two iterations were needed: a rustfmt reflow and one clippy clone_on_copy.\n\n## Deliberate exclusions\n\n- The whiteboard compiler and the seven RFC 0038 semantic queries — assessed at pickup with reasons recorded in the split comment, carried forward by bn-2qa0u.\n- @paginated on evidence.query: page_size / page_token / next_page_token are envelope-level and next_page_token is Absent for every operation the daemon serves. That is a cross-cutting `rule pagination.deterministic` debt across all @paginated operations, not evidence surface, and folding it in would have made this slice two things.\n- No new operation, no registry move, no protocol-minor bump, no RFC edit.\n\n## Observation for the lead (not fixed here)\n\nnotes/plan/notes/G0_SPIKE_MATRIX.md line 20 (the DX-12 row) still discloses 'the evidence graph's remaining Phase A substance (the evidence.subscribe event channel, the whiteboard compiler, and the full query set) is disclosed as unbuilt'. evidence.subscribe shipped with bn-3080b, so that sentence was already stale before this bone, and this slice narrows 'the full query set' further. I left it alone rather than edit another bone's evidence row unbidden — worth a sweep when PHASE-A-DEL-04 closes."}	blake3:JSHMrJdtuUGHp0OEtp-k-Aw2hp9zwsiEmExoO6Wg5uU
1786294636071218	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6Qw		item.comment	bn-35l6g	{"body":"Lead ratification: merged @ 6a639705bb7d (workspace destroyed, snapshot retained). Normative edit REVIEWED AND ACCEPTED: IDL 1.7→1.8 rule evidence.traversal — six clauses, symmetric adjacency justified from assertion-vs-relevance with RFC 0038's own rationale, depth counts edges with max-of-endpoints placing edges so answers name no dangling edge, max_depth 0 = the old membership reading so nothing askable became unaskable; version stays 3.4 on the F20/3.2-item-9 which-declared-answer-lands reading, stated honestly against the 1.5 precedent. Slice choice ratified: the stale no-edges justification was falsified at 3.3 by evidence.link — serving the declared bound was overdue; whiteboard compiler and seven semantic queries correctly deferred to bn-2qa0u with reasoning carried. One predicate across query/frontier/in_scope preserved. G0_SPIKE_MATRIX line-20 staleness noted for a hygiene sweep. Closing."}	blake3:sdKtAdw_ZUtyO7UUT4wGgN3iLs8zuKyN8pgqc5oenBw
1786294636111115	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6gw		item.move	bn-35l6g	{"state":"done"}	blake3:7tkdrbUhjgFDxtzZyWB83_Dksbh781R9AJLyv3Wx0Vw
1786294798582527	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6ww		item.comment	bn-8g9uj	{"body":"STAGING SPLIT DONE (1/2). bn-8g9uj STAYS OPEN as the parent carrier — the lead should leave it open until all five children land.\n\nTHE SPLIT — five children, all parented to bn-8g9uj, all labelled implementation + req:pr-11, all size m, chained with bn triage dep add in this order:\n\n1. bn-21vno — 'Context Pack compiler stages 1-2: pipeline spine, auditable intermediates, redaction pre-pass, root selection, backward causal slicing'. The spine every later group appends to: the closed thirteen-member guarantee vocabulary with rule C1 as a type and rule C2 enforced at seal; the engine-independent causal order (continuum-cir is a documented stub and this crate may not import an engine) together with the INDEPENDENT closure checker RFC 0028's Validation section requires; the ten-member Stage vocabulary plus the auditable-intermediate trail for RFC 0030's compared artifact 5; and the pre-pass/stage-1/stage-2 run, publishing through crate::accounting so INV-007's counting equation holds by construction. DELIVERED — see comment 2/2.\n   blocks ->\n2. bn-1kj2n — stages 3-4: property-automaton relevance filtering (PropertyPreserving, licensed by an INDEPENDENT monitor — 'an implementation that reuses the compiler's own automaton has checked nothing') and the static/dynamic dependence join licensing source/model item admissibility under INV-016. Owes the abstraction-relevant-hidden-event boundary ('a compiler MUST NOT exclude one on the grounds that no observer publishes it') and the slice-irrelevant vs heuristic-cutoff distinction as stage 3 sees it.\n   blocks ->\n3. bn-imhw2 — stages 5-7: proof-dependency slicing (ProofRelevant), observer projection (INV-013, 'never a guarantee by itself'), abstraction/refinement correspondence mapping. Its load-bearing work is the REFUSAL: no proof-service artifact and no section-16 correspondence graph are landed in this workspace, so those stages must record typed unsupported omissions and decline their guarantees rather than degrade (rule errors.unsupported_surface, INV-008, rule C1). The anti-vacuity mutant — a stage that quietly licenses ProofRelevant with no proof slice behind it — is that bone's most important test.\n   blocks ->\n4. bn-3ub6i — stage 8: minimal unsatisfied core / correction-set analysis. The six transcript-checkable minimality classes, each licensed only by its OWN transcript (rule C3: a consumer MUST NOT infer one class from another); ExplanationMinimal pinned as NOT emittable from a minimizer transcript (correction 5 — it is a docs/48 preregistered study objective); CardinalityMinimal refused without declared bounds; RFC 0028's named mutant 'a minimality class with no transcript' rejected, plus a removal-minimized slice that is no longer downward closed (the Lean minimality-soundness obligation's finite shadow).\n   blocks ->\n5. bn-1y4qc — THE EXIT PIECE (last child): context.compile daemon wiring + production root pack assembly + G0-DX-01 re-run at production grain. Its description carries the trip-wire warning verbatim: crates/continuumd/tests/pr11_exit_evidence.rs pins context.compile's refusal LIVE, so landing the wiring flips that exit layer red BY DESIGN and forces the production re-run in the SAME change. What changes there is the grain declaration (harness -> production, compiler no longer named as missing producer) and the re-blessed goldens; what does NOT change is the closure that rejects an artifact for hiding its grain or un-naming its missing producer. Also owed: dx01_falsification.rs's harness causal_core and assemble_root_pack replaced by the compiler, both golden evidence artifacts re-blessed, the G0-DX-01 matrix row's Decision cell and the START_HERE PR-11 Exit annotation updated to production grain, traceability regenerated. Root assembly settles the decision daemon::context deliberately declined (ctx_* question identity vs the store's content identity).\n\nTwo stages are in NO child, on purpose:\n- Stage 9 (heuristic ranking) — RFC 0028 blesses 'plain causal slice + expansion, stage 9 disabled' as the register row's named fallback and requires a deployment be able to run in that configuration. It is the lane's kill surface and belongs to research/32's own work, not to this build.\n- Stage 10 (budget packing) — continuum-context::budget::BudgetPacker is it in substance already, which is bn-3m65's own reading and the reason the parent's title says 'stages 1-8'."}	blake3:q1KuCIMz3LYsZpNZ5C-vsBJMpwJKJ_XLbLn59PPvOyk
1786294801189338	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy7Aw		item.comment	bn-1dsih	{"body":"[bn-1dsih report 1/5]\n\nDelivered in workspace bn-1dsih, commit 5558156, `just check` exit 0 (fmt, clippy -D warnings, 231 test binaries green, crate boundaries, kernel covenant, governance, dossier, Lean T0/T1). NOT merged, NOT pushed, bone left open, workspace left standing for the lead.\n\n## The lane\n\n`crates/continuum-forge/src/task.rs` — one public module, `continuum_forge::task`. Entry point:\n\n    pub fn assemble(&IntentContract, &CheckEnvironment) -> Result<SynthesisTask, TaskRefusal>\n\nIt makes BOTH calls RFC 0037 AO3 requires of \"a caller that needs both answers\", and both run before either is allowed to decide:\n\n1. `IntentContract::check(environment)` — the document's five cross-field rules (W2, W3, W7, W8, W9).\n2. `Optimization::require_non_vacuity()` — INV-012's obligation, AO2's second call. This is the production caller F12 said did not exist.\n\nThe second call is deliberately NOT behind an early return on the first. That is what makes \"the lane made the second call\" an observable property of the returned value rather than a claim about the source: a contract that fails both comes back as `TaskRefusal::NotWellFormed` whose `vacuous()` is `Some`, which a short-circuiting lane could never populate. Two tests pin exactly that pair (ill-formed + vacuous reports both grounds; ill-formed + non-vacuous reports only the first).\n\n`SynthesisTask` is constructible only by `assemble` — private fields, no public constructor — so a value of the type *is* the evidence the obligation was required and met, and `positive_behaviors()` is non-empty by construction (plan §14.5's \"Every synthesis task includes positive behaviors or progress scenarios\", held structurally rather than by convention). It carries plan §14.2's `fixed`/`objectives` blocks as far as a contract supplies them: intent_id, the declared non-vacuity behaviors verbatim, and the hard/soft objectives.\n\n## The refusal type (INV-008, never a bare boolean)\n\n    pub enum TaskRefusal { NotWellFormed(NotWellFormed), Vacuous(VacuousIntent) }\n\nTwo variants carrying two distinct payload types, not one struct with a reason tag: \"this is not a contract\" and \"this is a contract this lane may not act on\" are different outcomes with different remedies.\n\n`VacuousIntent::invariant() -> &'static str` returns the token `INV-012` **as data**, not only as message prose — so \"the refusal names INV-012\" is checkable by a caller and by a test, and cannot be lost to a rewording of `Display`. `NotWellFormed::verdict()` relays `continuum-intent`'s own `ContractVerdict` rather than re-spelling the W-rule vocabulary this crate does not own. Both types and the enum impl `Display` + `core::error::Error`; `TaskRefusal::vacuous()` answers \"was the obligation met\" identically whichever call refused first.\n\nRFC 0033 gap, recorded rather than papered over: RFC 0033 is a 25-line research-implementation sketch. It names the loop's \"non-vacuity screen\" and fixes **no wire vocabulary at all** — so \"follow RFC 0033 for the refusal's shape\" had nothing to follow. I delivered the refusal as a crate-level typed value and deliberately invented no wire spelling: `continuumd-native-protocol.idl` declares no error code for a refused synthesis task, this crate takes no protocol dependency, and choosing an `ErrorCode` for a verb with no family would put a second authority over a vocabulary RFC 0026 and RFC 0033 own. When the Forge verb lands, `TaskRefusal` is the value it renders. Stated in the module doc."}	blake3:El6r2PYuLwquewhEgOOSpe_DIFI4_ok9cgkOox_44n0
1786294801228641	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy7Qw		item.comment	bn-1dsih	{"body":"[bn-1dsih report 2/5]\n\n## Tripwire reconciliation 1 — INV-015 (crates/continuumd/tests/inv015_agent_least_authority_evidence.rs)\n\nVERBATIM WHAT CHANGED:\n\n- `boundary_the_host_effect_surface_has_not_arrived_and_its_crates_are_scaffolds`: the `(\"continuum-forge\", FORGE_LIB)` row was removed from the `scaffolds` array. The other SIX rows (effects-network/process/storage/time, proof-client, security) keep the original stricter form — zero pub items AND the self-declared scaffold sentence. The tripwire stays live for them; its doc comment now says explicitly that forge \"is *not* silently dropped\" and names where it moved.\n- NEW `boundary_forge_grew_a_task_assembly_lane_and_it_reaches_no_host_effect`, which re-establishes for a real surface what emptiness used to assert. Four legs, each swept live:\n  1. `FORGE_PUBLIC_SURFACE` — a recorded 17-item inventory of every `pub` line across every file under `continuum-forge/src`, gathered by a directory WALK (`forge_sources()`), not a hand-listed set, so a new module is swept without anyone remembering it. An unrecorded public item fails the test. This is the same tripwire in a different shape: the surface cannot grow past what INV-015 has audited without the audit being redone.\n  2. no code line in the crate names a host-effect facility (`std::fs|io|net|os|env|process|thread|time`, `SystemTime`, `Instant`, `Command`, `TcpStream`), comments excluded.\n  3. the crate's `[dependencies]` is exactly `[\"continuum-intent\"]` — read as text, deliberately sharing no code with `check_crate_boundaries.py`'s cargo-metadata walk (docs/03 §8 diversity).\n  4. unreachable from an agent connection.\n- Module-doc clause-4 row and honest-gap row 1 rewritten to match, and `mutants::negative_the_forge_host_effect_audit_detects_its_mutants` added: a planted `std::process::Command` call is found, a comment merely mentioning one is not, an added pub item is seen, a widened dependency set is seen, a planted daemon caller is seen.\n\nSURPRISE, AND WHY LEG 4 IS PHRASED CAREFULLY: my first draft asserted \"the registry declares no `forge` namespace\". That is FALSE and the test caught it. `continuumd/src/protocol/registry.rs` has declared `forge.create`, `forge.step`, `forge.archive`, `forge.materialize` since the protocol surface landed, and `protocol/operations/forge.rs` carries their request/response structs. The honest property — verified and now asserted — is one layer down: those four are declared vocabulary ahead of their subsystem (`rule errors.unsupported_surface`), every one declares `UnsupportedSemanticFeature` in its own `errors` clause, and **no `OperationFamily` answers for the `forge` namespace** (the eight registered families are workspace, intent, observe, context, task, verification, evidence — swept by looking for the namespace literal a family's `namespace()` would return). Plus: `continuumd`'s manifest does not depend on `continuum-forge`, and no `continuumd` source names `continuum_forge`. So a `forge.*` request meets a typed refusal at the dispatcher and never reaches any code in the crate. INV-015's fourth clause is intact and its basis is now stated correctly rather than optimistically."}	blake3:n-qy4VjIP7CnFbx0p1k4dMqSl0EqDLXmD25gauFDFyQ
1786294801258847	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy7gw		item.comment	bn-1dsih	{"body":"[bn-1dsih report 3/5]\n\n## Tripwire reconciliation 2 — R01 (notes/plan/notes/R01_RISK_CHECKPOINT.md + notes/plan/docs/08_RISK_REGISTER.md) — FOR LEAD REVIEW\n\nI checked the brief's suggested reading against the plan and it does NOT hold as stated. The brief proposed asserting \"Forge is a product-lane synthesis surface, not a frontier engine\". The first half is wrong: R01 §3a itself classifies `continuum-forge` by name among the \"search/synthesis crates ('frontier'/research-adjacent)\", and §3b ties it to §24.5's synthesis lanes research/29 and research/30. **Forge IS frontier-adjacent, and I did not reclassify it.** The defensible half is the second: it is not a frontier *engine*. So I split the claim in two and updated the observation truthfully:\n\n- Kept: Forge is frontier-adjacent. The forbidden-edge rules `forge-not-imported-by-verifier` and `certificate-checker-not-search` are untouched — I edited no table in `tools/check_crate_boundaries.py` — and both still pass with zero violations over 42 members (verified: `{\"status\": \"pass\", \"violations\": []}`). The one new edge is forge -> continuum-intent, which runs in plan §20's permitted direction.\n- Corrected: the sentence \"every frontier-adjacent crate a scaffold\" is now false as written. R01 §3b's table row for `continuum-forge` (1 file / 25 lines) is removed — leaving a stale measurement beside a two-file crate would be a false reading of a live workspace — and a dated amendment records what landed, what the crate now measures (2 files / 402 lines), and that twelve of the original thirteen crates keep their rows unchanged.\n- Control 3 NOT weakened. Its conclusion is re-derived under §3b's own test, applied unchanged: R01's failure mode is a frontier engine before its gate, and the lane is none of the ambitions it names — no grammar, no enumeration, no counterexample loop, no candidate, no archive, no engine adapter, no search. It is a contract-*admission* decision, the same category §3b already declines to count as a frontier engine for `continuum-semantic-diff`. PR 29 (\"Forge finite CEGIS v0\") remains unopened. The §3b conclusion paragraph now reads \"every frontier-adjacent crate is mechanically verifiable as holding no frontier engine — twelve as non-implementing scaffolds, and `continuum-forge` by two live tests\". I argue this NARROWS the evidence base rather than the control: what was thirteen self-declarations is now eleven self-declarations plus two executable checks over a real surface.\n- docs/08 R01 annotation amended in place, keeping the `(delivered: bn-1os6 …)` form the traceability extractor needs: \"…with every frontier-adjacent crate holding no frontier engine — twelve as doc-comment-only scaffolds and continuum-forge as an audited task-assembly lane that runs no search, amended 2026-08-09 by bn-1dsih\". No nested parentheses. Verified `just traceability` regenerates PLAN_REQUIREMENTS.json and PLAN_BONE_TRACEABILITY.md byte-identically — the extractor strips the delivered annotation, so R01's status stays `satisfied` and no regen is owed.\n- Nothing mechanical pins either R01 document: I swept the whole tree and no test, checker, or tool reads `R01_RISK_CHECKPOINT.md`, so these are prose corrections, not a broken gate.\n\nLEAD: the reading you may want to double-check is the \"not a frontier engine\" judgment, since it is the load-bearing one and it is a judgment call about categories, not a mechanical fact.\n\n## Tripwire 3 — crate boundaries (INV-004)\n\nRespected, untouched. The forbidden-edge table in `tools/check_crate_boundaries.py` was not edited. One new workspace edge, forge -> continuum-intent, in the permitted direction. `continuum-certificate/tests/inv004_no_self_certification.rs` still forbids `continuum-forge` in the checker's closure and still passes."}	blake3:95IcpehJCBHGHNRg-4Q9yA8drHUnM4Ja5SrPxnXU2l8
1786294801288060	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy7ww		item.comment	bn-1dsih	{"body":"[bn-1dsih report 4/5]\n\n## F12 bookkeeping\n\n`notes/plan/rfcs/0037-intent-contract.md`, F12 bullet, house style matched (the \"— **Paid by the bn-1lfek schema sweep, 2026-07-31**: …\" tail F3/F4/F5/F10/F11 all carry). Flag text kept as raised, discharge appended: \"— **Discharged by the bn-1dsih Forge task-assembly lane, 2026-08-09**: …\". No nested parentheses anywhere in it. Both halves are now pinned by tests: `inv012_nonvacuity_evidence.rs` asserts the flag-as-raised is still recorded AND that the discharge annotation is present, and the forge suite asserts the same annotation, each with the mutate-and-recheck anti-drift step.\n\n## inv012 evidence flip (crates/continuum-intent/tests/inv012_nonvacuity_evidence.rs)\n\nThe no-production-caller observation is flipped, not deleted, and stays a tripwire.\n\n- Module doc: \"`continuum-forge` … is still a PR-1/IMPL-01 scaffold — [test] pins that mechanically\" became \"What has changed since is *who calls it*: `continuum-forge`'s task-assembly lane landed at bn-1dsih and is now the production caller RFC 0037 AO2 names\". New clause-map bullet \"the second call's production caller, landed\".\n- `boundary_mutation_challenges_are_forges_own_responsibility_and_not_yet_landed`: the two assertions that forge's lib.rs contains \"PR-1 / IMPL-01 scaffold\" are replaced by live citations of the lane — `src/task.rs` contains `require_non_vacuity`, contains `pub fn assemble(` (so the call is reachable from a public lane, not buried in a private helper), and the lane's own suite still carries the skip-the-second-call mutant test by name. New consts `CONTINUUM_FORGE_TASK` / `CONTINUUM_FORGE_TASK_TESTS` via `include_str!` (bytes, no dependency edge — so `forge-not-imported-by-verifier` is untouched).\n- It remains a tripwire in the OTHER direction too: the same test pins that the SEARCH half is still unlanded — forge's lib.rs must still declare \"no enumeration, no counterexample loop\", and the lane must not contain `fn enumerate`, `fn mutate`, `struct Candidate`, `struct Archive`. The moment PR 29's machinery arrives, this boundary must be re-derived rather than assumed.\n\n## Test inventory\n\n`crates/continuum-forge/tests/non_vacuity_second_call.rs` — 11 tests, all over real corpus documents read at compile time, no clock/entropy/network/shell, every mutant a single bounded `replacen` on an input under 8 KiB (pinned by `fixture_sizes_stay_well_under_the_len_guard`):\n\nPOSITIVE: `positive_the_schema_example_assembles_and_the_task_carries_its_declared_behavior` (the dossier's own validated example, clean on all five rules, assembles; behaviors travel verbatim); `positive_the_die_hard_contract_assembles_with_zero_hard_and_zero_soft_objectives` (one behavior suffices — the corpus's own shape).\n\nNEGATIVE: `negative_a_wholly_well_formed_contract_with_an_emptied_non_vacuity_set_is_refused` (the sharp form — a contract clean on every W-rule is still refused; `invariant()` is `INV-012`; the message names the vacuity attack); `negative_the_die_hard_mutant_is_refused_and_the_refusal_names_the_invariant_as_data`.\n\nTHE MUTANT THAT SKIPS THE SECOND CALL: `mutant_a_lane_that_made_only_the_first_call_would_accept_the_vacuous_contract` — holds `check()`'s answer and the lane's answer side by side on the same bytes and requires them to disagree (`\"the contract satisfies W2, W3, W7, W8, and W9\"` vs `Err`), then shows the comparison can fail by running both over the unmutated example. This is correction 17's argument executed rather than restated.\n\nBOUNDARY: `boundary_an_ill_formed_and_vacuous_contract_reports_both_grounds`; `boundary_an_ill_formed_but_non_vacuous_contract_reports_only_the_first` (its control — a short-circuiting lane could not tell these apart, and a lane that fabricated the ground rather than running the call could not pass both); `boundary_one_single_character_behavior_discharges_the_obligation` (presence not content, RFC 0031/AO3 — reported, not patched); `boundary_the_two_refusals_are_distinct_types_not_one_flag` (INV-008).\n\nANTI-DRIFT: `the_disposition_this_lane_implements_is_the_one_rfc_0037_records` — three RFC anchors matched then each shown capable of failing, plus the F12 discharge annotation.\n\nPlus: inv012 evidence 15/15 green with the flipped assertions; inv015 evidence 30/30 green including the new Forge audit and its mutants; inv004, inv005, inv008 all still green."}	blake3:t8Uxlft4ZnHPYuTAON_UhpuSc1yHa0qVtpZ6775cNY0
1786294801320465	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy8Aw		item.comment	bn-1dsih	{"body":"[bn-1dsih report 5/5]\n\n## Gate results\n\n`maw exec bn-1dsih -- just check` exit 0, run in full twice. 231 test binaries, zero failures. Boundaries `{\"members\": 42, \"plan_20_crates\": 42, \"status\": \"pass\", \"violations\": []}` over all seven rules. Governance 12/12 GOV-1 requirements pass with self-tests. Dossier `program_status: ready`. Lean 19/19 built, axiom manifest up to date, T0/T1 axiom-free.\n\nTwo governance failures I hit and fixed honestly rather than silenced:\n- GOV-1-02 `no-unsafe-in-sources`: my host-effect facility list originally contained the literal token the `unsafe-forbidden` scanner searches for, making this file its own violation. Removed from the list and the module doc now CITES that rule as the enforcer instead of duplicating it — the workspace forbids the construct outright with no crate-level override, and GOV-1-02 already scans every source on every run.\n- GOV-1-07 `used-by-is-accurate`: the new forge -> continuum-intent edge made `tools/governance/dependency-rationale.toml`'s `used_by` list stale. Added `continuum-forge` and extended the rationale with why the edge exists — AO2 names this lane as the caller with standing, and a Forge-side re-implementation of the obligation would be the second spelling that table forbids everywhere else — and with the direction rule that enforces it.\n\n## Deliberate exclusions\n\n- No wire refusal shape, no `ErrorCode`, no IDL edit, no `forge` family. RFC 0033 fixes no wire vocabulary today and inventing one would claim authority over RFC 0026's. Forge stays wire-unreachable, which is also what keeps the INV-015 reconciliation honest.\n- No search: no grammar, typed holes, enumeration, CEGIS loop, counterexample classification, candidate, QD archive, engine adapter, or materialization. All PR 29. The lane runs no search at all.\n- No triviality/mutation judgement of a declared behavior. RFC 0037 AO3 puts content judgement on Forge at synthesis time and the guard is a presence requirement; `boundary_one_single_character_behavior_discharges_the_obligation` reports this openly rather than patching it, and `inv012_nonvacuity_evidence.rs` pins the absence.\n- No new dependency beyond `continuum-intent` (dev-dep repeated because `tests/*.rs` are separate crates). No `continuum-value` — test environments supply minted handles as literals rather than pulling in a hasher.\n- No doctest on `assemble`: a valid contract document is ~3 KiB and `include_str!` path resolution inside a doctest is not reliable, so the module documents the shape in prose and the integration suite carries the executable form.\n- Bone description says \"PR 22 surface\"; START_HERE's PR 22 is Promotion receipts and PR 29 is \"Forge finite CEGIS v0 … safety plus progress/non-vacuity\". I built to PR 29 / plan §14 and RFC 0037, which is where the obligation actually lives. Flagging the discrepancy rather than resolving it silently."}	blake3:xtCfEEi9Xem1DnJ2wtj0B8fR_i4FzP5cHEYRaXlk5zc
1786294830621650	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy8Qw		item.comment	bn-8g9uj	{"body":"FIRST CHILD DELIVERED (2/2) — bn-21vno, in workspace bn-8g9uj, committed fb0c0ad, NOT merged, workspace not destroyed. 6 files, +3275 lines, all inside crates/continuum-context.\n\n- src/guarantee.rs — the closed thirteen-member Guarantee enum in the SCHEMA's order, which differs from RFC 0028's own guarantee-table order; INV-003 decides ('schemas decide artifact shape, prose does not'), so the schema's order is the type's Ord and therefore the published sort key. Rule C1 is a type rather than a review note: RequestedGuarantees has no conversion into GuaranteeSet, and GuaranteeSet::claim needs a License whose constructor is pub(crate), so no consumer of this crate can mint permission to claim a guarantee whose checker never ran (a compile_fail doctest pins it). Rule C2 is enforced in GuaranteeSet::seal at the point stage 2 produces its precondition, so the later group that adds the replay checker finds the rule already in force. RequestedGuarantees::unachieved computes what C1's second sentence owes the manifest.\n- src/causal.rs — CausalOrder: a finite immediate-predecessor graph over named, kinded candidates, refusing edges to nowhere, self-edges, repeated nodes, and cycles (Kahn over BTree order, so the topological order is one sequence in every process). backward_closure is stage 2's computation; closure_violation is the INDEPENDENT checker — a function of the order's edges and the selection it is handed, knowing nothing about how that selection was made. Plus the reading this bone had to settle to be honest: a stage-2 drop is slice-irrelevant only if the order is DECLARED complete. Completeness::Partial{reason} (three typed reasons) forces heuristic-cutoff instead, because non-ancestry in an incomplete order proves nothing. RFC 0028's line — 'an item dropped because the compiler could not decide is heuristic-cutoff, never slice-irrelevant' — becomes a consequence of the input's own declaration rather than a per-call judgement.\n- src/stage.rs — Stage (all ten, root selection = 1 per correction 13), Phase (ordinal 0 = the redaction pre-pass, normatively ordered against stage 1 but not one of the ten), StageIntermediate (phase + working set + content digest; no clock and no duration, INV-005, because an intermediate that moved between two runs would make the parity comparison meaningless), and StageTrail, which refuses a repeated phase, an out-of-order phase — this is how correction 12's 'redaction runs before stage 1' is CHECKED rather than remembered — and a working set outside the candidate universe. Intermediates are retrievable per phase, which is RFC 0030 compared artifact 5's actual requirement.\n- src/compile.rs — CausalCompile::run: pre-pass, stage 1, stage 2. Three refusals it exists to make. (a) A redacted root is refused before stage 1 does any work ('a redacted root is not a root and slicing from one leaks the shape of what it dropped'). (b) Slicing runs over the WHOLE order, withheld nodes included, because a sub-order with them deleted reports a closure that is not one; when the backward closure reaches a withheld ancestor the node becomes an irretrievable redaction omission, it is not published, and CausallyClosed is NOT claimed — plan 18.4's 'never a confident smaller answer'. (c) A residual expansion query nothing published anchors is refused, since expansion is navigation over a published pack. The licence is issued from the independent checker's verdict on the selection ACTUALLY about to be published (after redaction removed items from it), never from the closure routine that built it — so a slicer bug costs the guarantee instead of producing a false one. RedactionReason (summarized/lost/purged) lands here; the redactions[] stub itself needs a commitment and an original class and is declined to bn-1y4qc by name.\n- tests/pr11_compiler_stages_1_2.rs — 15 tests from OUTSIDE the crate, at the G0-DX-01 row's grain. Positive: a 225-candidate order (4-event causal chain + 221 noise across two kinds) slices to exactly the four-event core, licensed closed by the independent checker run a SECOND time from the test itself; the 221 omissions reconcile exactly across two cells (event 165 + state_delta 56), all slice-irrelevant, all expandable behind one query; the trail's three phases are retrievable and stage 2 is shown GROWING what stage 1 selected (a trail that assumed monotone narrowing would have been wrong about the one stage that is not a filter), while five stages that did not run have no intermediate at all — distinct from an empty one. Negative: a textually assembled durability core is refused (RFC 0028 C2's own example); dropping ANY single core event is caught at its own successor, and dropping the root instead leaves a shorter CLOSED prefix, which is the honest answer; a redacted root is refused; a redacted ancestor costs the guarantee while the hole stays named, counted and irretrievable (222 omissions, reconciled); an unanchored residual query is refused; a partial order downgrades every drop to heuristic-cutoff while keeping the same core and the same closure licence. Boundary: an order that is entirely its own closure omits nothing; an empty order compiles to an empty, vacuously closed pack; two roots on one chain give the same core as the deeper one alone. Determinism (INV-005): two runs equal, trail digests equal, and a different compile has a different digest so the pin is not vacuous.\n\nHONEST SCOPE, stated in the test file itself before any number: this is stage group 1 only. The reduction reported is at CANDIDATE COUNT (225 -> 4), not bytes — bytes are content_budget.bytes on an assembled root pack (RFC 0028 correction 17) and root assembly is bn-1y4qc. Nothing here claims ReplayPreserving (stage 2 licenses only its precondition; the replay checker is not this crate's) or PropertyPreserving (bn-1kj2n's). The one guarantee this group can issue is CausallyClosed.\n\nGATES: maw exec bn-8g9uj -- just check → EXIT 0, run in the foreground to completion. fmt-check, clippy --workspace --all-targets -D warnings, cargo test --workspace --locked (continuum-context: 181 unit tests, the 15 new integration tests, 10 budget tests, doctests including the new compile_fail — all green), crate boundaries, kernel covenant, governance, dossier validator (status pass), lean (19 jobs, axiom manifest up to date, no sorry/axiom/native_decide). No src/ outside continuum-context touched, no existing test edited, no schema/RFC/plan file edited — this bone raises no new RFC flag and pays none.\n\nSURPRISE worth carrying to the later children: continuum_value::value::Name orders SHORTLEX (compare_blobs), not lexicographically. Every canonical list this crate publishes is in that order, so 'e_ack, e_loss, e_begin, e_submit' is the correct canonical selection for the dx01-shaped fixture. Two test expectations had to be corrected to it. Expect the same when bn-1kj2n/bn-imhw2/bn-3ub6i write their own goldens, and especially when bn-1y4qc re-blesses the dx01 and pr11-exit artifacts."}	blake3:E8VPMTLdb_XYkLvW4uXuXAo2pHgoqocK0r8hvHw7RoY
1786294864538454	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy8gw		item.comment	bn-21vno	{"body":"DELIVERED in workspace bn-8g9uj (the parent carrier's workspace, not this bone's own), committed fb0c0ad, NOT merged and NOT closed — the lead lands it. 6 files, +3275 lines, all inside crates/continuum-context.\n\nWHAT LANDED\n\nsrc/guarantee.rs — the closed thirteen-member Guarantee enum, declared in the SCHEMA's order. That order differs from RFC 0028's guarantee-table order (the table groups the minimality classes; context-pack.schema.json does not), and INV-003 decides: schemas decide artifact shape, prose does not. So the schema's order is the type's Ord and therefore the published guarantees sort key under rule ordering.deterministic. Rule C1 (requested is not achieved) is a TYPE here: RequestedGuarantees has no conversion into GuaranteeSet, and GuaranteeSet::claim takes a License whose constructor is pub(crate) — pinned by a compile_fail doctest, so a consumer of this crate provably cannot mint permission to claim a guarantee whose checker never ran. Rule C2 (ReplayPreserving implies CausallyClosed) is enforced in GuaranteeSet::seal, stated at the point stage 2 produces its precondition so that the group adding the replay checker finds it already in force rather than having to remember it. RequestedGuarantees::unachieved computes what C1's second sentence owes the manifest.\n\nsrc/causal.rs — CausalOrder, the engine-independent premise stage 2 slices over. Stage 2's declared input is 'CIR causal order'; continuum-cir is a documented stub and check_crate_boundaries.py forbids this crate an engine edge, so the order is a VALUE the compiler is handed, not an engine it calls — which is also the right separation, since a compiler that computed its own order and then checked closure against it would be checking its own arithmetic. Constructor refuses edges to nowhere, self-edges, repeated nodes, and cycles (Kahn over BTree order, so topological_order is one sequence in every process). backward_closure is stage 2's computation. closure_violation is the INDEPENDENT checker RFC 0028's Validation section requires: a function of the order's edges and the selection it is handed, knowing nothing about how the selection was produced. And the reading this bone had to settle to stay honest: Completeness is DECLARED by the producer, because a stage-2 drop is only slice-irrelevant ('provably outside the slice') when the order names every causal edge. Completeness::Partial{reason} — three typed reasons, no free text — forces heuristic-cutoff instead. RFC 0028's sentence 'an item dropped because the compiler could not decide is heuristic-cutoff, never slice-irrelevant' thus becomes a consequence of the input rather than a judgement made per call.\n\nsrc/stage.rs — the auditable-intermediate spine. Stage with all ten members (root selection = 1, correction 13), Phase with ordinal 0 for the redaction pre-pass (normatively ordered against stage 1 but not one of the ten — numbering it 1 would renumber the RFC's table, omitting it would make correction 12 unauditable), StageIntermediate carrying phase + working set + content digest and deliberately NO timestamp or duration (INV-005: an intermediate that moved between two runs of one compile would make the parity comparison meaningless), and StageTrail, which refuses a repeated phase, an out-of-order phase — this is how correction 12's 'redaction runs before stage 1' is CHECKED rather than assumed — and a working set naming anything outside the candidate universe. Intermediates are retrievable per phase, which is RFC 0030 compared artifact 5's actual requirement rather than a nice-to-have. Stage groups 2-4 append here.\n\nsrc/compile.rs — CausalCompile::run: pre-pass, stage 1, stage 2, ending in a ClosedAccounting, a StageTrail and a SealedGuarantees. Three refusals it exists to make. (a) A redacted root is refused before stage 1 does any work: 'a redacted root is not a root and slicing from one leaks the shape of what it dropped'. (b) Slicing runs over the WHOLE order, withheld nodes included, because a sub-order with them deleted would report a closure that is not one; when the closure reaches a withheld ancestor, the node is recorded as an irretrievable redaction omission, it is not published, and CausallyClosed is NOT claimed — 'where redaction removes an item a guarantee needs, the guarantee is not claimed and the reason is recorded', never plan 18.4's confident smaller answer. (c) A residual expansion query nothing published anchors is refused, since expansion is navigation over a published pack. The licence is issued from the independent checker's verdict on the selection ACTUALLY about to be published, after redaction has removed items from it — never from the closure routine that built it — so a slicer bug costs the guarantee instead of producing a false one. RedactionReason (summarized/lost/purged) lands here; the redactions[] stub needs a commitment and an original class and is declined by name to the root-assembly child (bn-1y4qc).\n\ntests/pr11_compiler_stages_1_2.rs — 15 tests from OUTSIDE the crate, at the G0-DX-01 row's grain: 225 candidates (a 4-event causal chain plus 221 noise across two kinds, several carrying durability vocabulary as red herrings) sliced by the production stage 2 to exactly the four-event core, licensed closed by the independent checker run a second time from the test itself; 221 omissions reconciling exactly across two cells (event 165, state_delta 56); the trail's three phases retrievable, with stage 2 shown GROWING what stage 1 selected and five never-run stages having no intermediate at all. Negatives: a textually assembled core refused (C2's own example); every single-core-event drop caught at its own successor, while dropping the ROOT leaves a shorter closed prefix — the honest answer, since closure is not a claim of completeness; a redacted root refused; a redacted ancestor costing the guarantee while the hole stays named, counted and irretrievable; an unanchored residual query refused; a partial order downgrading every drop to heuristic-cutoff. Boundaries: an order that is its own closure omits nothing; an empty order compiles to an empty vacuously closed pack; two roots on one chain give the deeper one's core. Determinism: two runs equal, trail digests equal, a different compile a different digest.\n\nHONEST SCOPE (stated in the test file before any number): stage group 1 only. The reduction is at CANDIDATE COUNT (225 -> 4), not bytes — bytes are content_budget.bytes on an assembled root pack (correction 17), which is bn-1y4qc's. Nothing here claims ReplayPreserving (only its precondition; the replay checker is not this crate's) or PropertyPreserving (bn-1kj2n's). CausallyClosed is the one guarantee this group issues.\n\nGATES: maw exec bn-8g9uj -- just check → EXIT 0 in the foreground. fmt, clippy -D warnings, cargo test --workspace --locked (181 unit + 15 new integration + 10 budget + doctests incl. the new compile_fail), boundaries, covenant, governance, dossier validator pass, lean 19 jobs with the axiom manifest up to date. No src/ outside continuum-context touched, no existing test edited, no schema/RFC/plan edited.\n\nNOTE FOR THE LEAD: this bone's work sits in the PARENT's workspace (bn-8g9uj), because the split and the first child were delivered in one pass. Merging bn-8g9uj lands bn-21vno; close bn-21vno at that merge and leave bn-8g9uj open as the carrier until bn-1kj2n, bn-imhw2, bn-3ub6i and bn-1y4qc land."}	blake3:AuZkHVQjyX-G1E-Wdo98-bw5dVEP_9LoTX9p6hMR2Gc
1786294871778180	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy8ww		item.comment	bn-1dsih	{"body":"Lead ratification: merged @ 3b6f4714668b (workspace destroyed, snapshot retained). Reviewed: both AO3 calls run before either decides so dual grounds report together; SynthesisTask's no-public-constructor makes the value itself the obligation's evidence; TaskRefusal two-variant typed refusal with INV-012 as data. RFC 0033 being a 25-line sketch, inventing no wire spelling was correct — crate-level refusal only, recorded. Tripwire reconciliations ACCEPTED: INV-015 forge row replaced by four-legged live boundary test with mutant coverage (and the registry-namespace surprise caught by the test is exactly why we pin these as tests); R01 amendment narrows evidence not control, keeps frontier-adjacent classification, delivered-annotation form preserved. F12 discharged in house style; inv012 evidence flipped to live citations while staying a tripwire on the absent search half. PR-22-vs-PR-29 discrepancy CONFIRMED: bn-1dsih's description was stale — PR 29 owns Forge CEGIS; building to PR 29/plan §14/RFC 0037 was right. Closing."}	blake3:2WaWZMufqzGxLlvzKzR1a1L-wyXuWcuWXVP4tB2ovFs
