1786296944177034	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEygQ0		item.comment	bn-2qa0u	{"body":"## Slice: the whiteboard note FORMAT + the compiler as a crate-level surface. No wire operation. (1/2)\n\nDelivered the recommended cut, with one correction of direction. Workspace `bn-2qa0u`, commit `7f872f3`. Not merged, not pushed, bone left `doing`.\n\n### Direction correction (pushback, please read)\n\nThe brief said \"the compiler that **assembles a whiteboard note from evidence-graph state**\". Every normative source says the opposite direction, and I built the normative one:\n- plan §11.5: \"The compiler **turns whiteboard entries into typed graph proposals**\".\n- RFC 0038: \"Converts **structured notes to proposed nodes**\".\n- docs/44's six rules are all note→graph (\"every claim becomes a proposed node\", \"references must resolve\", …).\n- This bone's own description agrees: \"prose cannot be the **input** format\".\n\nSo `WhiteboardNote::compile(&graph, &epochs) -> Compilation` is note → proposals. The reverse (docs/44's \"human-friendly whiteboard **view**\") has no schema, no surface and no decided semantics for which held node belongs in which section; it is named undelivered in the module docs, in RFC 0038's new closing paragraph, and carried on the successor bone.\n\n### What landed\n\n**Normative (flag for lead review):**\n- `notes/plan/schemas/whiteboard-note.schema.json` — NEW schema. draft 2020-12, `additionalProperties:false`, `$id` `https://continuum.dev/schema/v1/whiteboard-note.json`, `schema_epoch: 1`, `schema_kind: \"artifact\"`, `schema_id`/`schema_epoch` header const-pinned and required. Passes `sd08` (which re-derives every `$id` from file name + declared epoch) with no special-casing.\n- `notes/plan/schemas/examples/whiteboard-note.example.json` — registered in `SCHEMA_PAIRS` exactly like the other 19.\n- `notes/plan/rfcs/0038-…md` — \"Normative schemas\" now names the note schema; \"Whiteboard compiler\" gains **W1–W9** (every prose-to-schema decision) plus a \"Not decided here: the wire\" paragraph.\n- `notes/plan/docs/44_…md` and `plan.md` §11.5 (+ `plan.typ`, kept in sync) — one pointer sentence each: the seven headings are a map, the schema is the format.\n\n**Library:** `crates/continuum-evidence/src/whiteboard.rs` (new module) — `Section` (closed at seven, carrying both the plan's heading spelling and the schema's key), `NoteText`, `Entry`, `Decision`, `Experiment`, `WhiteboardNote`, `Compilation`/`CompiledNode`/`TaskProposal`/`Applied`, `NoteRefusal`, `CompilationRefusal`. `lib.rs` gains the module row and a \"the compiler is here, its wire verb is not\" section. Crate placement per plan §20 is `continuum-evidence`, whose responsibility statement and RFC 0038 both already say \"the library vocabulary is `crates/continuum-evidence`\". **No new dependency edge** — only what the crate already imports (`continuum-value`). `check_crate_boundaries.py` green.\n\n**Mechanical schema conformance (never hand-asserted):**\n- `tests/whiteboard_compiler.rs` + `tests/fixtures/whiteboard/*.json` (6 node records, 2 edge records) registered in `validate_dossier.py`'s `EXTERNAL_SCHEMA_PAIRS` against `evidence-graph-node`/`evidence-graph-edge` — the device `continuum-intent`'s contract fixtures already use. `check_schemas` now reports `pairs: 30, external_instances: 10`.\n- The **note** side is stronger: the test asserts `note.to_record()` renders byte-for-byte to `notes/plan/schemas/examples/whiteboard-note.example.json` itself (`include_str!`), and that file is validated against the new schema by the dossier gate with a real `jsonschema` validator. The dossier's normative example *is* what the library emits — no second copy to drift.\n- Two links, no hand-written middle: the validator checks conformance, the Rust suite checks \"these are the bytes\". Neither half passes alone.\n\n### Gate results — `maw exec bn-2qa0u -- just check`\n\nGreen: `fmt-check`, `lint` (clippy `-D warnings`), `test` (whole workspace), `boundaries`, `covenant`, `dossier` (`status: pass`, `spec_debt.open: []`, `schemas: pairs 30 / external_instances 10`, `handle_prefix_registry` clean), `lean`. `just traceability` re-run and `PLAN_REQUIREMENTS.json` regenerated (the gate demanded it; the diff is bone-state churn and the lead will regen again after merge).\n\n**One red, PRE-EXISTING and not mine:** `governance` line 94, `check_dependency_audit.py`. `Cargo.lock` resolves blake3 1.8.6 / cc 1.4.2 / find-msvc-tools 0.1.10 while `tools/governance/dependency-audits.toml` records 1.8.5 / 1.4.0 / 0.1.9; `check_t12_evidence.py` fails through the same delegate. **Reproduced on main @ affa890 with a clean tree**; this workspace adds no dependency and leaves `Cargo.lock` byte-identical. Not fixed here on purpose: the fix is either restoring the lockfile or recording a real vet with who/criteria/notes, and a worker filling in an audit attestation to green its own gate is the forged-attestation shape the manifest exists to prevent. Tracked as **bn-sk44r**.\n\n### Successor carrier\n\n**bn-1as8e** — \"Evidence graph remaining surface: whiteboard wire verb + view, the seven RFC 0038 semantic queries\", labels `implementation`, `req:phase-a-del-04`. Carries the wire surface (with its open sub-questions), the reverse-direction view, and the seven queries with the original deferral reasoning. PHASE-A-DEL-04 is **not** annotated as delivered.\n\nDecisions W1–W9, the test inventory, and the deliberate exclusions are in the next comment."}	blake3:QqzJuG2R37maLqWhvV1Tv-zMI7jkzXilGf-ZWskqISs
1786297008973754	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEygg0		item.comment	bn-2qa0u	{"body":"## bn-2qa0u (2/3) — every §11.5 prose-to-schema decision (RFC 0038 W1–W9)\n\nplan §11.5 gives seven headings in a code block and nothing else. Each of these was undecided; each is now decided **explicitly** in the schema and recorded in RFC 0038 as W1–W9.\n\n1. **W1 — cardinality.** One `goal`, required (§11.5 opens \"for complex invention *tasks*\" and spells `Goal` singular; a note with two goals is two notes). The other six are arrays that MAY be empty but MUST be **present** — an omitted section and an empty section must not be two spellings of one note, since the compilation has to be a function of the note's content.\n2. **W2 — heading decides node kind**, and the author cannot name a kind directly (otherwise the seven headings are decoration): `Goal`→`property` (§11.2 `Property` = \"a property to be established\"), `Known facts`→`assumption`, `Candidate invariant`→`invariant_candidate`, `Counterexample`→`counterexample`, `Unresolved obligation`→`proof_goal` (§11.2 `ProofGoal` = \"a goal handed to a proof worker\"), `Decision`→`decision` (§11.2 `ReviewDecision`). **`Known facts`→`assumption` is the load-bearing one**: reading an author's \"known fact\" as `observed` would be exactly the promotion docs/44 forbids prose from performing. `Goal`→`property` rather than `proof_goal` keeps `proof_goal` for the obligations, which is what a proof worker is actually handed.\n3. **W3 — `Experiment` is not a node.** docs/44: \"experiments become task proposals\"; §11.2's twenty kinds have no task kind, so growing a closed vocabulary was refused. An `Experiment` entry carries neither `claim` nor `subject` and compiles to a typed `TaskProposal` returned beside the nodes.\n4. **W4 — references resolve, subjects do not have to.** `references` MUST each name an artifact the graph holds a node about (docs/44 \"references must resolve\"); `subject` (what the entry is *about*, → the node's `artifact`) MUST NOT, because an entry whose subject the graph already held could propose nothing new. References become `provenance.inputs` — derivation, never assertion.\n5. **W5 — no status member at all, and prose is never parsed.** RFC 0038's \"rejects … status claims without evidence\" is met by the **absence of a field**, not a filter (D3: a field a producer can fill is a field a producer can lie in). A scanner for status *words* was explicitly rejected — it would make the graph a function of wording. `text` is human commentary and never enters a node (INV-003). `status_words_in_prose_reach_nothing` builds two notes differing only in prose (\"PROVED, validated, certified…\" vs \"still open\") and shows identical node identity, both `proposed`.\n6. **W6 — a `decision`'s `references` has `minItems: 1`**, so an unsupported conclusion is *unrepresentable* rather than refused (docs/44 \"conclusions require supporting edges\"). Where the graph holds several nodes about one cited artifact, one `SUPPORTS` edge is drawn from **each** — choosing between them is a semantic judgement the compiler cannot make. *Alternative considered and rejected:* refuse the ambiguity. The format gives an author no way to name a node — nodes are named by `ev_` handles the library deliberately does not mint (D2/D4) — so \"exactly one\" would be unsatisfiable in a busy graph.\n7. **W7 — `SUPPORTS` is the only edge kind emitted.** A `Counterexample` entry emits **no** `REFUTES` and **no** `COUNTEREXAMPLE_TO`: whether a counterexample refutes a candidate is a judgement over artifact content the graph does not hold, and asserting it from a section heading is the empty-success shape `rule errors.unsupported_surface` forbids. `CHECKED_BY` is unreachable by construction (D3 makes it the checker's own artifact).\n8. **W8 — refusal is whole-note; nothing is ever dropped or merged.** Typed refusal naming section + line index; no partial proposal set (INV-017 read at the note's granularity). No dedup/merge step either — that is how \"unresolved contradictions remain visible\" is met: two entries in tension are two proposals, and resolving by authoring order is what plan §11.7 forbids.\n9. **W9 — the note is provisional and is NOT a new plan §4.4 artifact class.** The biggest call. Minting `wb_*` in §4.4 would oblige `ArtifactClass::ALL` (currently 19, `crates/continuum-workspace/src/artifact_path.rs`) and the daemon's store-placement and compatibility surfaces to grow — far outside this slice. Precedent for a schema-bound artifact with a generic handle exists: `intent-registry-record.record_id` and `synthesis-candidate.candidate_id` both use `^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*_[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$`. So `note_id` uses that spelling, `not`-excluding `^(ev|cap)_`: `ev_` names a node or edge and the note appears in `provenance.inputs` where that would read as graph content; `cap_` confers authority and a note confers none. Both exclusions are enforced in the type too (`RESERVED_NOTE_CLASSES`).\n\nTwo smaller decisions worth naming:\n- **Labels.** Each compiled node carries one `Label`, `whiteboard:<schema key>`, so which section of which note proposed it is recoverable from the node (docs/44 \"Credit and provenance … derivation\"). It is inside the node's identity **on purpose**: the same claim offered as a known fact and as a candidate invariant is not the same assertion. Note the plural asymmetry (`whiteboard:goal` vs `whiteboard:decisions`) — the label is the schema key, mechanically, rather than a second hand-maintained vocabulary.\n- **`claim` / `idempotency_key` / time / epochs.** `claim` is required and explicit per entry — deriving it from wording would make \"is this the same claim the graph holds?\" a function of phrasing. `idempotency_key` is derived `\"<note_id>:<schema_key>:<index>\"`: recompiling a note is exactly the retry RFC 0026's keys make safe, and it is outside node identity (plan §11.7) so two holders naming the note differently still converge. `created_at` comes from the note (INV-005: the compiler reads no clock). The epoch pinning is a `compile` **parameter**, not a note field and not a default — a compiler that quietly pinned nothing would be choosing for its caller."}	blake3:-7ARIjTr-rLSRFlXkb7WT7lOLdeLEFTYhw_lD6psXGQ
1786297029234285	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEygw0		item.comment	bn-2qa0u	{"body":"## bn-2qa0u (3/3) — test inventory and deliberate exclusions\n\n### Test inventory\n\n`whiteboard.rs` unit tests (20): `the_sections_are_the_seven_plan_11_5_headings`, `every_section_names_its_schema_key`, `every_compiled_node_is_proposed`, `a_compiled_node_carries_its_section_label_and_the_note_as_an_input`, `an_unresolved_reference_refuses_the_whole_note` (neg), `a_refused_note_proposes_nothing` (neg + anti-vacuity), `a_resolved_reference_compiles` (anti-vacuity), `a_subject_the_graph_does_not_hold_is_not_a_refusal` (boundary), `status_words_in_prose_reach_nothing`, `an_experiment_becomes_a_task_proposal_and_not_a_node`, `a_conclusion_without_support_is_unrepresentable` (neg + anti-vacuity), `a_decision_gains_one_supports_edge_per_supporting_node`, `the_compiler_emits_no_check_edge`, `two_identical_entries_are_both_proposed` (boundary: converges on apply), `a_note_may_not_name_itself_evidence_or_a_capability` (neg + anti-vacuity on `evidence_9f`), `an_empty_line_is_refused` (boundary: one space *is* a line), `the_note_supplies_the_time`, `the_caller_supplies_the_epochs` (pinning moves identity), `compiling_twice_gives_identical_identities`, `the_record_is_the_schema_object`. Plus a `compile_fail` doc test — an entry cannot be given a status argument, the device `node.rs` uses — and its positive twin.\n\n`tests/whiteboard_compiler.rs` (6): `the_note_renders_to_the_dossiers_own_example`, `every_emitted_node_renders_to_its_registered_fixture`, `every_emitted_edge_renders_to_its_registered_fixture`, `the_fixtures_are_a_whole_compilation_and_not_a_selection` (anti-vacuity: the fixture tests would still pass over a compiler that emitted a subset, so counts and the task lane are pinned here), `a_client_can_apply_a_compilation_and_the_graph_holds_exactly_it` (8 fresh appends; reapply → 0 fresh, put-if-absent), `the_compiler_writes_no_status_and_records_no_check`.\n\n`crates/continuumd/tests/inv003_no_prose_only_evidence.rs`: added the `whiteboard-note` row to the hand-maintained `SCHEMA_BOUND` table, which that suite compares bidirectionally against the real `notes/plan/schemas/` listing (\"a new machine artifact class without a schema\"). It caught this addition; the row is the honest answer, not a silencing.\n\n### Deliberate exclusions\n\n- **No wire operation.** No `whiteboard.compile`, no `EvidenceQuery` field, no IDL / plan §10.2 / RFC 0027 registry change, no protocol-minor bump. sd01 registry agreement untouched (still 73 operations, 18 namespaces).\n- **No new plan §4.4 artifact class** (W9) — `ArtifactClass::ALL` and the daemon's store/compatibility surfaces untouched.\n- **No reverse direction** (docs/44's \"human-friendly whiteboard view\").\n- **No `redacted_references`** on the note: `sd14`'s target list is explicit and excludes it; a provisional note carries handles and prose, and prose is not redacted in place.\n- **No `REFUTES` / `COUNTEREXAMPLE_TO` / `CHECKED_BY` inference** (W7).\n- **No JSON reader in the library.** The crate keeps its \"types in, canonical `Value` records out\" shape. The JSON renderer that puts those records in front of the validator lives in the integration-test crate only, handles the five kinds these records use, and panics rather than guessing — never linked into the library, and deliberately not a second wire codec (`continuumd`'s is the wire's).\n- **None of the seven RFC 0038 semantic queries.** `graph.rs`'s argument for the six stands unchanged (they need artifact content the graph does not hold). `TaskProposal` is a down-payment on \"task generation\" only in the narrow docs/44 sense that experiments become proposals; graph-driven task generation (\"close missing proof edge\", \"find counterexample to candidate\", …) is untouched."}	blake3:ITN0kk_U19HbQn-RjUcb30TgA7cJsIlanIn1fKboNy8
1786297094899430	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyhA0		item.comment	bn-2qa0u	{"body":"Lead ratification: merged @ 2078389061e8 (workspace destroyed, snapshot retained). Direction correction ACCEPTED — note→proposals is what §11.5/RFC 0038/docs/44 all say; the brief was backwards. W1-W9 schema decisions reviewed and accepted, in particular W2's Known-facts→assumption (reading observed would be the promotion docs/44 forbids), W5's no-status-member (a note cannot write status by construction), W7's SUPPORTS-only emission, and W9's not-an-artifact-class (no obligation on ArtifactClass::ALL or store surfaces). Mechanical conformance is the right shape: the normative example IS the library's byte-for-byte output, fixtures validated by the real jsonschema validator. Declining to self-attest the dependency audit was correct — a worker greening its own gate with an attestation is the forged-attestation shape; lead takes the lockfile drift (bn-sk44r). Successor bn-1as8e carries the wire verb + reverse view + seven queries. Closing."}	blake3:dr2hkLHfUoDcrwdm4KZefyVk3FXvqJltz2BipmXhRH4
1786297094943276	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyhQ0		item.move	bn-2qa0u	{"state":"done"}	blake3:LlwPfXwLl73UqYRBkbsPlCy3Qbno7CaZZLWW8_jY7x4
1786297103640070	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyhg0		item.comment	bn-1kj2n	{"body":"[bn-1kj2n delivery 1/4]\n\nSTAGE GROUP 2 DELIVERED — bn-1kj2n, workspace bn-1kj2n, committed 633ebc5, NOT merged, workspace not destroyed. 6 files, +4106/-42, all inside crates/continuum-context. No src/ outside continuum-context touched, no existing test edited, no schema/RFC/plan/Cargo file edited; Cargo.lock byte-identical.\n\nWHAT EACH STAGE DECIDES\n\n- src/property.rs — stage 3's INPUT and its FILTER, and nothing else. PropertyAutomaton is a finite deterministic monitor over candidate identities, taken as a value the compiler is handed for exactly causal.rs's reason (continuum-cir is a stub and this crate may not import an engine); (state, symbol) is a BTreeMap key so nondeterminism is TransitionRepeated rather than last-writer-wins (INV-005). The load-bearing shape is that an automaton declares TWO sets, not one: the alphabet (what an observer publishes) and hidden_dependencies (the abstraction-relevant events no observer publishes), disjoint by construction. PropertyAutomaton::relevant() is their union and is what PropertyFilter::retain seeds from, then re-closes — so RFC 0028's \"a compiler MUST NOT exclude an abstraction-relevant hidden event on the grounds that no observer publishes it\" is the filter's definition rather than a review note, and the output stays downward closed because PropertyPreserving is licensed \"with stage 2\". Coverage{Total, Partial{CoverageGap x3}} is the stage-3 twin of causal::Completeness: under Total a drop is provably outside the property-directed slice (slice-irrelevant), under Partial an unenumerated hidden dependency could have made it relevant so the same computation proves nothing (heuristic-cutoff). Stage 3 is where RFC 0028's sentence about the *property-directed* slice is exact rather than analogous.\n- src/monitor.rs — the INDEPENDENT checker, and the only thing that licenses PropertyPreserving. PropertyMonitor::verdict(order, selection) is the finite instance of RFC 0028's \"Formal model\" preservation obligation and is checked in the theorem's own shape: EmptyAlphabet / NoObservedEvent (typed Inapplicable — no licence and no accusation), then ObservedEventDropped, then AbstractionDependencyDropped, then NotCausallyClosed, then VerdictDiverged.\n- src/dependence.rs — stage 4. It licenses NO guarantee; it licenses admissibility, and Admissibility is Admissible{reference, published anchors} | Inadmissible{NoCorrespondence, UncorroboratedSource, AnchorNotSelected}. The join is a conjunction of an untrusted static side (SourceCorrespondence) and a trusted dynamic one (ExecutionDependence).\n- src/compile.rs — stages 3 and 4 wired into the run in phase order, opt-in via with_property(automaton, monitor) and with_dependence(join). Both checks now run on the FINAL published selection (after redaction removed items, after stage 3 narrowed, after stage 4 grew), not on stage 2's, which is the same rule the stages-1-2 group stated and the only place it can honestly be applied once later stages move the set."}	blake3:W_sm0HU5YKftgyepm6d4X18D33Hvgiq7HcDcC-6bQds
1786297103687530	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyhw0		item.comment	bn-1kj2n	{"body":"[bn-1kj2n delivery 2/4]\n\nTHE INDEPENDENCE ARGUMENT (RFC 0028 Validation: \"an implementation that reuses the compiler's own automaton has checked nothing\")\n\nThree separations, in decreasing order of what they buy, and the third is mechanical:\n\n1. It is a DIFFERENT COMPUTATION, not the same one run twice. PropertyFilter *selects* (seed from the relevance set, take a backward closure). PropertyMonitor *runs the automaton* — twice, over the whole causal order and over the proposed selection — and compares the runs. Neither result is derivable from the other by reading the other's code, which is precisely the closure_violation precedent.\n2. It READS ONLY WHAT A CHECKER MAY READ: an order and a selection. Not the filter's intermediate, not the trail, not the accounting, not the stage-3 decision. So it answers about selections the filter cannot produce, which is what makes disagreement falsifiable rather than definitional.\n3. It is a DIFFERENT MODULE, pinned from outside the crate. tests/pr11_compiler_stages_3_4.rs::the_monitor_module_imports_only_the_premise_the_filter_also_reads reads src/monitor.rs's own bytes via include_str!, splits off the #[cfg(test)] half, and asserts the production half's `use crate::` lines are exactly `causal::{CausalOrder, ClosureViolation}` and `property::{AutomatonVerdict, PropertyAutomaton}` — the shared PREMISE, exactly as stage 2's slicer and checker share a CausalOrder — plus no `PropertyFilter::` call and no `use crate::compile`. It fails the moment someone imports the filter into the checker.\n\nWhat the separation does NOT buy, stated so nothing downstream over-reads it: the two are given the same *property*. RFC 0028's sentence is about reusing the compiler's automaton IMPLEMENTATION — a \"check\" that replays the filter's own decision — not about the two disagreeing over what the property is. with_property takes them as separate arguments so a stricter monitor IS expressible, and a_monitor_stricter_than_the_filter_refuses_the_licence exercises exactly that: the filter does what it was told, the monitor observes one more event, PropertyPreserving is refused and nothing else about the pack changes. C6 is unchanged — the artifact still records no checker identity.\n\nTHE INV-016 ADMISSIBILITY RULES\n\nINV-016 has two halves and stage 4 pays both as types.\n\n- NO SILENT UPGRADE. SourceCorrespondence (untrusted: comments, annotations, generated maps, analyses over files this workbench did not write) and ExecutionDependence (trusted: what actually ran) are TWO TYPES WITH NO CONVERSION BETWEEN THEM, so a caller structurally cannot pass the untrusted side where the trusted side is expected. Corroboration is an explicit act of attestation. admissibility() decides in this order: (1) nothing claims it -> NoCorrespondence; (2) the execution attests none of the claimed anchors -> UncorroboratedSource, decided BEFORE the slice is consulted so an uncorroborated claim never gets partial credit for pointing at a published item; (3) every corroborated anchor is outside the published core -> AnchorNotSelected.\n- NO INTERPOLATION. CorrespondenceRef is a two-member sum of exactly SourceRef and ModelActionRef, the landed constructors, and adds no third path; SelectedItem::new is pub(crate) and neither into_selected_item takes a string parameter. Compilation::admissible_items() builds items through those and nothing else.\n- STRUCTURE IS CHECKED; ASSERTIONS ARE CORROBORATED. This is the line that decides several behaviours and is stated in the module docs. The join's structure is the daemon's: a claim about a candidate this compile never held is CompileError::Causal(UnknownEndpoint), and a claim about a candidate whose kind is not source/model is CompileError::NotACorrespondenceKind (stage 4's column names those two and nothing else). The CONTENT of a claim — its anchors — is untrusted, so it is never obeyed and never fatal: a fabricated anchor simply fails to corroborate, cannot fail the compile, and cannot add a candidate to the pack. Untrusted input has no path to control flow and no path to the candidate set.\n- EVERY STAGE-4 DECLINE IS heuristic-cutoff, NEVER slice-irrelevant, and this is the module's own stated reading where RFC 0028 is silent rather than an inference. Stage 4 licenses admissibility, not irrelevance; proving an item outside the property-directed slice is stage 3's decision. For UncorroboratedSource the reading is forced rather than merely conservative: an uncorroborated claim is UNDECIDED, never DISPROVED, and recording it as provably irrelevant would be the pack asserting a proof about untrusted input — the same silent upgrade INV-016 forbids, pointed the other way. So even a join whose execution side is exhaustive declines to heuristic-cutoff.\n- REDACTION WINS OVER STAGE 4: a withheld source/model candidate is skipped by the join entirely and keeps the irretrievable `redaction` record the pre-pass earned it, rather than being re-labelled as a stage-4 decline.\n\nWHICH STAGE'S REASON A DROP CARRIES (the slice-irrelevant vs heuristic-cutoff distinction, distinguishable here for the first time)\n\nOne ladder, documented in compile.rs, applied once per candidate: selected -> withheld(redaction) -> claimed-and-declined by stage 4 (heuristic-cutoff) -> in the stage-2 slice but not stage 3's (the automaton's Coverage) -> otherwise (the order's Completeness). Stage 4's decision is taken BEFORE stage 3's deliberately: stage 3 drops every source and model candidate, because no property automaton steps on a source span, and recording those as *provably* outside the property-directed slice would be false about exactly the items stage 4 exists to decide. Where two stages both declined an item the manifest records the weaker claim — a drop is slice-irrelevant only where the stage that decided it could prove it. The visible consequence: ONE manifest now carries both reasons, each traceable to its deciding stage, and the same compile with only the automaton's declaration changed from Total to Partial moves exactly the two stage-3 drops from the proved cell to the undecided one while the 215 stage-2 drops keep their proof, because the causal order is still complete."}	blake3:lsi43RzDq-L_O6r7OD1qA1tBFjLqo2igsnkXkiMZa6g
1786297103722180	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyiA0		item.comment	bn-1kj2n	{"body":"[bn-1kj2n delivery 3/4]\n\nTEST INVENTORY — 57 new unit tests (238 total in the crate, was 181) + 21 new integration tests from outside the crate\n\nsrc/property.rs (17): alphabet is exactly the transition symbols and relevant() is strictly wider; observed-cannot-also-be-hidden; repeated transition refused (including a byte-identical redeclaration); violating state from nowhere refused, with the accept-case anti-vacuity; no-transition automaton observes nothing; a run ignores non-alphabet symbols; a missing transition holds the state rather than trapping; TWO TRACES REACH TWO VERDICTS (anti-vacuity for the verdict machinery — an automaton that concluded the same thing about everything would make the monitor's comparison meaningless); the filter keeps the hidden event and drops the irrelevant chain; the filter re-closes what it keeps; the filter cannot re-admit what a redaction hole removed; a relevant seed from outside the order is refused while an irrelevant stray is merely dropped; coverage decides the omission reason across all three gaps; distinct tokens; determinism.\n\nsrc/monitor.rs (10): the whole order and its property core both preserve (anti-vacuity); THE MONITOR REJECTS HAND-BUILT SELECTIONS THE FILTER CANNOT PRODUCE (three of them, each at its own arm, plus the honest one still passing); a dropped observed event caught EVEN WHEN THE SLICE IS CLOSED — the closure checker licenses it and the monitor refuses it, so neither guarantee implies the other; a dropped abstraction dependency caught; unclosed refused; a stray item outside the order refused; NoObservedEvent vs EmptyAlphabet kept distinct; an EMPTY selection of an observed execution is a disagreement and not an inapplicability (the two arms exist to keep exactly that apart); determinism.\n\nsrc/dependence.rs (17): corroborated+published admissible; AN UNTRUSTED CLAIM ALONE IS NOT ADMISSIBLE, with the claimed anchor deliberately published so nothing about the slice is doing the work; THE SAME CLAIM ATTESTED BY THE EXECUTION IS ADMISSIBLE (the anti-vacuity that proves the check discriminates rather than refusing everything); corroborated-but-unpublished anchor; unclaimed candidate; a fabricated anchor is not corroborated; execution attestation alone admits nothing (no typed reference to build from); partial corroboration admits on the corroborated anchor alone; repeated claim / anchorless claim / repeated attestation refused; every decline is heuristic-cutoff; the summary is exactly the span's position and exactly the action's name; determinism.\n\nsrc/compile.rs (13 new): stage 3 licenses PropertyPreserving with the five-phase trail; stage 3 narrows and stage 4 grows; a monitor that disagrees costs the guarantee; a monitor that observes nothing here is Inapplicable and not Preserving; A PROPERTY-PRESERVING PACK IS ALWAYS CAUSALLY CLOSED (three policies); a redaction hole costs property preservation too; stage-3 drops carry the automaton's coverage (exact (kind, reason) -> count cell maps, both declarations); stage 4 admits only a corroborated dependence; a join claim about another kind / about a candidate from nowhere refused; a withheld candidate is never admitted; a stage that did not run leaves no intermediate; determinism.\n\ntests/pr11_compiler_stages_3_4.rs (21, from outside the crate, at the G0-DX-01 row's grain): a 225-candidate order — the stages-1-2 fixture widened by an isolated abstraction-relevant hidden e_flush, a property-irrelevant diagnostic chain e_probe->e_report, and three source/model candidates covering corroborated / uncorroborated / corroborated-but-unpublished — compiles to a 6-item core, licensed [PropertyPreserving, CausallyClosed] by a monitor re-run a SECOND time from the test file itself; the trail's five phases show the pipeline is not monotone (3 -> 7 -> 5 -> 6) and six stages that did not run have no intermediate; 219 omissions reconcile exactly across four cells.\n\nThe two mutants the bone asked for, both at the top of the file's \"mutants\" section:\n- A MUTANT STAGE-3 FILTER, written in the test rather than the crate, that keeps the closure of the events the automaton STEPS ON and nothing else — precisely the compiler RFC 0028 prohibits. Its output is causally closed, so stage 2's checker licenses it without complaint; the property monitor refuses it at AbstractionDependencyDropped{e_flush}, and the honest filter's output passes the same monitor. Plus a closed-prefix mutant rejected at ObservedEventDropped, and each single-event drop from the property core caught at its own arm.\n- AN UNTRUSTED-SOURCE-ONLY DEPENDENCE CANNOT UPGRADE: m_commit's claimed anchor e_submit IS published, so the refusal is entirely about who attested it; it is named and counted as source/model heuristic-cutoff rather than hidden; and the same claim with an ExecutionDependence attestation IS admitted, which is the anti-vacuity and also the shape of the rule (the only route in is an explicit act of attestation with no conversion from the untrusted side).\n\nAlso: a corroborated dependence anchored outside the core; a fabricated anchor ignored rather than obeyed (the compile still reports 225 candidates and reconciles); a join claim about an event candidate refused; a redaction hole costing both guarantees and neither the honesty; the undecidable-drop distinction as exact cell maps; boundaries (an execution the automaton does not observe licenses nothing and says why; a filter that empties the slice cannot name where to get anything back; an entirely-relevant slice is not narrowed; a join that admits nothing records that it RAN, which is not the same fact as a stage that did not run); the crate-boundary disciplines (License::issue still unreachable, so PropertyPreserving cannot be minted from outside any more than CausallyClosed could; an admitted source item is a position and never a snippet); and INV-005 determinism with a not-vacuous companion."}	blake3:BjFXaxi0H2v0rV0DcBTEUufgT1l_ljs7uaILy2kus3k
1786297103756042	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyiQ0		item.comment	bn-1kj2n	{"body":"[bn-1kj2n delivery 4/4]\n\nGATE RESULTS — `maw exec bn-1kj2n -- just check` run in the foreground to completion.\n\nGREEN: fmt-check; lint (clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -D warnings); test (cargo test --workspace --locked, 233 `test result: ok` lines, 0 failures, recipe exit 0); boundaries; covenant; dossier (status pass); lean (19 jobs, axiom manifest up to date, every T0/T1 theorem axiom-free).\n\nRED, PRE-EXISTING, NOT THIS BONE'S: the `governance` recipe stops at check_dependency_audit.py, and check_t12_evidence.py ('cargo-vet/advisory scanning') and check_r16_evidence.py --self-test fail through it as delegates. Root cause is a single upstream defect already tracked as bn-sk44r: commit 3b6f471 (bn-1dsih, 2026-08-09 13:01, four commits back from HEAD) bumped Cargo.lock to blake3 1.8.6 / cc 1.4.2 / find-msvc-tools 0.1.10 without touching tools/governance/dependency-audits.toml, which still records 1.8.5 / 1.4.0 / 0.1.9. Verified pre-existing by running check_dependency_audit.py --self-test at the TRUNK root with a clean tree and none of this bone's files present: status fail, identical six violations. This workspace adds no dependency and leaves Cargo.lock byte-identical (`git diff --stat Cargo.lock` is empty). I deliberately did NOT repair it: option (b) means writing a who/criteria/notes vetting attestation for three upgraded crates I have not vetted, which is the forged-attestation shape the manifest exists to prevent and which bn-sk44r's own description already names; option (a) means mutating a shared reproducibility asset from a feature bone's workspace, which would collide with every concurrent workspace. Lead: bn-sk44r has to land before or with this.\n\nDELIBERATE EXCLUSIONS, named rather than implied\n\n- NO SEAL-LEVEL RULE FOR PropertyPreserving. The pipeline never claims it without CausallyClosed — the monitor checks closure itself as the preservation obligation's hypothesis — but this is NOT added to GuaranteeSet::seal. RFC 0028 states six composition rules and this is not one of them; inventing a seventh would put a rule in the artifact contract the RFC does not carry. a_property_preserving_pack_is_always_causally_closed pins the pipeline-level fact instead.\n- STAGE 4'S ITEM `id` AND ARRAY POSITION. admissible_items() builds through the landed constructors under the candidate's own identity; deciding the published selected[] order and the pack the items land in is whole-pack assembly's (bn-1y4qc).\n- A COUNTERFACTUAL ADMISSIBILITY RULE. RFC 0028 requires CounterfactualUnderNamedModel and a named causality model for that kind; stage 4's column names source and model and nothing else, and widening it here would be vocabulary this bone was not asked to invent.\n- PRODUCING a source correspondence or a property automaton. Plan §16's correspondence graph, correspondence.bind, and CML property elaboration are not in this workspace; both are values the compiler is handed, for exactly CausalOrder's reason.\n- STAGES 5-7's REFUSALS are bn-imhw2's and are untouched here: nothing in this change records a proof-slice omission, an observer projection, or a correspondence mapping, and ProofRelevant is still unclaimable.\n- MonitorDisagreement::VerdictDiverged is a deliberately unreachable fail-safe arm (with checks 3-5 passed the two observed sequences are equal and the automaton is deterministic), kept so a future automaton whose verdict is not a function of its observed sequence fails there rather than passing silently. The crate's precedent is CompileError::Unreachable, and the docs say so rather than leaving it to be discovered.\n\nSURPRISES worth carrying to bn-imhw2 / bn-3ub6i / bn-1y4qc\n\n- Name's SHORTLEX order bites again exactly as bn-21vno warned: the canonical six-item core here is [e_ack, e_loss, e_begin, e_flush, e_submit, s_writer], and the join's claimed list is [s_ghost, m_commit, s_writer]. Length first, then lexicographic.\n- continuum_value::value::Name does NOT implement Hash. AutomatonState had to drop a derived Hash; anything newtyping a Name hits this.\n- A downward-closed slice can only lose a NON-root element by breaking closure, so the mutant that drops an abstraction-relevant hidden event AND stays closed needs that event to be causally ISOLATED and named as a root. That is why the fixture's e_flush is isolated rather than mid-chain, and it is what makes \"the closure checker licenses it, the property monitor refuses it\" demonstrable at all rather than merely asserted."}	blake3:6qNdlfpfjDj5sB-ahvZ8n-Q9JuXv5ChEJ2JHVXm5giI
1786297114022792	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyig0		item.comment	bn-sk44r	{"body":"Still red, and it blocks bn-1kj2n (Context Pack compiler stages 3-4) the same way. Re-confirmed at the trunk root with a clean tree at HEAD f4c1f17 + claims commits: check_dependency_audit.py --self-test is status fail with the same six violations, check_t12_evidence.py fails its 'cargo-vet/advisory scanning' control through the same delegate, and check_r16_evidence.py --self-test fails because it delegates to check_t12_evidence. Everything else in 'just check' is green for bn-1kj2n's workspace: fmt-check, lint, test (233 ok lines, 0 failures), boundaries, covenant, dossier (pass), lean (19 jobs, axiom manifest current). Located the introducing commit for the record: 3b6f471 'feat: Forge task-assembly lane makes the non-vacuity second call (bn-1dsih)', 2026-08-09 13:01, Cargo.lock only, 9 insertions / 6 deletions, with no accompanying edit to tools/governance/dependency-audits.toml — which makes (a) 'the lockfile drifted by accident' the more likely of this bone's two candidates, since that commit's own subject is about Forge task assembly and not about dependencies. Not repaired from bn-1kj2n's workspace, for this bone's own stated reason."}	blake3:aJtXveosbEAo5kZp6bAtCWcukz7Mrx7ZIq8cFOOdyUM
1786297193391773	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyiw0		item.update	bn-sk44r	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"trace:meta"}}	blake3:puO45vCg6v0fvBRN0PJ8JXOt5VhXDfWegydlj34Dhk0
1786297193427810	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyjA0		item.comment	bn-sk44r	{"body":"Lead adjudication: option (a) — accidental drift. Evidence: 3b6f471 (bn-1dsih) touched Cargo.lock ONLY, no dependency-audits.toml edit, and no new external dependency motivated the three bumps (forge's new edge is intra-workspace to continuum-intent); a non---locked cargo invocation in that workspace floated them. Fix applied at trunk root by the lead: cargo update --precise back to blake3 1.8.5 / cc 1.4.0 / find-msvc-tools 0.1.9 (the audited set). check_dependency_audit.py now status pass with zero violations; cargo test --workspace --locked clean. No vet attestation written — nothing was actually vetted, and both workers were correct to refuse greening their own gate with one (the forged-attestation shape). If a future bone genuinely wants the newer versions, it records a real vet per (b). Lesson for briefs: workspace cargo invocations must be --locked."}	blake3:2danGib00IbgtgKuX2R5JChiAGDppUsb3cshIlkWZCQ
1786297193459413	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyjQ0		item.move	bn-sk44r	{"state":"done"}	blake3:nzJT0Jv6z6xjNogHarrq24YiO5X7tLMqtNbWCzJZ94Y
1786297222910201	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyjg0		item.comment	bn-1kj2n	{"body":"Lead ratification: merged @ ef5ca8676795 (workspace destroyed, snapshot retained). Review: stage 3's filter DEFINES the hidden-dependency inclusion (union-seed + re-close) rather than checking it after the fact; monitor independence is argued three ways with the third mechanical (include_str! pin on monitor.rs's production imports — no PropertyFilter, no compile); stage 4's no-conversion-between-types design makes the INV-016 upgrade structurally impossible rather than policed; both checkers run on the final published selection. Both requested mutants demonstrated non-vacuously — the causally-isolated-root construction for closure-licenses-but-monitor-refuses is exactly the right rigor. 78 new tests. The dep-audit red it reported was pre-existing and is fixed at trunk (bn-sk44r closed, option a). Closing."}	blake3:eeK645C5gI8oYf47AQjAduvShEGjWgeiI2tv3h17s2w
1786297222950876	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyjw0		item.move	bn-1kj2n	{"state":"done"}	blake3:2Z0w3XCnnrnT6AsaoReSabZRaCEOkuWThmNAAVYQDrY
