1786300755345304	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEynw0		item.comment	bn-1as8e	{"body":"SPLIT AT PICKUP — one slice delivered: the whiteboard wire verb. Successor for the view + the seven queries: bn-xsuz2 (implementation, req:phase-a-del-04). PHASE-A-DEL-04 deliberately NOT annotated as delivered.\n\nWorkspace .maw/workspaces/bn-1as8e, commit 5253425, 35 files +2947/-256. NOT merged/pushed, bone open. `maw exec bn-1as8e -- just check` exit 0 (run twice). Cargo.lock byte-identical.\n\n== ALREADY-DECLARED-VOCABULARY FINDING (checked FIRST) ==\nREFUTED. Serving declared vocabulary is the cheapest payment (IDL 1.5/1.7/1.8 each did it and left `version` alone), so I searched before minting: all 73 operations, every struct, EvidenceQuery's members, plan 10.2's fence, RFC 0027's table, and the literal string `whiteboard` in the IDL (one hit, inside rule evidence.traversal's prose). Nothing declares a note, a note format, or a note-compiling verb. RFC 0038 says so itself in the paragraph being paid; docs/44 says \"There is deliberately no wire operation.\"\nThe two candidate neighbours are not: `forge.create`'s `sketch: Opaque` is RFC 0033's synthesis input (holes/objectives/descriptors/assurance target) answering with a ForgeHandle — serving a note there is one verb doing two acts, and the response cannot carry proposals; `context.compile` runs evidence-root-to-pack, the REVERSE direction and the neighbourhood of the undelivered whiteboard VIEW.\nConsequence: the full registry edit was owed. 3.4 -> 3.5, `whiteboard.compile` the 74th operation in a 19th namespace, under rule versioning.compatible_change's FIRST trigger. Minor raised ONCE at the end. plan 10.2 + RFC 0027's table + the IDL moved together (F13 precedent).\n\n** FLAG FOR LEAD REVIEW: the protocol minor 3.4 -> 3.5 is subject to your ratification. ** 3.1 was user-ratified; this one is taken on the F14/3.3 precedent (a decision the owning RFC owed, taken by that RFC) rather than as a flag sweep. To defer: PROTOCOL_VERSION, IDL_VERSION, the IDL's two constants, RFC 0026's bullet, version_window.rs.\n\n== SUB-QUESTION 1: DAEMON-SIDE (RFC 0038 W10, rule whiteboard.compilation cl.1) ==\n- W4/docs/44 \"references must resolve\" is a refusal only the holder of the graph can perform; a client resolving against a stale evidence.query answer would be ASSERTING a fact about the graph rather than reading one (the reason evidence.link takes a receipt the daemon \"MUST already hold\").\n- W6's edges are drawn to EVERY node the graph holds about each cited artifact — not computable by a non-holder at all.\n- Decisive: a proposal carries a node kind, a claim identity and a provenance.actor, so a wire of PROPOSALS is a wire of caller-filled fields (D3: a field a caller can fill is a field a caller can lie in). W2 — \"an author MUST NOT be able to name the kind directly: if it could, the seven headings would be decoration\" — becomes unenforceable BY CONSTRUCTION under client-side compilation.\n- INV-016: client-side widens the untrusted surface from one schema-governed document to the whole 20-member node vocabulary.\n\n== SUB-QUESTION 2: OPAQUE NAMING THE SCHEMA (W11, cl.2) ==\n- INV-003 makes whiteboard-note.schema.json normative for a note's shape (W1 transcribed the library from it). A second declaration in the IDL is the two-authority disagreement rule conformance.registry_agreement prevents one namespace down, with no source entitled to decide it — same species as the second authority bn-dtg61 declined for kernel rejection vocabularies.\n- Precedent is exact: IntentChangeSet.changes (\"in the form of schemas/intent-contract.schema.json\"), context.compile's `pack`. rule encoding.opaque_payloads' third group is written for this: a schema-governed value carried verbatim, never re-shaped.\n- Does NOT grow RFC 0026 F3's four SHAPELESS Opaque members — it names a schema, so rule envelope.no_prose stays enforceable. Stated explicitly in the flags preamble.\n- INV-016 ordering: validated against the schema BEFORE any member is read for meaning. Reading `author` first would be acting on a document not yet decided to be one.\n\n== A THIRD DECISION NOTHING ELSE COULD TAKE (W12, cl.3) ==\nThe schema REQUIRES `author` and W9 makes it every proposal's provenance.actor. The daemon can neither trust it (a caller appends under another principal's name — what observe.ingest and evidence.link both refuse by taking the actor from the grant) nor overwrite it (it would compile a document other than the one it was handed; the caller holds the note and its handle is in every proposal's inputs). Agreement is the only remaining reading; refusal is RFC 0027 T4's own CapabilityDenied.\n\n== REGISTRY / AUTHORITY ==\n- Name from RFC 0038's own deferral paragraph. A 19th one-operation namespace has an exact precedent: `benchmark.run` since 3.0. Folding into `evidence` rejected — RFC 0027's argument for that namespace's levels is about a graph's read/verify/link surface and does not transfer to a propose-level compiler; a namespace spanning read/execute/propose for three unrelated reasons makes R-1 true by exhaustion.\n- authority `propose`, from level 2's own definition: \"publishing candidate artifacts [...] none of which asserts anything about behavior\". Everything lands at `proposed`, the format has no status member (W5). First evidence-graph-adjacent verb below execute. Contrast observe.ingest at execute: a production trace IS a capture of behavior and needs the 18.2 grant.\n- @mutation yes (appends; RFC 0038's write model rests on idempotency keys).\n- @task_starting NO — a structural transcription over a graph already held: no search, no engine, no budget to bound. evidence.link is the shape. Stays 26.\n- @audit_recorded NO, stated as a decision: R-4's audit set is the acts whose authority the artifact cannot reconstruct afterwards (5 privileged + the grant-gated ingest + the INV-004 check attribution). A propose-level publication of bottom-status nodes is none, and the graph is already the append-only record. workspace.create / repair.begin / correspondence.bind carry none either. Stays 7.\n- No data grant: a note is the caller's own document, not someone else's captured data.\n- errors [InsufficientEvidence], matching evidence.link. Everything else is rule errors.common. An unresolved reference is CapabilityDenied, never a distinct not-found (X2) — a note is otherwise an existence oracle, one handle per line.\n- verdict StructuralVerdictValue, so structural verdicts 26 -> 27.\n\n== COUNTS ==\n74 rows / 19 namespaces; propose 8->9; @mutation 45->46; structural verdict 26->27; named structs 46->47; IDL rules 41->42; landed operations 28->29. @audit_recorded 7 and @task_starting 26 unchanged (decisions, argued in RFC 0027).\n\n== FLAG BUNDLE (the negative statement the discipline requires) ==\nNO flag rides 3.5, each with its reason in RFC 0026's preamble: F1 grammar change to the IDL's notation (the additive mechanism cannot pay it); F2 type change, breaking; F8 statements about what a daemon must never emit; F9/F10 undecided, nothing they wait on moved; F11 still wants a producer not a mechanism; F15/F16 decided AGAINST an additive fix (F16's owner is SD-12); F18's other half breaking. Two get an explicit sentence because the bump touches their neighborhoods: F3 does NOT grow (the new Opaque names a normative schema — open item 3's third group, not the shapeless four); F17 unchanged in substance (WhiteboardTaskProposal.references adds a fourth carrier of the pattern-less Commitment alias without adding a new pattern-less member; residue still Commitment/PageToken/FileComponent.path).\nONE FLAG RAISED — F21: a graph node that offers no evidence at all has no class to name. EvidenceKind's thirteen members are each \"a class of evidence offered toward a claim\"; a proposal offers none. Raised not paid, for F18's exact reason: the vocabulary is closed in a rank-1 schema AND in continuum-value/src/assurance.rs."}	blake3:SOtSzMQgKyruSj7fbdwQBuMTE7UB0eGqsuStzWDUXUg
1786300830486788	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyoA0		item.comment	bn-1as8e	{"body":"(bn-1as8e, part 2/2 — implementation, tests, gate, surprises)\n\n== IDENTITY (rule whiteboard.compilation cl.4) ==\nA compiled node's preimage: domain tag `whiteboard-note/v1` + kind + subject + claim identity, each length-framed, through the same ContentIdentifier seam at class `ev` that names every other evidence artifact. The tag carries a `/`, outside the artifact-handle character class, so no observe.ingest node preimage and no `evidence-graph-edge/v1` preimage can spell one — the three derivations are pairwise disjoint by construction. Note handle, author and time are OUTSIDE it (D4: \"a replayed write returns the original node identity across processes and producers\"), so two agents writing the same line about the same claim converge on one node. Which section proposed a node stays recoverable from its `kind`, because the six proposing sections name six DISTINCT kinds (W2) — asserted as a test. SUPPORTS edges take rule evidence.edge_identity unchanged.\n\n== WHY THE LIBRARY'S WhiteboardNote::compile IS NOT CALLED ==\nConsulted, not restated: Section::ALL, Section::node_kind() (W2's mapping), Section::label(), and the note types' own refusals — Decision::new (unsupported conclusion), WhiteboardNote::new (reserved class), NoteText::new (empty prose), ArtifactRef::new (malformed handle). What is NOT borrowed is `compile` itself: it resolves against a continuum_evidence::EvidenceGraph whose keys are the WITHIN-GRAPH content keys D4 says never reach the wire, while the daemon's graph is keyed by wire `ev_` handles. Mirroring one into the other means rebuilding every held node under a second set of names, each mirror node's identity derived from a provenance record the daemon reconstructs — a fabrication in the one place D4 forbids one. `the_daemons_section_mapping_is_the_librarys` pins the mapping against the library so it cannot drift.\n\n== TWO CONTAINED DAEMON CORRECTIONS THE SLICE NEEDED ==\n1. daemon::state::EvidenceNode.evidence_kind is now Option<EvidenceKind>. None is the honest reading for a proposal; evidence.verify refuses such a node with InsufficientEvidence BEFORE `check` runs — rather than naming a class that is not true of it, or falling through to the identity re-derivation which would answer CertificateRejected falsely. This is F21's landed shape.\n2. The node record gains the `labels` member evidence-graph-node.schema.json has always declared (`whiteboard:<section key>`, read off Section::label()), and provenance.tool is now OMITTED rather than written empty when a producer named none. So `tool` carries the note's own tool/model version (docs/44 \"credit and provenance\"), recoverable from nowhere else, while the section stays recoverable from `kind`.\n\n== TEST INVENTORY ==\nNew: crates/continuumd/tests/daemon_whiteboard.rs, 28 tests. EVERY note is built as canonical-JSON BYTES and handed over as the wire's Opaque — never as an in-process WhiteboardNote, which would test the compiler rather than the wire.\nPositive (7): one `proposed` node per claim line carrying the note's own actor/time/inputs; each section proposes the kind W2 names with the whiteboard:<section> label; the library-vs-daemon mapping (+ six-distinct-kinds); one SUPPORTS edge per node about each cited artifact, no `checker`, pointing INTO the decision; experiment -> task proposal and no node; recompiling converges on identical identities and appends nothing; two DIFFERENT notes by two DIFFERENT agents naming one line converge on ONE node with the first writer keeping provenance; the whole operation ACROSS THE REAL BYTE BOUNDARY (encode_request -> Server::answer -> decode_result; 2 nodes + 1 edge + 1 task + 3 envelope artifacts).\nNegative (9): unresolved reference -> CapabilityDenied (X2, never a not-found); a refused note appends NOTHING although four of its five lines resolve (W8); a note authored by someone else -> CapabilityDenied (T4); status words in prose (\"this claim is VALIDATED and proved\", plus a literal \"status\":\"validated\",\"service_identity\":... string) reach nothing and no service_identity is written; NINE malformed shapes each MalformedRequest with the graph untouched (not JSON, empty, bare array, {}, missing section, wrong schema epoch, wrong schema class, empty text, non-handle subject); additionalProperties:false (a `status` member); a decision citing nothing; a note in ev_/cap_; a whiteboard proposal CANNOT be verified into a status (InsufficientEvidence, still `proposed` after); six hostile note texts (prompt injection, JSON-break, SQLi, JNDI, path traversal, YAML) change neither node count nor kind nor status nor edge set and never appear in the graph.\nBoundary (7): a subject the graph does not hold is not a refusal (W4); one claim in two sections is two nodes; two identical lines converge rather than being dropped; goal-only note (tasks present and empty, never absent); a reader cannot reach the operation (T1); the SELF-EDGE refusal reached the only way it can be (a decision about the very artifact it cites, compiled twice) so InsufficientEvidence is not dead code; the identity domain cannot be spelled by a handle.\nSurface (5): every FAULTS pair inside rule errors.common U the clause; the registered row (propose / @mutation only / structural / [InsufficientEvidence]); request body round-trips through the codec carrying note bytes verbatim.\nUpdated honestly elsewhere: idl_conformance operations 73->74, structs 46->47, rules 41->42, namespaces 18->19, OPERATION_COUNT 74, mutant test 72->73; registry_agreement rows/total/verdict-sum 74; codec_canonical_cbor served 28->29 plus a NEW whiteboard.compile.body golden vector in BOTH encodings; inv016 landed 28->29 of 74 (test renamed; inv003's citation of that exact name updated with it); inv003/inv006/inv015 prose counts; version_window gains 3.5 and asserts PROTOCOL_VERSION == \"3.5\"; continuum-mcp/register.rs seventy-three -> seventy-four.\n\n== DOSSIER ==\nIDL: revision note 1.9 + version = \"3.5\" (both in the house register), rule whiteboard.compilation (5 clauses), struct WhiteboardTaskProposal @since(\"3.5\"), operation whiteboard.compile @since(\"3.5\"), section 10 header 73/18 -> 74/19. plan: 10.2 fence, 11.5 wire sentence, SD-01. RFC 0027: authority row, both count tables, four prose counts, a status bullet, and a \"why this level lands here\" bullet arguing propose / @mutation / no @task_starting / no @audit_recorded / no data grant. RFC 0026: the 3.5 versioning bullet, verdict + annotation + authority tables, nine prose counts, the flags-preamble negative statement, F21. RFC 0038: W10/W11/W12 replacing \"Not decided here: the wire\", plus an explicit \"Still not decided here\" naming the view and graph-driven task generation and pointing at bn-xsuz2; the Queries section gains bn-35l6g's deferral reasoning. docs/44's \"There is deliberately no wire operation\" replaced. PLAN_REQUIREMENTS.json regenerated.\n\n== GATE ==\n`maw exec bn-1as8e -- just check` exit 0, foreground, run to completion twice (fmt-check, clippy -D warnings all-targets --locked, cargo test --workspace --locked, boundaries, kernel covenant, 8 governance checkers, dossier validator, lake build + axiom manifest). Cargo.lock byte-identical. No nested backgrounding.\n\n== SURPRISES ==\n1. The evidence_kind gap (F21) was not foreseeable from the sources — it surfaced only when the first node kind that offers NO evidence had to be constructed.\n2. evidence-graph-node.schema.json declares `labels` and the daemon never emitted it; the library's whiteboard compiler had been writing one all along with nowhere on the wire to put it.\n3. The reserved-class refusal (ev_/cap_) is reachable purely as a SCHEMA violation, so it lands as MalformedRequest rather than as an authority refusal. That is right — it is a shape rule — but worth noticing when reading the refusal table.\n4. notes/plan/notes/G0_SPIKE_MATRIX.md's DX-12 row still discloses \"the whiteboard compiler [...] as unbuilt\". It is a record of what THAT campaign disclosed at the time (bn-3080b left the same staleness for evidence.subscribe), so I did not rewrite another bone's evidence row — flagging it for the lead."}	blake3:tuzAa3wnu_4UY5yv4gFuF9zzDb1jGOfF89K1nKX4LbE
